\par\par \b \uc2 Russian Toy-Store Owner to List With Billion-Dollar Valuation--2nd Update\b0\par\par\uc2 By Olga Razumovskaya \par \uc2 879 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Februar 2017\par 14:22\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2017, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 MOSCOW--Russia's top children's goods retailer listed its shares Wednesday in the first major initial public offering in Russia since the annexation of Crimea, as investors cautiously return amid a nascent economic recovery and the possibility of better diplomatic relations with Washington.\par \par \uc2 The owners of PAO Detsky Mir raised $355 million in the sale, which priced shares at the bottom end of the expected range of 85 rubles (U.S. $1.43) a share, valuing the company at around $1 billion.\par \par \uc2 Russia's economy is emerging from a two-year recession caused by Western sanctions over its military \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2  and a plunge in the price of crude oil, its main export. The Russian stock market has rebounded in recent months after oil prices regained some ground and U.S. President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  indicated he wants to improve relations, which have been at their worst since the end of the Cold War.\par \par \uc2 The main owner of \uc2 Detsky Mir\uc2 , conglomerate \uc2 AFK Sistema\uc2 , put off plans for an IPO in 2014, and there hasn't been a major initial stock sale since then. Analysts said the placement would open the door for other companies to list shares.\par \par \uc2 "Global investors are showing appetite for Russian assets," said \uc2 Jacob Grapengiesser\uc2 , a partner at investment fund \uc2 East Capital\uc2 . He said he expected a couple of placements a month this spring.\par \par \uc2 Fewer than 10% of the bids for \uc2 Detsky Mir\uc2  stock came from Russian investors, with the rest coming from foreign funds in Europe, the Middle East and Asia, a person familiar with the placement said. More than 30% of the bids came from U.S. investors, the person said.\par \par \uc2 Several \uc2 Detsky Mir\uc2  shareholders are selling some of their stock via the IPO, including Sistema, and the Russia-China Investment Fund, in addition to executives at Sistema and \uc2 Detsky Mir\uc2  itself. Sistema will retain a majority stake in \uc2 Detsky Mir\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The interest from investors will be a boon for Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 , who faces a presidential election next year. He has built sky-high approval ratings since he came to power in 2000 on the back of economic growth. But more recently Mr. Putin has relied on a patriotic wave from the Crimea annexation and confrontation with the West, pollsters say.\par \par \uc2 Forty-four percent of Russians expect the economic crisis in Russia to last two years or longer, according to a January survey by the Levada-Center pollster.\par \par \uc2 Detsky Mir\uc2 , Russian for Children's World, traces its history to a store opened two years after the end of World War II near Lubyanka, the KGB's headquarters in central Moscow. As Russian salaries shot up in the 2000s amid a boom in Russia's crude production and the price of oil, \uc2 Detsky Mir\uc2  grew into a chain that now operates 525 stores in Russia and Kazakhstan and controls 13% of the children's goods market. (\uc2 Detsky Mir\uc2  no longer owns the central Moscow store.) Chief Executive \uc2 Vladimir Chirakhov\uc2  said the company plans to open around 250 new stores by 2020.\par \par \uc2 Russian consumers have been battered by the recession, bearing the brunt as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  let the national \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the ruble, trade freely and lose around half its value against the dollar, sending inflation soaring. Real wages, which had flourished in the last decade-and-a-half, dropped by 9% in 2015, sending retail sales plunging. Real wages crept up 0.6% last year, but retail sales were down 5.2%, even as the ruble has strengthened in recent months and inflation has hit historic lows. Analysts say consumers are reluctant to spend after the damage wreaked on household finances by the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  crisis and inflation spike.\par \par \uc2 Russia's \uc2 Federal State Statistics Service\uc2  said last week that the economy contracted 0.2% last year, less than the government had forecast.\par \par \uc2 "Industry has driven the recovery, while consumer-facing sectors remain weak," said William Jackson, senior emerging markets economist at Capital Economics, a London-based research firm.\par \par \uc2 Sales of cars and light commercial vehicles fell 5% in January compared with the same period last year, the \uc2 Association of European Businesses\uc2  said Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 Still, \uc2 Detsky Mir\uc2  was shielded by the fact that people prefer not to scrimp on items for their children, analysts said.\par \par \uc2 "There are things one simply cannot cut back on: like diapers and certain children's goods," said Marat Ibragimov, an analyst at BCS Global Markets.\par \par \uc2 The birthrate has also recovered in the last decade after a slump in the 1990s, aided by a state program that pays woman as much as 453,000 rubles ($7,650) for giving birth to a second child.\par \par \uc2 "I know that the wages may be declining, but there's still a list of things I need to buy," said Olga, a shopper at a downtown store who is 33 weeks pregnant and declined to provide her last name. "I'm operating on a budget now and am looking for cheaper goods but I still have this list. A baby still needs a crib and a stroller."\par \par \uc2 Write to Olga Razumovskaya at olga.razumovskaya@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 February 08, 2017 08:22 ET (13:22 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020170208ed280020k\p