\par\par \b \uc2 UK Morning Briefing: Profit-Taking to Dent FTSE; Brent Tops $50\b0\par\par\uc2 1,631 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 August 2016\par 07:07\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Snapshot: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 FTSE called lower; dollar firmer, EUR/USD 1.1340; bonds should hold gains; Brent crude $50.78; gold $1347.09\f28 \par \par \uc2 -Williams Says Fed Should Move to Raise Rates 'Sooner Rather Than Later'\par \par \uc2 -Russia Builds Up Army Near Ukraine Border\par \par \uc2 Watch For: Public sector finances; no major results scheduled \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Global News: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve Bank of San Francisco President \f28 \uc2 \f2 John Williams\f28 \uc2 \f2  said Thursday that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  should move to raise interest rates "sooner rather than later," siding with a growing chorus of Fed officials who believe the economy is strong enough to tolerate higher borrowing costs and worry about overshooting the Fed's inflation target.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Mr. Williams described a healthy economy with on-pace jobs growth, strong consumer spending and improving household balance sheets. He also said there are no signs that the U.K.'s decision to exit the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European Union\f28 \uc2 \f2  will throw the U.S. economy off course.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Russia is bolstering its military presence on its western border, sending tens of thousands of soldiers to newly built installations within easy striking distance of Ukraine.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The moves, which come as Moscow ratchets up confrontation over the Black Sea peninsula of Crimea, are a centerpiece of a new military strategy the Kremlin says is meant to counter perceived threats from the \f28 \uc2 \f2 North Atlantic Treaty Organization\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Military analysts say the deployments appear to be an effort to build a more permanent and robust military posture around \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ukraine\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , where Russia has carried out covert military \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  aimed at maintaining influence in its West-leaning neighbor. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Shares: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 London shares are called lower on Friday, according to IG, with the FTSE 100 seen down 17 points.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "I think a bit of profit-taking is going on," wrote Angus Nicholson, Market Analyst at IG.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Most of the gains in the U.S. session overnight were helped along by another surge in the oil price, but there was little fundamental data supporting the move except more jawboning from OPEC's former president. I think there is skepticism in the market that oil can pull this feat off again and there aren't a ton of other sectors primed to pick up the slack."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Asian shares were broadly higher Friday with oil's push into bull territory lifting energy stocks across the region.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Nikkei Stock Average was trading 0.6% higher, helped by a weaker yen and strong energy prices. In Australia, the S&P/ASX 200 was trading 0.4% higher with \f28 \uc2 \f2 BHP Billiton\f28 \uc2 \f2  and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Rio Tinto\f28 \uc2 \f2  posting gains.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Hang Seng Index was about unchanged with the Shanghai Composite trading 0.2% lower.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. stocks rose Thursday as oil prices rallied.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The S&P 500 added 0.2%, the Dow Jones Industrial Average rose 0.1%, and the Nasdaq Composite gained 0.2%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. stocks have notched all-time highs in recent weeks, while European stocks have staged a steep recovery from their post-Brexit lows.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Even with those highs, "people are not feeling as good as you might think they'd feel," said Seth Masters, chief investment officer at AllianceBernstein's wealth-management arm, pointing to deteriorating measures of investor sentiment.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We've gotten really used to very low interest rates. That creates vulnerability if anything changes," he said. \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 EUR/USD has slipped from Thursday's nearly two-month high of 1.1365 to trade at around 1.1340, as a dollar-buying wave swept across Asia.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The prominent spike of USD/KRW, a bellwether for other dollar/Asia \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  pairs, may have triggered hedging via selling of EUR/USD. Traders often hedge their bets in less-liquid Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by initiating a counter-balancing dollar position in a more-liquid major \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  pair, typically EUR/USD.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 If the dollar is rising against emerging market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , but traders don't want to cross a large bid-offer spread to cover their paper losses, they might nullify their short-dollar position by selling EUR/USD.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro could dip further toward the 1.1255 floor of the Bollinger uptrend channel if dollar-buying continues. But the bullish chart signal will be maintained for EUR/USD if that chart support holds up. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Bonds: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 European government bonds could strengthen further on Friday, with equity markets poised for losses.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Minutes from the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's meeting last month were released Thursday, demonstrating the region's concern about the impact of Brexit.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Officials worried about the fallout from Britain's vote, and while they were not ready to take action just yet, they left open the door for new stimulus, possibly as soon as September.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In response, investors snapped up European bonds, sending yields lower. According to data from \f28 \uc2 \f2 Tradeweb\f28 \uc2 \f2 , benchmark and peripheral 10-year notes saw yields falling, with Spanish debt leading the way, its yield hitting new intraday and closing lows of 0.911% and 0.919%, respectively.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Yields on French and Italian bonds are also near record lows, although Portuguese debt bucked the trend: The yield on its 10-year note climbed to 2.982%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. government\f28 \uc2 \f2  bond prices strengthened on Thursday, as investors continued to react to the latest signal that Fed policy makers are hesitant to raise rates in September.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The yield on the benchmark 10-year Treasury note was 1.536% in late afternoon trading. It fell to 1.558% on Wednesday from 1.576% on Tuesday after the release of minutes from the Fed's July policy meeting.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The minutes didn't signal that they were just about to pull the hike trigger," said Priya Misra, head of global rates strategy at \f28 \uc2 \f2 TD Securities\f28 \uc2 \f2  in New York.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 According to one measure, investors believe the FOMC is more likely than not to keep rates constant when it meets Sept. 20-21, but could very well raise rates in December. Federal-funds futures, which are used to place bets on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central-bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy, showed on Thursday that investors and traders see an 18% likelihood of a rate increase at the Fed's September meeting, according to \f28 \uc2 \f2 CME Group\f28 \uc2 \f2 . They see a 48.8% likelihood of an increase at the December meeting.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Most people seem to think that the FOMC will give more warning than they have" before hiking rates, said \f28 \uc2 \f2 Mary Ann Hurley\f28 \uc2 \f2 , vice president of trading at \f28 \uc2 \f2 D.A. Davidson & Co\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investors and traders will be watching closely when \f28 \uc2 \f2 Janet Yellen\f28 \uc2 \f2  speaks at the Fed's Jackson Hole symposium next week. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Energy: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In early Asian trade, West Texas Intermediate crude was 0.1% higher with Brent giving up its gains to trade 0.3% lower.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Oil entered into a bull market in the U.S. as falling stockpiles and talk of a production cap by the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Organization of the Petroleum Exporting Countries\f28 \uc2 \f2  led to gains. The WTI benchmark has climbed 22% since Aug. 2.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Crude is actually technically back to a bull market," said Margaret Yang, a market analyst at CMC Markets.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. crude-oil prices have risen more than 20% since they dipped below $40 a barrel early this month, boosted by falling stockpiles and hopes that the world's largest exporters will cap output. It's the latest in a series of big swings for the oil market.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Prices are still down by more than half from when they traded above $100 a barrel in 2014, but they are also up more than 80% from just six months ago, when they tumbled below $30 a barrel.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "It's certainly a nice move, and it looks like there is some momentum behind it," said Bart Melek, head of commodity strategy at \f28 \uc2 \f2 TD Securities\f28 \uc2 \f2 . "There is a change in narrative here, as far as the oil markets are concerned." \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Metals: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Gold prices fell in Asia as traders booked profits after a jump the previous day.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The outlook for the precious metal is bullish because of the likelihood that lower interest rates will prevail for a longer period, although analysts forecast a U.S. rate rise may be on the cards by year-end. Physical demand from India, one of the largest consumers, has started picking up.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 At 0238 GMT, gold was trading at $1,347.09/ounce, down from the previous close at $1,353.80/ounce. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Prices: \par  \par DJIA      18597.70  23.76 0.13% \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nasdaq\f28 \uc2 \f2      5240.15  11.49 0.22% \par S&P 500    2187.02   4.80 0.22% \par FTSE 100   6868.96   9.81 0.14% \par FTSE 250  17871.04 112.36 0.63% \par FTSE AIM \par All-Share   784.29   3.77 0.48% \par Closing \par  \par Futures: \par Dow      18545.00 -20.00 -0.11% \par S&P       2181.75  -1.75 -0.08% \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nasdaq\f28 \uc2 \f2     4802.00  -6.50 -0.14% \par As of 0306 GMT \par  \par Nikkei    16474.67  -11.34 -0.07% \par Shanghai   3090.50  -13.62 -0.44% \par Hang Seng 22891.02 -132.14 -0.57% \par S&P/ASX    5518.10   10.30  0.19% \par As of 0325 GMT \par  \par USD/JPY 100.23-24 +0.35% \par Range    100.43    99.87 \par EUR/USD 1.1331-34 -0.18% \par Range    1.1361   1.1333 \par GBP/USD 1.3138-40 -0.21% \par Range    1.3186   1.3128 \par EUR/GBP 0.8623-26 +0.03% \par Range    0.8639   0.8616 \par As of 0250 GMT vs. NY close \par  \par U.S. Treasurys: \par 2Y  100 3/32 +2/32 0.71%  -2.83BP \par 5Y  100 2/32 +5/32 1.114% -3.09BP \par 10Y 99 22/32 +7/32 1.536% -2.21BP \par Closing \par  \par Oil futures: \par Brent crude -11c at $50.78 \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nymex\f28 \uc2 \f2  crude  +3c at $48.25 \par At 0219 GMT \par  \par Asian Gold: \par Spot gold at $1347.09 vs $1353.80 \par At 0238 GMT \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Contact: Write to paul.larkins@wsj.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 August 19, 2016 01:07 ET (05:07 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020160819ec8j000cd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
