\par\par \b \uc2 Bank of Russia Buys Another $200M for International Reserves\b0\par\par\uc2 By Andrey Ostroukh   \par \uc2 211 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Juni 2015\par 09:37\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 MOSCOW--The Bank of Russia bought another $200 million for the country's international reserves on Friday, data showed Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which reports its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  with a two-day lag, started buying foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on May 13 in an attempt to replenish depleted reserves. Since the \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  started, the bank has bought as much as $2.53 billion.\par \par \uc2 Bank of Russia is topping up foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves after it spent billions of dollars to defend the ruble over the past year. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has plummeted in value because of the decline in the price of oil and economic sanctions imposed on Russia by the West following the annexation of Crimea and Moscow's support of separatists in eastern \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The daily amount of \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  account for less than 5% of average turnover on the Moscow exchange, but in the absence of other news the purchases limit the ruble's upside. The ruble gained 0.1% to 53.57 versus the dollar in early trade on Tuesday, supported by a recovery in oil prices.\par \par \uc2 Write to Andrey Ostroukh at andrey.ostroukh@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 June 02, 2015 03:37 ET (07:37 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020150602eb62000s0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
