\par\par \uc2 Analysis\par \b \uc2 Scant U.S. Hope for Russian Reversal\b0\par\par\uc2 By Philip Shishkin \par \uc2 893 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Dezember 2014\par 14:34\par \uc2 Dow Jones Top Energy Stories\par \uc2 DJTES\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON--Western policy makers are waiting to see whether a weakened Kremlin will call off its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2  to win a reprieve from economic sanctions that have combined with low oil prices to push the Russian ruble off a cliff.\par \par \uc2 But there is limited reason for optimism, current and former officials say, as the penalties imposed on Russia for invading Ukraine have so far failed to persuade President Vladimir Putin to call off Russia's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in east \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Further, a political climate in Washington that has turned against the Russian leader likely means the sanctions will stay for the foreseeable future. Bowing to pressure from Congress, President Barack Obama intends to sign a bill that for the first time turns the Russia sanctions into federal law, the White House said Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 The bill's impact is largely symbolic, allowing White House leeway in measures it enforces. But it adds to the growing perception that Russia is fast becoming off limits to Western investment.\par \par \uc2 Russia's economic fall showed no sign of abating on Tuesday, as its battered \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  plunged 20% against the dollar to a record low. That came despite the Russian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's surprise move to raise interest rates to 17%.\par \par \uc2 The White House on Tuesday gave no indication it harbors second thoughts about economic restrictions that the U.S. and the European Union began leveling against the Russian economy after the Kremlin moved to annex Crimea in March.\par \par \uc2 Moscow-backed separatists later seized chunks of east Ukraine, drawing yet more sanctions from the West.\par \par \uc2 Washington and European capitals later accused Moscow of not fulfilling its commitments under a shaky Ukrainian peace deal struck in September.\par \par \uc2 Moscow has denied backing the separatists and ignoring the truce terms, and has instead accused the West of fomenting the crisis in Ukraine to damage Russia's interests.\par \par \uc2 "It will be up to President Putin to decide whether or not the economic costs are worth it to him and are worth it to the Russian people," White House spokesman Josh Earnest said Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Earnest, echoing a similar comment in London Tuesday by Secretary of State John Kerry, said that sanctions could be lifted if Moscow took steps to de-escalate the conflict. "But we have not seen Russia follow through on those promises."\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Russian Foreign Minister Sergei Lavrov dismissed the impact of Western sanctions.\par \par \uc2 "I can assure you that Russia will not only survive, but will come out stronger out of this," he said in an interview with French television channel France 24. "We have been in much worse situations in our history, and every time we were getting out of these fixes much stronger. This will happen this time."\par \par \uc2 Western economic restrictions aren't the only reason the Russian economy is hurting. The low price of oil, a key Russian export, has created major problems for the commodity-dependent Russian budget. That, combined with what U.S. officials call erratic economic management, paved the way for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  crisis.\par \par \uc2 A senior U.S. official said on Tuesday that damaging the Russian economy for the sake of it, or ousting Mr. Putin, were never Washington objectives. Top Russian leaders accuse Washington of pursuing regime change in Russia. This official noted that in the past Mr. Putin sometimes responded with tactical shifts to his Ukraine policy to minimize Western fallout, but it was too early to say whether the latest ruble plunge would change the Kremlin's calculus.\par \par \uc2 U.S. officials believe Moscow could be on the verge of insolvency, and the plunge in crude oil prices over the past month, they said, could push Moscow into a balance-of-payments crisis. "Their system seems to be freezing up," said a senior U.S. official.\par \par \uc2 For months now, U.S. officials have been concerned that Mr. Putin may not be getting sound economic advice, or contrarian opinions, and may instead be increasingly reliant on the echo chamber of a small circle of advisers unwilling to contradict the president.\par \par \uc2 "No one wants to go to Putin and tell him about problems," one senior U.S. official said. "It's a dysfunctional system."\par \par \uc2 The sanctions that have had the biggest impact on the Russian economy are those that restricted Western lending to key Russian banks and energy companies, which have in turn have had a cascading effect on the rest of the economy's access to capital, analysts say.\par \par \uc2 "It's an absolute crisis, and a disastrous one," said Anders Aslund, a senior fellow at the Peterson Institute for International Economics, and a former advisor to the Russian government in the 1990s. "What they didn't realize is how vulnerable they are."\par \par \uc2 Some Russia analysts have detected potentially positive signs, seeing a decline in official emphasis on east Ukraine and a reduction in the use of the imperial term "Novorossiya" to describe the rebel-held areas of the east.\par \par \uc2 But Michael McFaul, a former U.S. ambassador to Russia, said a Russian retreat appeared unlikely.\par \par \uc2 "It's hard for me to imagine what concessions Putin will be willing to offer," he said. But adverse economic conditions, he noted, might prevent the Russian leader from backing further adventures in Ukraine or other former Soviet lands.\par \par \uc2 Jay Solomon and Felicia Schwartz contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJTES11020141218eaci0001f\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
