\par\par \b \uc2 Russia's 'difficult' rate hike approved by Putin\b0\par\par\uc2 By Lidia Kelly, Elena Fabrichnaya and Alexander Winning \par \uc2 783 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Dezember 2014\par 03:10\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2014 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 MOSCOW (Reuters) - Late on Monday night \uc2 Russia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor wrung out an agreement with her officials to impose the country's biggest rate hike since the financial crisis of 1998 -- with the approval of President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Elvira Nabiullina\uc2  had to halt the collapse of the ruble -- down 56 percent against the dollar so far this year -- not only to avoid the chaos of another economic implosion but to remove a growing threat to Putin. His ratings are at an all-time high following the annexation of Ukraine's Crimea, but depend too on his promises of financial stability.\par \par \uc2 A source close to the Kremlin could not say whether Putin had ordered the rate hike, or if he simply approved it.\par \par \uc2 "I cannot tell you who called whom. But this was not some minor policy tuning, this was a huge rate hike, of course the president was involved," said the source.\par \par \uc2 The Kremlin was quoted as saying by RIA news agency on Tuesday that it would not comment on the decision as the bank was independent of it.\par \par \uc2 Initially some board members suggested increasing the main rate by 400-450 basis points, sources familiar with the situation told Reuters, to try to shore up an economy pushed to the brink of recession by plummeting oil prices and Western sanctions resulting from Putin's action on Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 But by 10:00 p.m. (2.00 p.m. ET), all had agreed to an unprecedented 650 basis points hike, the biggest since 1998 - when an economic collapse prompted the rouble to crash and caused millions of Russians to lose their savings as the country defaulted.\par \par \uc2 "The meeting was long, the meeting was difficult and the decision was very difficult," said one of the sources close to the matter.\par \par \uc2 Oleg Vyugin\uc2 , chairman of the board of \uc2 MDM Bank\uc2  and a former first deputy \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chairman, said the Kremlin may not have issued direct instructions.\par \par \uc2 "This (the Kremlin's influence) does not necessarily have to be in the form of consultations," he said.\par \par \uc2 "It could be a tacit understanding - as a carte blanche: you make decisions and you are responsible for them."\par \par \uc2 DELAY\par \par \uc2 The meeting ended soon after 10:00 p.m. At around 2330, a Reuters correspondent saw Nabiullina's black Audi -- with its blue flashing light of Russia's political elite -- drive back through the gates of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 It is not clear why she returned, but sources familiar with \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  procedures say her signature was most probably needed on the final document.\par \par \uc2 After the decision, it took the bank almost three hours to send text messages to a group of journalists who cover monetary policy. Those messages arrived at 0047 local time, alerting them that a press release would appear on the bank's website.\par \par \uc2 "The decision was ready, but there were technical glitches," said the source familiar with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 By 0051 on Tuesday, an hour and six minutes after the ruble stopped trading on the MICEX index, the bank's announcement was published.\par \par \uc2 At Tuesday's opening, the ruble opened up 9 percent. But it soon fell back to record lows and was trading down 17 percent on the day at 0705 ET.\par \par \uc2 Speaking to Russian state-owned Rossiya-24 television channel after the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets had opened, a pale Nabiullina defended her decision, saying it was aimed at curbing the negative effect of the rouble weakness.\par \par \uc2 Investors and analysts had praised the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  for its action to support the ruble when it fell on news that Russia would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  militarily in \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Back in March the bank spent as much as $10 billion a day to defend the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , though since then it has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  only sporadically.\par \par \uc2 But on Tuesday Russian lawmakers called for Nabuillina's resignation, dubbing her a "pest". Her first deputy in charge of monetary policy, Ksenia Yudayeva, was also criticized.\par \par \uc2 "I am not sure whether Nabiullina can survive this," said Timothy Ash, head of Emerging Markets research at Standard Bank in London. "She has now had the worst of all worlds, allowing the ruble to crash, having lost close to $100 billion in \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves and having hiked policy rates by more than 1,000 basis points now this year."\par \par \uc2 "I am asking myself who has been running monetary and exchange rate policy in Russia in recent weeks. Was it the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  or the Kremlin?"\par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Oksana Kobzeva, Katya Golubkova and Vladimir Abramov; Writing by Lidia Kelly; Editing by Elizabeth Piper and Sophie Walker) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2014-12-17T02:10:59.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020141216eacg00jod\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
