\par\par \b \uc2 Russia Raises Key Interest Rate to 17% -- 3rd Update\b0\par\par\uc2 1,034 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Dezember 2014\par 00:48\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par By Alexander Kolyandr, Andrey Ostroukh and Chiara Albanese \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 MOSCOW--\f28 \uc2 \f2 Russia's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , in a bid to stem a sharp decline of the ruble, raised its key interest rate Tuesday to 17% from 10.5%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 In a statement published after midnight Moscow time, the Bank of Russia said it also is raising its repo rate to 18% from 11.5% and increasing the volume of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  it offers banks at the repo auctions to $5 billion from $1.5 billion.\par \par \uc2 The bank was criticized for a lack of action Monday when the ruble lost more than a 10th of its value against the dollar in the sharpest daily drop in more than a decade. The bank raised the rate by 100 basis points on Thursday, which failed to put a stop to the ruble's decline.\par \par \uc2 In its statement, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it is raising the key rate by 650 basis points as it needs to "limit devaluation and inflation risks, which have risen seriously" recently.\par \par \uc2 In a separate statement, the bank said it is fixing the rate for infrastructure-project loans at 9%, 800 basis points below the key rate.\par \par \uc2 The ruble's drop Monday--the largest in a single day since 1999--brings the slump to nearly half since the start of the year and more than one-fifth this month. As the ruble fell, \uc2 Russia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said the economy could shrink by as much as 4.7% next year if oil averages $60 a barrel, not far below the current price of Brent crude, the global benchmark.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's dive and forecasts of heightened economic pain raise the stakes for President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 , who is locked in a confrontation with the West over his \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The Kremlin's options for stemming the slide are narrowing. Earlier this year, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold nearly $75 billion to prop up the ruble, but with little effect. It allowed the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to float freely in November, and has spent some $6 billion since then.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had been shying away from large-scale interest-rate increases, which have the potential to cripple the economy.\par \par \uc2 On Monday, before the rate increases, traders and analysts said they were struggling to comprehend the strategy of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which has more than $400 billion in foreign reserves. Analysts say the money has the potential to protect Russia from a full-blown financial crisis.\par \par \uc2 The bank on Monday reiterated its view that the ruble is undervalued by as much as one-fifth, saying the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will recover once the price of oil stabilizes. Officials have blamed speculators for the ruble's plunge.\par \par \uc2 Timothy Ash, an analyst at Standard Bank in London, said there was a risk that the population might panic, pulling deposits from Russian banks, although there were no signs of that in Moscow on Monday evening.\par \par \uc2 Hit by capital flight, a sluggish economic performance and Western sanctions over Ukraine, the ruble has been depreciating steadily in the second half of this year. In January, one dollar bought 33 rubles. A rapid drop in oil prices, which started in September, boosted the trend and sent the ruble to record lows.\par \par \uc2 Some lawmakers have called for the government to impose capital controls, limiting the ability of companies and individuals to take money out of the country, but government officials have ruled out that possibility. Monday, the bank increased its forecast of the net amount of capital that is expected to leave Russia this year to $134 billion, from $128 billion in November.\par \par \uc2 Capital controls likely would be an instrument of last resort, given the risk of undermining investors' confidence in Russia, said Phoenix Kalen, emerging-markets strategist at \uc2 Soci\u233\'00\'E9t\u233\'00\'E9 G\u233\'00\'E9n\u233\'00\'E9rale SA\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "The question is whether that commitment to uphold market legitimacy can withstand both the geopolitical crisis and the collapse in oil prices," the strategist said.\par \par \uc2 Surveys show Russians are increasingly nervous about the ruble's slide, but there are few signs of protest. Mr. Putin's approval rating remains at more than 70%, after a spike upward earlier this year when Russia annexed Ukraine's Crimea peninsula. He has no domestic political rivals, and street protests against his rule in 2011 petered out.\par \par \uc2 Poorer people--Mr. Putin's political base--spend mostly in rubles and don't have large savings or take vacations abroad, so they are somewhat insulated from the ruble's weakness. Retailers are phasing in price changes to try to shield consumers from rapid rises.\par \par \uc2 "It's all not so shocking. Part of society thinks that the president has foreseen everything and will save" the situation, said Alexei Makarenko, a political analyst at the Center for Political Technologies, a Moscow think tank. "Another part thinks, 'It's bad, of course, but what can you do?' "\par \par \uc2 There was no comment from the Kremlin on Monday. The presidential website published a transcript of a meeting between Mr. Putin and his minister for housing.\par \par \uc2 Russian state television has largely ignored the ruble's decline. Monday night's main newscast on the Rossiya-1 channel had no mention of the record-breaking plunge even as the decline continued during the program. Weekend news programs focused on the reported success of the government's efforts to control rising prices.\par \par \uc2 Monday, the dollar rose to a record high of 66.2 rubles in trading after the market officially closed, before pulling back slightly. Trading volumes were low because many investors are hanging back.\par \par \uc2 Russian authorities, including Mr. Putin, have vowed to stamp out speculative activity they say is behind the ruble's decline. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which acts as the country's financial markets regulator, on Monday ordered the Moscow stock exchange to stop trading in some derivatives for certain clients, citing "possible market manipulations."\par \par \uc2 Moscow's dollar-denominated RTS Index was down 10% Monday, while the ruble-denominated Micex lost 2.4%.\par \par \uc2 Write to Alexander Kolyandr at alexander.kolyandr@wsj.com, Andrey Ostroukh at andrey.ostroukh@wsj.com and Chiara Albanese at chiara.albanese@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 December 15, 2014 18:48 ET (23:48 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020141215eacf0048c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw90\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1350\pichgoal600
