\par\par \b \uc2 BARING EMERGING \uc2 EUROPE PLC\uc2  - Final Results\b0\par\par\uc2 NORTHERN TRUST; PR Newswire   \par \uc2 25,074 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 November 2014\par 08:00\par \uc2 PR Newswire UK Disclose\par \uc2 PRNDIS\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014 PR Newswire Europe Limited.  All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Annual Report & Audited\par \par Financial Statements\par \par for the year ended 30 September 2014\par \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Baring Emerging Europe PLC\f28 \uc2 \f2 \par \par Contents\par \par Directors and officers                  2\par \par Financial highlights                    3\par \par Performance                             3\par \par Discount                                3\par \par Investment objective                    3\par \par The Alternative Investment Fund Manager 3\par \par Financial calendar                      4\par \par Special considerations and risk factors 4\par \par Chairman's statement                    5\par \par Report of the Alternative Investment    8\par Fund Manager:\par \par Review                                  8\par \par Equity portfolio                        12\par \par Classification of assets                14\par \par Strategic report                        15\par \par Report of the Directors                 20\par \par Statement of Corporate Governance       25\par report\par \par Audit Committee report                  31\par \par Statement of Directors'\par responsibilities\par \par in respect of the Annual Report and the 32\par financial statements\par \par Directors' remuneration report          33\par \par Independent Auditor's report            36\par \par Income statement                        38\par \par Balance sheet                           39\par \par Reconciliation of movement in           40\par shareholders' funds\par \par Cashflow statement                      41\par \par Notes to the accounts                   42\par \par Notice of Annual General Meeting        54\par \par Notes to the Notice of Annual General   56\par Meeting\par \par Directors and officers\par \par Directors\par \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Steven Bates\f28 \uc2 \f2 , Chairman\par Josephine Dixon\par Saul Estrin\par Jonathan Woollett\par Ivo Coulson\par Frances Daley (appointed 29 April 2014)\par \par Secretary\par \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 M. J. Nokes\f28 \uc2 \f2 , F.C.A.\par \par Registered office\par \par 155 Bishopsgate\par London EC2M 3XY\par Company number 4560726\par \par Alternative Investment Fund Manager\par \par Baring Fund Managers Limited\par 155 Bishopsgate\par London EC2M 3XY\par Telephone: 020 7628 6000\par Facsimile: 020 7638 7928\par \par Auditor\par \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 KPMG LLP\f28 \uc2 \f2 \par 15 Canada Square\par London E14 5GL\par \par Custodian & Depositary\par \par State Street Bank & Trust Company Limited\par 20 Churchill Place\par Canary Wharf\par London E14 5HJ\par \par Administrator\par \par Northern Trust Global Services Limited\par 50 Bank Street\par Canary Wharf\par London E14 5NT\par Telephone: 0207 982 2000\par \par Registrars and transfer office\par \par Capita Asset Services\par The Registry\par 34 Beckenham Road\par Beckenham\par Kent BR3 4TU\par Telephone: 0871 664 0300\par (calls cost 10 pence per minute plus network extras)\par Overseas: +44 208 639 3399\par Email: ssd@capitaregistrars.com\par \par Website\par \par \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.bee-plc.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.bee-plc.com}}}\uc2 \f2 \par \par Financial highlights\par \par                                                 2014                      2013\par \par Net asset value per                          695.92p                   846.16p\par ordinary share ("NAV")\par \par Revenue return per                            18.55p                    19.44p\par ordinary share\par \par Dividends per ordinary                        19.00p                    19.00p\par share\par \par Share price                                  614.50p                   745.50p\par \par Ongoing charges (based on                      1.46%                     1.30%\par average NAV)\par \par Performance (total return basis)\par \par                                                    Year ended 30 September 2014\par \par Net asset value per ordinary share#                                      -15.9%\par \par Share price #                                                            -15.4%\par \par Benchmark*                                                               -13.3%\par \par *The Benchmark Index is the MSCI EM Europe 10/40 Index.\par \par #Source: AIC.\par \par Discount (at 30 September)\par \par                                                 2014                      2013\par \par Discount to net asset                          11.7%                     11.9%\par value per share*\par \par *Based on the net asset value including income.\par \par Investment objective\par \par The investment objective is to achieve long-term capital growth, principally\par through investment in securities listed on or traded on an Emerging European\par securities market or in securities of companies listed or traded elsewhere,\par whose revenues and/or profits are, or are expected to be, derived from\par activities in Emerging Europe.\par \par The Alternative Investment Fund Manager\par \par The Alternative Investment Fund Manager is Baring Fund Managers Limited which\par is authorised and regulated by the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Financial Conduct Authority\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\par \par Financial calendar\par \par                                                                            Date\par \par Annual general meeting for 2014                                 15 January 2015\par \par Announcement of interim results                                             May\par \par Announcement of final results                                          November\par \par Interim report posted                                                       May\par \par Annual report posted                                                   December\par \par The Company's share price is published in the Financial Times.\par \par Special considerations and risk factors\par \par Shareholders should be aware that the value of the Company's Shares and the\par income from them may fluctuate. In addition, there is no guarantee that the\par market prices of shares in investment trusts will fully reflect their\par underlying Net Asset Value.\par \par The risks inherent in investments by the Company in Emerging Europe are of a\par nature and degree not typically encountered in investing in securities of\par companies listed on the major securities markets. Such risks are both political\par and economic and in addition to the normal risks inherent in any equity\par investment.\par \par Investments in the Company should be regarded as long-term in nature. There can\par be no guarantee that the Company's investment objectives will be achieved.\par \par BFM and the Company are required to make certain disclosures available to\par investors in accordance with the Alternative Investment Fund Managers Directive\par ("AIFMD"). Those disclosures that are required to be made pre-investment can be\par found on the Company's website \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.bee-plc.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.bee-plc.com}}}\uc2 \f2  under the prospectus and\par literature heading, the document is titled "Pre-investment disclosures", dated\par 21 July 2014. There have been no material changes to the disclosures contained\par within the document since publication in July 2014.\par \par Chairman's statement\par \par Dear Shareholder,\par \par The Greek philosopher Heraclitus is mostly remembered for the phrase `Always\par expect the unexpected'. In investment, there are so many possible unexpected\par outcomes that it is impossible to devise strategies that work in all cases, but\par 2014 proved that sometimes there are no strategies that would have worked\par except being out of the market altogether. The Russian annexation of Crimea and\par the subsequent war in Eastern Ukraine came out of the blue and have set the\par stage for what has been a poor year in BEE's markets.\par \par Quite apart from what was happening in Russia, political problems in Turkey and\par a faltering Eurozone economy were the icing on a pretty unappetizing cake.\par Matthias Siller, your investment manager, talks about all of this in more\par detail in his investment review, which follows, but I thought I should set out\par the Board's perspective on what is happening in Russia in particular.\par \par While the Crimean annexation was mostly opportunist, the situation in Eastern\par Ukraine, which the Russians refer to as a `frozen war,' is more characteristic\par of policies which have been seen elsewhere. The objective is to sow instability\par without going to the expense of integrating the destabilized area into Greater\par Russia. This strategy is popular in Putin's heartland, is bolstered by\par government control of the media and is a mechanism which is being used, perhaps\par coincidentally, to increase the role of the State in commercial enterprise,\par especially in sectors where there is a geopolitical angle, such as oil and gas.\par Western sanctions, by virtue of having worked, have embedded these factors.\par Declines in the stock market and in the Rouble have followed, and Russia is now\par an investment pariah. Matthias explains in his review how this has panned out\par for a company called Sistema, which was held in the portfolio. This company,\par one of the better governed in Russia, is locked in a dispute about the\par ownership rights to its assets with organs of the State.\par \par From an investment perspective, therefore, our biggest task is how to deal with\par the Russian conundrum. If one can rid oneself of the sour taste of what is\par happening, valuations in the market are now at levels which assume the worst.\par For investors who can wait until the valley floor is crossed, this could be the\par investment opportunity of a generation, but the width of the valley is unknown.\par \par Please forgive the rant on this subject, but I did want to illustrate the\par complexity of the problem for an investor, even one with specialized expertise\par in the region such as BEE. Russia has been such a distraction that it is\par important not to let it overwhelm the excellent medium term outlook in our\par other markets. Central Europe grows ever more prosperous, despite what is\par happening in the Eurozone; Turkey has an opportunity to put the political\par disruption of the last couple of years behind it; and even Greece offers\par interesting opportunities. This region remains the most lowly valued in the\par world, with the good being punished alongside the bad. This provides unusually\par fertile ground for us.\par \par The rest of this statement is more usual, in that it will cover the investment\par returns, discount management and governance as well as the rather boring\par regulatory nonsense with which we have had to struggle with during the year.\par \par Investment Returns\par \par For our fiscal year, the Net Asset Value ("NAV") calculated on a total return\par basis, fell by 15.9%. In contrast, the benchmark fell by 13.3%. While this\par return is behind the benchmark, and so by definition disappointing, it should\par be noted that in markets such as those we have seen over the past year, the\par outcome is somewhat random. There are a number of reasons for this. First, the\par weight of Russia in the benchmark (54%) and our position relative to that\par benchmark is by far the most significant factor weighing on relative return.\par As we did not expect the turn events took, we were overweight the Russian\par market at the start of its swoon and this created a deficit which would have\par been impossible to close without throwing caution to the wind. Second, the\par marked increase in volatility took its toll as we repositioned the portfolio\par for the new order. Third, analysis of the quality of a company had no bearing\par on its contribution to return. As Matthias explains, Sistema, which is a very\par decent company by Russian standards, accounted for around half the whole\par portfolio's underperformance by itself. Normal investment rules were in effect\par suspended and fundamental corporate virtues identified by the investment team\par went unrewarded.\par \par On the positive side of the ledger, stock selection in Poland added significant\par value, and the market rose despite its proximity to the deflationary slowdown\par unfolding in the Eurozone. We also benefitted from being underweight in Turkey\par during a year when that market also fell.\par \par As you know, we also look at peer group performance and here too, it was not a\par great year. We were ranked 37th out of 51 peers, a third quartile position\par which is not where we expect to be over the long run. Indeed, in the longer\par term, over 2, 3 and 5 years we remain towards the top of the second quartile\par and over ten years we are firmly top quartile. Given the calibre and resources\par of the investment team, the Board remains confident that the longer term\par performance of your Company will not disappoint and that the outcome for the\par current year reflects a number of somewhat unusual factors.\par \par Discount Management\par \par Throughout the year, the discount has averaged 11.15%, within a range of 6.73%\par to 14.98%. We have operated the buyback programme during the year ended 30\par September 2014, and we have bought back 1,344,000 shares for cancellation.\par These repurchases, made at an average discount of 11.2%, have enhanced the NAV\par by approximately 6 pence per share this year. Subsequent to the year end a\par further 131,000 shares have been repurchased for cancellation. Although we have\par bought back fewer shares than last year, the Board is conscious of the risks of\par shrinking the Company's capital to the point where stock market liquidity is\par adversely affected. Over the last five years, we have bought back 17,729,666\par shares for a total cost of \u163\'00\'A3136.4 million, not so very far from our current\par NAV.\par \par It is not surprising that investor interest in Emerging Europe has faded. Quite\par apart from the geopolitical hiccup of 2014, returns over the long run have\par lagged developed markets and been disappointing. This is reflected in a decline\par in the number of shares in BEE traded. In the most recent year, 6.4 million\par shares changed hands, compared with 12 million the previous year, itself down\par from nearly 15 million the year before. Emerging Europe has become a\par specialized investment destination and in current circumstances, it seems\par unlikely that the area will easily attract a wider investment following.\par \par We are disappointed that we have not been able to bring the average discount in\par below 10% in this difficult period, but will continue to operate the buyback,\par recognizing that discount control is important for our shareholders.\par \par Governance\par \par Regulation\par \par As foreshadowed last year, we have spent a lot of time, energy and money\par getting ready for the Alternative Investment Fund Management Directive\par ("AIFMD"). We finally put this to bed in July, having spent \u163\'00\'A369,000 and\par committing to an additional ongoing cost of 2 basis points per annum paid to a\par `depositary'. I am afraid we had no choice about any of this. It isn't\par surprising that the tide of regulation in our industry is moving as it is, but\par it is a shame that the nature of that regulation brings nothing to the benefit\par of the shareholder. In practice, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of AIFMD will create a little\par extra bureaucracy but the major expense is now behind us. There will no doubt\par be further regulation in due course, about which we can probably do little.\par \par Dividend and Income Account\par \par The revenue return per ordinary share (on a weighted average basis) was 18.55\par pence compared to 19.44 pence last year. As explained in last year's annual\par report, this year we are applying half of our investment management fee to the\par capital account. This has had the effect of boosting the income available for\par dividend payments compared to last year. On a like for like basis, this year's\par revenue per share should be compared to an amount last time of 22.74 pence.\par \par I set out in my last statement the view that dividends were becoming a more\par reliable source of return for investors in our region. I spoke too soon. As\par Matthias explains in his review, it seems likely that Russian companies, which\par is where the bulk of our income originates, will come under pressure to\par reinvest in their businesses rather than pay dividends. This is the direct\par result of sanctions cutting off corporate access to international capital\par markets. What this means is as yet unclear, but it makes forecasting income for\par the year ahead an impossible task. We must wait and see. As before, the\par portfolio is not managed with an income target in mind, with the revenue\par surplus resulting from the process rather than being the objective of that\par process.\par \par That said, this year, we are recommending a dividend of 19 pence per share.\par Whilst this is higher than the reported revenue return per share of 18.55p,\par which is calculated on a weighted average of the shares in issue during the\par year, the impact of the share buybacks discussed above means that we can pay\par 19p per share and still retain a small revenue surplus for the year.\par \par Borrowing\par \par In anticipation of a better year than we have had, we arranged a borrowing\par facility for $25 million with State Street. This is as yet undrawn, but remains\par available to Matthias should the investment environment offer exceptional\par opportunity.\par \par Directorate\par \par We are continuing our succession planning exercise and this year Jo Dixon will\par be stepping down as a director and as Chair of the Audit Committee. Jo has been\par on the Board for 10 years and has brought professionalism, depth and humour to\par the role. It is normal to say that we will miss her but in this case, that\par sentiment is genuine rather than boilerplate. It is also normal to say that she\par will be a hard act to follow, which she will, but we think we have found\par someone who can in the shape of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Frances Daley\f28 \uc2 \f2 . Frances has had a career as a\par Finance Director out in the real world and was appointed on 29th April this\par year. As the forthcoming AGM is the first following her appointment, she will\par be standing for election, and I urge you to support her nomination.\par \par The rest of us are all standing for re-election and will do henceforth on an\par annual basis (unless retiring), as is now thought to be best practice.\par \par Shareholder Communication\par \par I always say here that we are ready as a Board to talk to any shareholder about\par anything, and that is certainly true. Most of you, of course, would rather\par listen to Matthias and his views on markets than hear me drone on about\par corporate governance, but at the AGM you get the chance to get both. Matthias\par will be giving his customary presentation on the investment scene in Emerging\par Europe before we conduct the formal business of the meeting. Details of when\par and where the AGM will be held can be found on page 54 of this report.\par \par Outlook\par \par What happened in Russia has been, rather unfairly, a setback to the development\par of a more robust investment case for the Emerging European region as a whole.\par It has demonstrated how easily a fragile institutional structure can be\par derailed by politics. Despite the power games being played out in Turkey, and\par the dependence of Central Europe on the Eurozone, prospects for good long run\par investment returns in this region have only been enhanced by the value created\par by the sell-off.\par \par Companies are still getting better. The world is becoming more interconnected\par and growth opportunities in these markets dwarf anything to be found in the\par developed world. Setbacks such as we have seen drive stock markets to valuation\par levels that fully reflect the risks. The major uncertainty remains timing.\par Investors who have the stamina will undoubtedly benefit as conditions\par normalize, but it would be wrong to ignore the possibility that things might\par get worse first. We did not foresee what would happen this year, and there are\par no predictions here except to repeat the other aphorism for which Heraclitus is\par remembered - `There is nothing permanent except change'.\par \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Steven Bates\f28 \uc2 \f2 \par Chairman\par \par 24 November 2014\par \par Report of the Alternative Investment Fund Manager\par \par for the year ended 30 September 2014\par \par How we manage the Company\par \par At Barings, we believe that a sound research process is the starting point of\par any successful investment approach. In our view, it is most effective to\par analyse both companies and countries, with the goal of investing in the most\par attractive companies in the most attractive countries.\par \par Our research focuses on growth at a reasonable price, on sensitivity to\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  movements, and to other external factors; on the soundness or\par otherwise of government policy (in the case of a country), or business plan (in\par the case of a company); and last but not least, on the level of valuation. This\par research gives rise to an assessment of the fundamental drivers of return, and\par to this we add a subjective judgement as to the level of return we expect from\par each asset in which we might invest. We also check that these rankings are\par consistent with the broader thematic developments we expect as a firm. These\par rankings then allow us to construct a disciplined and relatively concentrated\par portfolio of our most attractive candidates.\par \par Performance\par \par The Company's performance over the last 12 months was -15.9%, underperforming\par the MSCI Emerging Europe 10/40 index which returned -13.3%.\par \par While stock selection in Turkey, Hungary, Greece and Poland contributed\par positively to performance, stock selection and a slightly overweight position\par in the Russian market kept performance behind the index.\par \par The Company's ranking within the peer group of Emerging European funds is below\par median over the 12 month period, but ahead of median over the longer term, as\par measured over 2, 3, 5 and 10 years.\par \par The year was characterised by increasing political tensions and regional\par conflicts, falling commodity prices and diverging GDP growth patterns across\par the region. Although volatility in global developed equity markets reached\par multi-year lows during the period, many parts of the emerging European markets\par stayed highly volatile. Emerging European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets also came\par under considerable pressure, especially in the past two months. In general,\par though, this weakness does not reflect problems in emerging Europe itself but\par rather the ripple effect from weakness in the Euro prompted by disappointing\par European growth numbers.\par \par The Turkish and Russian stock exchanges, the largest in the region, exhibited\par the most volatile behaviour over the last 12 months as geopolitical conflicts\par and political risks affected equity markets. These risks were, however,\par unrelated, highlighting the importance of a geographically diversified\par portfolio. The volatility of the portfolio has been substantially lower than\par either the volatility of the Russian or Turkish market.\par \par In Turkey, the alleged involvement of high ranking AK Party and government\par members in a corruption probe in December 2013 led to a direct confrontation\par between US-based Islamic scholar and preacher Fethullah Gulen and Prime\par Minister Erdogan. The conservative supporters of Gulen, many of whom hold high\par level posts in the police and judiciary, were widely seen as instigators of\par police raids, which made the arrests connected with the corruption case all the\par more delicate. While highly emotional, the confrontation quickly found its way\par onto the political stage where regional elections in March served as a\par referendum on Prime Minister Erdogan's policies. The AK Party's success in\par Istanbul and large areas of the country meant that the Turkish electorate had\par given a clear vote of confidence in Mr Erdogan, who managed to maintain\par positive momentum into the presidential election campaign in the summer. Mr\par Erdogan did, indeed, secure the presidency, but with a smaller margin than\par originally expected. Mr Erdogan plans to give the role of President more power\par and needs a constitutional change so to do. This will require a 60% vote in\par favour of change in Parliament and a referendum. His political stance might\par well become more conciliatory over the coming months in order to secure\par broad-based backing for his plans. The Turkish economy saw consumer confidence\par recovering in the aftermath of the municipal elections. However, relatively\par weak demand in Europe, the Syrian and Iraqi wars and a high (but peaking)\par inflation environment kept further acceleration of economic activity at bay.\par \par In Russia, economic development was held ransom by politics as the country's\par annexation of the Crimea and its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventionist\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  attitude in the separatist\par movements in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ukraine\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's eastern districts of Luhansk and Donetsk sparked\par widespread international condemnation and economic sanctions.\par \par The sanctions, implemented by the EU and US, aim both at individuals in the\par political establishment and on state controlled companies. They restrict the\par ability of individual businesses to co-operate with Western companies as well\par as severely limiting access to international financial markets.\par Further, they have created an environment of insecurity, leading to an\par acceleration of capital outflows, exacerbating what were already relatively\par low levels of liquidity in the country and simultaneously weakening the Russian\par Rouble. In that sense, Western policy towards Russia can be described as\par successful as its goal of undermining trust in the economy and creating tension\par has been achieved.\par \par One indicator pointing in this direction could be the sudden attack led by\par state prosecutors on one of the largest conglomerates in the country, Sistema.\par Alleged irregularities over the privatization of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bashneft\f28 \uc2 \f2 , an oil and gas\par company contributing approximately 40% to the conglomerate's net asset value,\par are being cited by state prosecutors as reasons why an official investigation\par has been launched over the behaviour of the company and its management board. A\par cynic might say that Sistema, which follows good international practice with\par regard to disclosure, has pursued a successful investment strategy and has no\par debt, made an irresistible target for a bureaucracy that seeks to increase the\par share of the state in the most strategic of all sectors: oil and gas.\par \par While the summer months saw the hottest phase in the armed confrontation\par between the regular Ukrainian army and Russian backed separatist movements in\par the Eastern part of Ukraine, the international pressure started to have an\par effect on the Russian side and created an environment where both sides\par preferred de-escalation.\par \par The subsequent round of talks between Ukraine's Prime Minister Petro\par Poroshenko, Russia's President Vladimir Putin and EU negotiators in Minsk saw a\par peace deal brokered, and armed confrontation has since drastically declined in\par Ukraine. This, together with surprisingly rapid agreement on the commercial\par terms for Russian gas deliveries to the Ukraine, suggest a significant\par reduction in tension. Over the course of the year, economic growth in Russia\par has slowed substantially and fixed capital formation registered a 2.5%\par contraction. While we believe recent developments show the willingness of the\par Russian leadership to engage in a dialogue over its Ukrainian relationship, a\par gradual Rouble devaluation to encourage import substitution and improve the\par competitiveness of the commodity sector may well suit policy makers. A\par pronounced reduction of inventories during recent months will also need to be\par replaced at some point, shoring up growth in 2015 if the geopolitical\par environment allows for it.\par \par While the small, open economies of Central Europe saw a confirmation of the\par improving economic environment of early 2014, a weaker European economy started\par to take its toll on leading indicators over the summer. Rapidly falling\par inflation rates and in some cases even deflationary trends have brought\par government bond yields to record lows in Poland, Hungary and Romania.\par Additionally this environment gave local Central Banks ample room to cut rates\par and support domestic demand while renewed EU-financed infrastructure spending\par should serve as an additional boost to economies.\par \par As pointed out earlier, in the presence of strong disinflationary pressures\par (energy, food, etc.) we believe that stimulative monetary policy is here to\par stay in Central European countries. Not surprisingly, the market has been\par rewarding companies with sustainable and growing dividend yields - the Polish\par utility Energa and insurer PZU, both falling into this category, were amongst\par the strongest contributors to performance over the course of the last 12\par months. Sistema, on the other hand, was the leading loss-making position\par relative to benchmark in the portfolio even though we took the decision to sell\par the largest part of our holding the day the bad news broke. The Greek stock\par market was added to the Emerging European universe in November 2013 and saw a\par number of bank recapitalisations in 2014. This, combined with consolidation\par amongst the banks, has equipped the sector to withstand adverse credit quality\par development. Furthermore, the Greek banking sector has re-priced its deposit\par base, lowering its financing costs and improving core profitability.\par \par The portfolio's income from dividends remained at a record high level,\par generating more than 3% of NAV in income.\par \par Company weighting versus Benchmark Index by country of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at 30\par September 2014\par \par Country of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2                          Company                  Benchmark\par \par Czech Republic                                  0.0%                       2.8%\par \par Greece                                          5.1%                       7.4%\par \par Hungary                                         4.3%                       2.3%\par \par Poland                                         12.4%                      19.7%\par \par Russia                                         53.9%                      50.1%\par \par Turkey                                         15.0%                      17.7%\par \par Other                                           8.5%                          -\par \par Cash                                            0.8%                          -\par \par                                               100.0%                     100.0%\par \par Source: Barings, MSCI.\par \par Strategy\par \par The Company seeks to invest into Emerging European companies with above average\par growth potential, strong balance sheets and high standards of corporate\par governance.\par \par While recent developments surrounding Ukraine give some hope for further\par de-escalation, the negative impact on Russian business has certainly been felt.\par The 3 main vectors along which these policies affect our strategy are as\par follows:\par \par First, the Russian state will play a more active role in business and, while\par seeking to mitigate the effects of international sanctions, will adopt a policy\par that will favour higher re-investment rates over dividend payouts in strategic\par sectors.\par \par Second, on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  front, the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Russian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may well favour a\par gradual depreciation of the Russian Rouble as this is the key to unlocking a\par powerful self-help mechanism which leads to import substitution, so supporting\par the Kremlin's main constituency in rural areas and mining communities. In these\par circumstances, inflation will remain elevated.\par \par Third, while we can see a scenario in 2015 where the relationship with the\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 European Union\f28 \uc2 \f2  and the US improves on the back of the mutual interest in\par de-escalation, the immediate impact on the Russian economy might not\par necessarily change the economic outlook. While access to international bond and\par money markets would help to supply liquidity and support growth, we sense that\par it will take substantial trust building exercises by the Russian government to\par revive confidence in companies, small and large, and allow for a pick-up in\par investment led growth.\par \par With Russian oil stocks outperforming in a weak Rouble environment, we decided\par to reduce the portfolio's exposure to the energy sector by selling out of\par Rosneft, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Tatneft\f28 \uc2 \f2  and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Surgutneftegas\f28 \uc2 \f2 . Anticipating pressure on European refinery\par margins, the Turkish refiner Tupras was also sold, which reduced the weighting\par of the Energy sector to 22 %.\par \par We increased our holding in Mail.ru, the largest Russian gaming platform and\par owner of the leading social network site VKontakte full control of which was\par recently secured on attractive terms. Additionally, we invested in \f28 \uc2 \f2 Luxoft\f28 \uc2 \f2 , a\par software developer for the industrial and financial sector.\par \par In Turkey, we concentrated our positions by selling out of retailer BIM and\par bottler \f28 \uc2 \f2 Coca Cola Icecek\f28 \uc2 \f2  while adding tyre producer Brisa.\par \par In Poland, we increased exposure to the Polish financial sector and added to\par our position in PZU, the country's largest insurer. A falling interest rate\par environment enhances the attractiveness of the insurer's high dividend-payout\par ratio.\par \par Holdings in the basic materials sector were decreased by selling out of the\par Polish copper miner KGHM and reducing the position in the largest Russian\par mining company \f28 \uc2 \f2 Norilsk Nickel\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\par \par By participating in the initial public offerings of the Polish electricity\par utility Energa and the Romanian utility Electrica, we reduced our underweight\par in this sector.\par \par Outlook\par \par Looking forward, we see a broad based, diversified set of opportunities for the\par Company. Investor expectations are depressed because of the crisis in the\par Ukraine, and while energy prices have been falling substantially over the last\par quarter, Russian self-help in the form of import substitution might\par substantially soften the blow. Valuations are very low.\par \par Turkey, although having experienced substantial volatility over the course of\par the last 12 months, should be one of the largest beneficiaries of lower global\par energy prices. Additionally the extremely busy election calendar over the last\par couple of years now comes to an end. After having voted in the 2015\par parliamentary elections, Turks will be able to enjoy a record period of almost\par 5 years without major elections, paving the way for the acceleration of the\par structural reform agenda promoted by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 ministry of finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and the ministry\par of economics.\par \par Central European markets are likely to suffer from subdued economic activity in\par their main trading partner (Germany), but will be able to tap into EU cohesion\par funds which should balance somewhat the impact of poor German demand.\par \par Equity portfolio\par \par The Company's equity portfolio at 30 September 2014, is set out in the\par following table:\par \par                 Holding         Primary country  Market value \u163\'00\'A3     % of equity\par                                 of investment               000       portfolio\par \par 1               \f28 \uc2 \f2 Lukoil Holdings\f28 \uc2 \f2  Russia                   14,326           10.96\par \par 2               \f28 \uc2 \f2 Sberbank        Russia\f28 \uc2 \f2                     9,845            7.53\par \par 3               PZU             Poland                    7,943            6.08\par \par 4               Novatek         Russia                    7,629            5.84\par \par 5               Halk Bank       Turkey                    6,652            5.09\par \par 6               Gazprom         Russia                    6,488            4.96\par \par 7               OTP Bank        Hungary                   5,490     