013          70.9     2.3    6.7      0.3   14.8   2.8     2.2     - 100.0\par \par Baring Fund Managers Limited\par \par 13 October 2014\par \par Strategic report for the year ended 30 September 2014\par \par The Directors submit to the shareholders their Strategic report, Director's\par report and the audited financial statements of the Company for the year ended\par 30 September 2014.\par \par Business and tax status\par \par In the opinion of the Directors, the Company has conducted its affairs during\par the period under review, and subsequently, so as to maintain its status as an\par investment trust for the purposes of Chapter 4 of Part 24 of the Corporation\par Tax Act 2010. The Company has obtained written approval as an investment trust\par from \f28 \uc2 \f2 HM Revenue & Customs\f28 \uc2 \f2  for all accounting periods up to the year ended\par \par 30 September 2013 and has made a successful application under Regulation 5 of\par the Investment Trust (Approved Company) (Tax) Regulations 2011 for investment\par trust status to apply to all accounting periods starting on or after 1 October\par 2013 subject to the Company continuing to meet the eligibility conditions\par contained in Section 1158 of the Corporation Tax Act 2010 and the ongoing\par requirements outlined in Chapter 3 of Part 2 of the Regulations.\par \par The Company is an investment company as defined in Section 833 of the Companies\par Act 2006. The Company is not a close company for taxation purposes.\par \par Alternative Investment Fund Management Directive (" AIFMD")\par \par In order to comply with AIFMD, the Company has appointed Baring Fund Managers\par Limited ("BFM") to act as its Alternative Investment Fund Manager ("AIFM")\par pursuant to an Alternative Investment Fund Management Agreement entered into by\par the Company and the AIFM on 21 July 2014 (the "AIFM Agreement"). BFM has been\par approved as an AIFM by the UK's \f28 \uc2 \f2 Financial Conduct Authority\f28 \uc2 \f2 . The investment\par management agreement entered into by the Company and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Baring Asset Management\par Limited\f28 \uc2 \f2  ("BAM") on 12 November 2002 (the "IMA") has been terminated although\par BFM has delegated the portfolio management of the Company's portfolio of assets\par to BAM. The AIFM Agreement is based on the IMA and differs to the extent\par necessary to ensure that the relationship between the Company and BFM is\par compliant with the requirements of AIFMD. The fees payable to BFM and the\par notice period under the AIFM Agreement are unchanged from the IMA. The Company\par and BFM have also entered into a Depositary Agreement with State Street\par Trustees Limited ("State Street") pursuant to which State Street has been\par appointed as the Company's Depositary for the purposes of AIFMD.\par \par The Company is managed by external parties in respect of investment management,\par custodial services and the day-to-day accounting and company secretarial\par requirements. As noted above the Alternative Investment Fund Manager is BFM and\par details of the agreement with BFM are given in note 3 to the accounts. The\par Depositary and Custodian is State Street Bank & Trust Company Limited.\par Secretarial services are provided by Northern Trust Global Services Limited.\par The Company has no employees. The Directors are all non-executive.\par \par Investment objective\par \par The investment objective is to achieve long-term capital growth, principally\par through investment in securities listed on or traded on an Emerging European\par securities market or in securities of companies listed or traded elsewhere,\par whose revenues and/or profits are, or are expected to be, derived from\par activities in Emerging Europe.\par \par Investment policy\par \par The policy of the Directors is that, in normal market conditions, the portfolio\par of the Company should consist primarily of diversified securities listed or\par traded on Emerging European securities markets (including over the counter\par markets). Equity securities for this purpose include equity-related instruments\par such as preference shares, convertible securities, options, warrants and other\par rights to subscribe for or acquire, or relating to, equity securities. The\par Company may also invest in debt instruments such as bonds, bills, notes,\par certificates of deposit and other debt instruments issued by private and public\par sector entities in Emerging Europe.\par \par In addition, Emerging European exposure may be obtained by indirect means.\par Investments may, for example, be made in securities of companies listed on\par securities markets outside Emerging Europe that derive, or are expected by the\par Directors to derive, the majority of their revenues and/or profits and/or\par growth from activities in Emerging Europe.\par \par The Company may also invest in other funds in order to gain exposure to\par Emerging Europe where, for example, such funds afford one of the few\par practicable means of access to a particular market, or where such a fund\par represents an attractive investment in its own right. The Company will not\par invest more than 15% of its gross assets in other UK listed investment\par companies (including investment trusts).\par \par The Company may from time to time invest in unquoted securities, but the amount\par of such investment is not expected to be material. Furthermore the Board has\par agreed that the maximum exposure to unquoted securities should be restricted to\par 5% of the Company's net assets. At the year end there was one unquoted investment\par valued at nil in the portfolio.\par \par For the purposes of this investment policy the Board has defined Emerging\par Europe as the successor countries of the former Soviet Union, Poland, Hungary,\par the Czech Republic, Slovakia, Turkey, the States of former Yugoslavia, Romania,\par Bulgaria, Albania and Greece. There is no restriction on the proportion that\par may be invested in these countries. During 2014 the Greek equity market was\par reclassified as an emerging market and was included in the MSCI EM Europe 10/40\par Index on 27 November 2014, the Company's benchmark. A resolution to amend the\par investment policy to include Greece was passed at the Annual General Meeting\par held on 14 January 2014.\par \par In addition the Board has agreed that up to 2% of the total assets may be\par invested in other countries provided that any investments made are companies\par listed on a regulated stock exchange.\par \par The Board has agreed that the maximum value of any one investment should not\par exceed 12% of the Company's total portfolio save with the prior written consent\par of the Board. Where excess occurs due to market movement the manager will\par notify the Board of this and will reduce the holding to below 12% within six\par months.\par \par In addition to the above restriction on investment in a single company the\par Board seeks to achieve a spread of risk in the portfolio through monitoring the\par country and sector weightings of the portfolio. There will be a minimum of 30\par stocks in the portfolio.\par \par The Company's Articles provide that the Company may borrow an amount equal to\par its share capital and reserves. At 30 September 2014, the only loan facility in\par place was a US$25 million loan facility with State Street Bank and Trust\par Company Limited which can be used as a source of gearing. In order to provide a\par mechanism to gear the portfolio the Board has authorised the Alternative\par Investment Fund Manager to invest in long only derivatives in Polish, Russian\par and Turkish index futures where feasible. The Alternative Investment Fund\par Manager has discretion to operate with an overall exposure of the portfolio to\par the market of between 90% and 110%, to include the effect of any derivative\par positions. Once again gearing was not employed during the year.\par \par Return per ordinary share\par \par                        30         30         30         30         30         30\par                 September  September  September  September  September  September\par                      2014       2014       2014       2013       2013       2013\par \par                   Revenue    Capital      Total    Revenue    Capital      Total\par \par Return per         18.55p  (153.31)p  (134.76)p     19.44p     54.76p     74.20p\par ordinary share\par \par Revenue return (earnings) per ordinary share is based on the net revenue on\par ordinary activities after taxation of \u163\'00\'A33,621,000 (2013: \u163\'00\'A34,239,000). Capital\par return per ordinary share is based on net capital losses for the financial year\par of \u163\'00\'A329,918,000 (2013: net capital profits of \u163\'00\'A311,946,000). These calculations\par are based on the weighted average of 19,515,035 (2013: 21,815,561) ordinary\par shares in issue during the year.\par \par At 30 September 2014 there were 18,904,043 ordinary shares of 10 pence each in\par issue (2013: 20,248,043) which excludes 3,318,207 ordinary shares held in\par treasury (2013: 3,318,207 shares held in treasury). The shares held in treasury\par are treated as not being in issue when calculating the weighted average of\par ordinary shares in issue during the year.\par \par Dividends\par \par The Board does not seek to target any particular level of dividend, and intends\par rather to distribute by way of dividend most of the net earnings available for\par this purpose. The Board recommends an annual dividend of 19p per share compared\par with 19p for the previous period. Subject to approval of the Annual General\par Meeting, the recommended annual dividend will be paid on 2 February 2015 to\par members on the register at the close of business on 5 January 2015. The shares\par will be marked ex-dividend on 2 January 2015.\par \par Discount\par \par The Directors have adopted a policy with regard to the market rating of the\par Company's shares and seek to limit the discount to NAV at which the Company's\par shares trade to a level significantly lower than 10%, using as necessary the\par Company's share repurchase authority. During the year ended 30 September 2014,\par 1,344,000 shares were repurchased at a cost of \u163\'00\'A39,772,000 (4,123,000 shares\par were repurchased during the year ended 30 September 2013 at a cost of \u163\'00\'A3\par 30,448,000). Any shares repurchased will either be held in treasury and may be\par issued at a later date at or above net asset value, or cancelled.\par \par As reported last year the Company has changed the period during which the\par shares must trade below a 12% average discount which would trigger a tender\par from the 90 days prior to the results announcement to the full trading year.\par This means that if the shares average greater than a 12% discount for the 365\par day period prior to the publication of the Company's results for the financial\par year, a tender will be triggered as set out above. The relevant NAV number for\par these purposes is the NAV cum income.\par \par Performance\par \par At each Board meeting, the Directors consider a number of performance measures\par to assess the Company's success in achieving its objectives of which the most\par important are as follows:\par \par Performance against the peer group\par \par The Board monitors performance relative to a broad range of competitor funds,\par as defined by the Morningstar Emerging Europe Universe. In the year ended 30\par September 2014 the Company was ranked 37th out of 51 funds in this universe.\par Over three years to 30 September 2014 it was ranked 14th out of 49 funds and\par over five years it was ranked 20th out of 47 funds.\par \par Performance against the Benchmark Index\par \par A chart of NAV performance versus Benchmark Index for the six years ended 30\par September 2014 (total return) is set out in the Directors' Remuneration report\par on page 34.\par \par Discount to NAV\par \par In the year ended 30 September 2014 the shares traded at an average discount of\par 11.15% on the NAV cum income.\par \par Ongoing charges\par \par The annualised ongoing charges figure for the year was 1.46% (2013: 1.30%).\par This figure, which has been prepared in accordance with the recommended\par methodology of \f28 \uc2 \f2 the Association of Investment Companies\f28 \uc2 \f2  represents the annual\par percentage reduction in shareholder returns as a result of recurring\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operational\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  expenses excluding performance fee. No performance fee is payable\par in respect of the year ended 30 September 2014 (2013: no performance fee was\par paid). The Board reviews each year an analysis of the Company's ongoing charges\par figure and a comparison with its peers.\par \par Principal risks\par The key risks to the Company fall broadly under the following categories:\par \par Investment and strategy\par \par The Board regularly reviews the investment mandate and long-term investment\par strategy in relation to the market and economic conditions. The Board also\par regularly monitors the Company's investment performance against the Benchmark\par Index and the peer group and its compliance with the investment guidelines.\par \par Accounting, legal and regulatory\par \par In order to qualify as an investment trust, the Company must comply with the\par provisions contained in Section 1158 of the Corporation Taxes Act 2010. A\par breach of Section 1158 in an accounting period could lead to the Company being\par subject to corporation tax on gains realised in that accounting period. Section\par 1158 qualification criteria are continually monitored by Baring Fund Managers\par Limited and the results reported to the Board at its regular meetings. The\par Company must also comply with the Companies Act and the UKLA Listing Rules. The\par Board relies on the services of the administrator, Northern Trust Global\par Services Limited and its professional advisers to ensure compliance with the\par Companies Act and the UKLA Listing Rules.\par \par Loss of investment team or Alternative Investment Fund Manager\par \par A sudden departure of the Alternative Investment Fund Manager or several\par members of the investment management team could result in a short-term\par deterioration in investment performance. The Alternative Investment Fund\par Manager takes steps to reduce the likelihood of such an event by ensuring\par appropriate succession planning and the adoption of a team-based approach, as\par well as special efforts to retain key personnel.\par \par Discount\par \par A disproportionate widening of the discount relative to the Company's peers\par could result in loss of value for shareholders. The Board regularly discusses\par discount policy and has set parameters for the Company's broker to follow with\par regard to the buy-back of shares.\par \par Corporate governance and shareholder relations\par \par Details of the Company's compliance with corporate governance best practice,\par including information on relations with shareholders, are set out in the\par Corporate Governance report on pages 25 to 30.\par \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Operational\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par \par Like most other investment trust companies, the Company has no employees. The\par Board currently consists of six non-executive Directors, two of whom are female\par and the other four are male and is chaired by \f28 \uc2 \f2 Steven Bates\f28 \uc2 \f2 . The Company\par therefore relies upon the services provided by third parties and is dependent\par on the control systems of the Alternative Investment Fund Manager and the\par Company's service providers. The security, for example, of the Company's\par assets, dealing procedures, accounting records and maintenance of regulatory\par and legal requirements, depend on the effective \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of these systems.\par These are regularly tested and monitored. The Depositary and Custodian and the\par Alternative Investment Fund Manager also produce annual reports on internal\par controls which are reviewed by their respective auditors and give assurance\par regarding the effective \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of controls.\par \par Financial\par \par The financial risks faced by the Company are disclosed in note 19 on pages 50\par to 53.\par \par Future developments\par \par The future development of the Company is much dependent upon the success of the\par Company's investment strategy in the light of economic and equity market\par developments in the countries in which it invests. The Alternative Investment\par Fund Manager discusses the outlook in his report on page 11.\par \par Social, community and human rights\par \par The Company does not have any specific policies on social, community or human\par rights issues as it is an investment company which does not have any physical\par assets, property, employees or \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of its own.\par \par For and on behalf of the Board\par \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Steven Bates\f28 \uc2 \f2 \par \par Chairman\par \par 24 November 2014\par \par Report of the Directors\par \par Directors\par \par The present Directors are listed below and on page 2. They are all\par non-executive and have served throughout the year apart from \f28 \uc2 \f2 Frances Daley\f28 \uc2 \f2  who\par was appointed to the Board on 29 April 2014, the Board consists of two females\par and four males.\par \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Steven Bates\f28 \uc2 \f2  (57) spent 18 years with the Fleming group until 2002, latterly as\par co-head of emerging markets of \f28 \uc2 \f2 JPMorgan\f28 \uc2 \f2  Fleming Asset Management. He has\par extensive experience in both emerging and developed markets. He is a director\par of Guardian Capital which is a specialist asset management business and is also\par the chief investment officer of Salisbury Partners. He is also on the boards of\par a number of financial companies involved in emerging markets. He was appointed\par a Director of Baring Emerging Europe PLC on 27 January 2003 and was appointed\par Chairman of Baring Emerging Europe PLC on 19 January 2010.\par \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Josephine Dixon\f28 \uc2 \f2  (55) is a director of Worldwide Healthcare Trust PLC, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Standard\par Life Equity Income Trust PLC\f28 \uc2 \f2  and \f28 \uc2 \f2 JPMorgan European Investment Trust PLC\f28 \uc2 \f2 , and\par has recently joined the board of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Strategic Equity Capital plc\f28 \uc2 \f2  and is a\par Chartered Accountant who has previously held a number of senior executive\par positions, including that of finance director in a publicly quoted company. She\par was appointed a Director of Baring Emerging Europe PLC on 5 July 2004.\par \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Saul Estrin\f28 \uc2 \f2  (62) is a Professor and the founding Head of the Department of\par Management at the \f28 \uc2 \f2 London School of Economics\f28 \uc2 \f2  where he is a specialist on\par emerging markets. He was formerly a Professor at the \f28 \uc2 \f2 London Business School\f28 \uc2 \f2  and\par Research Director of the Centre for New and Emerging Markets, which analysed\par the prospects for private sector development and business opportunities in\par emerging markets. He has written numerous books and articles on emerging\par economies. He was appointed a Director of Baring Emerging Europe PLC on 5 July\par 2004.\par \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Jonathan Woollett\f28 \uc2 \f2  (57) is a founding partner of Acoro Capital Partners LLP, an\par investment partnership focusing on unlisted financial sector investments in\par Central and Eastern Europe. He has nearly 20 years experience in the region\par with 10 years to 2008 at the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European Bank for Reconstruction and Development\f28 \uc2 \f2 \par ("\f28 \uc2 \f2 EBRD\f28 \uc2 \f2 ") in London. Prior to \f28 \uc2 \f2 EBRD\f28 \uc2 \f2  he was the director responsible for the\par establishment of asset management and mutual fund businesses in Central and\par Eastern Europe at Credit Suisse Asset Management. Prior to \f28 \uc2 \f2 Credit Suisse\f28 \uc2 \f2 , he\par worked for \f28 \uc2 \f2 UBS\f28 \uc2 \f2 , having started his banking career with \f28 \uc2 \f2 Deutsche Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  in 1979.\par He was appointed a Director of Baring Emerging Europe PLC on 23 July 2008.\par \par Ivo Coulson (51) has over 25 years of experience in the City, first with BZW as\par a director in their investment management division and then as a director with\par SG Warburg in their equity trading \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , latterly heading up their closed\par end fund team. He is currently head of portfolio management at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Stanhope Capital\par LLP\f28 \uc2 \f2 , a prominent multi family office based in the West End of London and a non\par executive director of \f28 \uc2 \f2 JPMorgan Smaller Companies Investment Trust PLC\f28 \uc2 \f2 . He was\par appointed a Director of Baring Emerging Europe PLC on 29 September 2010.\par \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Frances Daley\f28 \uc2 \f2  (56) trained as a Chartered Accountant with a predecessor firm to\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Ernst & Young\f28 \uc2 \f2  and spent 9 years in Corporate Finance followed by 18 years in\par various CFO roles. From 2007 to 2012 she was group finance director of the\par private equity backed Lifeways Group, the UK's largest provider of specialist\par support to adults with learning disabilities and mental health needs. She is\par also Chair of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Haven House Children's Hospice\f28 \uc2 \f2  and Deputy Chair of James Allen's\par Girls' School. She was appointed a Director of Baring Emerging Europe PLC on 29\par April 2014.\par \par There were no contracts or arrangements subsisting during or at the end of the\par financial year in which any Director is or was materially interested. No\par Director held a shareholding in any of the investments in the Company's\par portfolio during the year ended 30 September 2014.\par \par Substantial shareholdings\par \par At 21 November 2014, the Company had received notification of the following\par disclosable interests in the ordinary share capital of the Company:\par \par                                      Number of shares                         %\par \par City of London Investment            3,191,793 shares                    17.00%\par Management Ltd\par \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Lazard Asset Management\f28 \uc2 \f2               1,884,529 shares                    10.04%\par LLC\par \par Sarasin & Partners LLP               1,294,795 shares                     6.90%\par \par Corporate governance\par \par The statement of Corporate Governance, as shown on pages 25 to 30, is\par incorporated by cross reference into this report.\par \par Going concern\par \par The Directors believe that, having considered the Company's investment\par objectives, risk management policies, capital management policies and\par procedures, nature of the portfolio and expenditure projections, the Company\par has adequate resources and an appropriate financial structure in place to\par continue in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operational\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  existence for the foreseeable future. The assets of the\par Company consist mainly of securities which are readily realisable. For these\par reasons, they consider that there is reasonable evidence to continue to adopt\par the going concern basis in preparing the accounts.\par \par Performance against the peer group\par \par The Board monitors performance relative to a broad range of competitor funds,\par as defined by the Morningstar Emerging Europe Universe. In the year ended 30\par September 2014 the Company was ranked 37th out of 51 funds in this universe.\par Over three years to 30 September 2014 it was ranked 14th out of 49 funds and\par over five years it was ranked 20th out of 47 funds.\par \par Witholding tax\par \par The Company has sought to recover excess withholding tax from companies held in\par Poland and has engaged \f28 \uc2 \f2 KPMG LLP\f28 \uc2 \f2  for this exercise. During the year ended 30\par September 2010, an amount of \u163\'00\'A3208,000 was recovered. A further claim for\par \u163\'00\'A3242,000 was successful but that decision was subject to an appeal by the Polish tax\par authorities which was heard on 7 November 2014; whilst the appeal was rejected\par the full written justification of the judgement has not yet been received and\par this amount therefore this has not been recognised in the financial statements\par for the year ended 30 September 2014. As reported in 2011 the Company has\par engaged \f28 \uc2 \f2 KPMG LLP\f28 \uc2 \f2  to advise on the recovery of excess withholding tax on\par dividends received from companies in Russia. To date an amount of \u163\'00\'A323,000 was\par recovered in the year to 30 September 2013 and a further \u163\'00\'A320,000 was recovered\par in the year to 30 September 2014. There is potential to recover further amounts\par of withholding tax but this has not been recognised in the financial statements\par for the year ended 30 September 2014 as the amounts reclaimable are uncertain\par and have not yet been quantified.\par \par Socially responsible investment\par \par The Board has delegated the investment management function to Baring Fund\par Managers Limited. The Alternative Investment Fund Manager's primary objective\par is to produce superior financial returns to investors. It believes that over\par the long term sound social, environmental and ethical policies make good\par business sense and takes these issues into account when, in its view, they have\par a material impact on either the investment risk or the expected return from an\par investment.\par \par Global greenhouse gas emissions for the year ended 30 September 2014\par \par The Company has no greenhouse gas emissions to report from the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of\par the Company, nor does it have responsibility for any other emission producing\par sources under the Companies Act 2006 (Strategic Report and Directors' Report)\par Regulations 2013.\par \par Annual General Meeting ("AGM")\par \par THIS SECTION IS IMPORTANT AND REQUIRES YOUR IMMEDIATE ATTENTION.\par \par If you are in any doubt as to any aspect of the proposals referred to in this\par document or as to the action you should take, you should seek your own advice\par from a stockbroker, solicitor, accountant, or other professional adviser.\par \par If you have sold or otherwise transferred all of your shares, please pass this\par document together with the accompanying documents to the purchaser or\par transferee, or to the person who arranged the sale or transfer so they can pass\par these documents to the person who now holds the shares.\par \par The AGM will be held on Thursday, 15 January 2015 at 2.30pm. The formal notice\par of the AGM is set out on pages 54 to 57. Separate resolutions are proposed for\par each substantive issue. Resolutions relating to the following items of special\par business will be proposed at the AGM, for which shareholder approval is\par required in order to comply with the Companies Act 2006.\par \par Authorities to allot shares and to disapply pre-emption rights (Resolutions 11\par and 12)\par \par Approval is sought to give the Board the authority to allot ordinary shares or\par grant rights to subscribe for or convert any securities into ordinary shares up\par to an aggregate nominal amount equal to \u163\'00\'A393,865 (representing 938,650 ordinary\par shares of 10 pence each). This amount represents approximately 5% of the issued\par ordinary share capital (excluding treasury shares) of the Company as at 21\par November 2014, being the latest practicable date prior to publication of the\par notice of meeting on pages 54 to 57 (the "Notice"). As at the date of the Notice,\par 3,318,207 ordinary shares are held by the Company in treasury.\par \par The Directors do not intend to allot ordinary shares pursuant to this power\par other than to take advantage of opportunities in the market as they arise and\par only if they believe it is advantageous to the Company's existing shareholders\par to do so.\par \par Resolution 12 would, if passed, give the Board the authority to allot shares\par (or sell any shares held in treasury) for cash on a non pre-emptive basis up to\par an aggregate amount of \u163\'00\'A3110,456 This amount represents 1,104,560 shares and is\par approximately 5% of the total share capital of the Company in issue (including\par treasury shares) as at 21 November 2014, being the latest practicable date\par prior to publication of the Notice. This will enable the Company to issue new\par shares (or to sell treasury shares) to investors when the Directors consider\par that it is in the best interests of shareholders to do so. This power will not\par be utilised when it would result in any dilution of the net asset value per\par ordinary share.\par \par In respect of this amount, the Board confirm their intention to follow the\par provisions of the Pre-Emption Group's Statement of Principles regarding\par cumulative usage of authorities within a rolling three year period. The\par Principles provide that usage in excess of 7.5% of share capital should not\par take place without prior consultation with shareholders.\par \par The full text of the resolutions is set out in the Notice.\par \par If Resolutions 11 and 12 are approved, the authorities will expire at the\par conclusion of the AGM in 2016.\par \par Authority to purchase own shares (Resolution 13)\par \par At the AGM held on 14 January 2014, shareholders renewed the Director's\par authority to buyback up to 14.99% of the Company's ordinary shares. Pursuant to\par this authority, a total of 1,344,000 shares were purchased and cancelled during\par the year under review. This represented 3.14% of the issued share capital at 30\par September 2014. The prices paid for these shares ranged from 612.04p to 696.00p\par and the total cost amounted to \u163\'00\'A33,881,000. 131,000 further shares have been\par brought back since the Company's year end.\par \par The Board proposes that the Company should be given renewed authority to\par purchase ordinary shares in the market either for cancellation or to be held,\par sold, transferred or otherwise dealt with as treasury shares in accordance with\par the Companies Act.\par \par The Directors consider that the renewal of this authority is in the interests\par of shareholders as a whole as the repurchase of ordinary shares at a discount\par to their net asset value ("NAV") would enhance the NAV of the remaining\par ordinary shares. Accordingly a special resolution will be proposed at the AGM\par to authorise the Company to make market purchases of up to 14.99% of the\par ordinary shares in issue, equivalent to 2,814,079 ordinary shares as at 21\par November 2014, being the latest practicable date prior to publication of the\par Notice. Under the Listing Rules of the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Financial Conduct Authority\f28 \uc2 \f2 , this is the\par maximum percentage of its equity share capital that a company may purchase\par through the market pursuant to such authority.\par \par Purchases of shares will be made within guidelines set from time to time by the\par Board and will only be made in the market at prices below the prevailing NAV\par and, in any event, not below a minimum price of 10 pence per share.\par \par The authority for the Company to purchase its own ordinary shares will, by\par virtue of the Treasury Share Regulations 2003 and the Companies (Share Capital\par and Acquisition by a Company of its Own Shares) Regulations 2009, allow the\par Company to hold ordinary shares so purchased in treasury, as an alternative to\par immediate cancellation.\par \par Any exercise by the Company of the authority to purchase shares will occur only\par when market conditions are appropriate. Purchases will be funded either by\par using available cash resources, debt or by selling investments.\par \par This authority shall expire at the earlier of the conclusion of the AGM in 2016\par or 14 July 2016, unless such authority has been renewed prior to such time.\par \par The Board considers that all the resolutions to be put to the meeting are in\par the best interests of the Company and its shareholders as a whole. The Board\par unanimously recommends that you vote in favour of them.\par \par Conflict of interest\par \par Section 175 of the Companies Act 2006, which came in to effect on 1 October\par 2009, introduced a duty for directors to avoid unauthorised conflicts of\par interest. The Articles of Association approved by Resolution 2 at the General\par Meeting held on 15 January 2009 allows the Directors to authorise such\par conflicts and potential conflicts, where appropriate. The Board has expanded\par the terms of reference of the Audit Committee to review conflicts and potential\par conflicts and make recommendations to the Board as to whether any such\par conflicts should be authorised.\par \par Companies Act 2006 Disclosures\par \par In accordance with Section 992 of the Companies Act 2006 the Directors disclose\par the following information:\par \par the Company's capital structure is summarised on page 48, voting rights are\par summarised on page 56, and there are no restrictions on voting rights nor any\par agreement between holders of securities that result in restrictions on the\par transfer of securities or on voting rights;\par \par there exist no securities carrying special rights with regard to the control of\par the Company;\par \par details of the substantial shareholders in the Company are listed on page 21;\par \par the Company does not have an employees' share scheme;\par \par the rules concerning the appointment and replacement of Directors, amendment of\par the Articles of Association and powers to issue or buy back the Company's\par shares are contained in the Articles of Association of the Company and the\par Companies Act 2006;\par \par there exist no agreements to which the Company is party to that may affect its\par control following a takeover bid; and\par \par there exist no agreements between the Company and its Directors providing for\par compensation for loss of office that may occur because of a takeover bid.\par \par The Board recognises the requirement under Section 417(5) of the Act to detail\par information about environmental matters (including the impact of the Company's\par business on the environment), any Company employees and social and community\par issues; including information about any policies it has in relation to these\par matters and effectiveness of these policies. As the Company has no employees or\par policies in these matters this requirement does not apply. Notwithstanding, the\par Alternative Investment Fund Manager takes into account these considerations\par when making investment decisions and determines its voting instructions at\par investee company meetings accordingly.\par \par Auditor\par \par The Company's Auditor, \f28 \uc2 \f2 KPMG LLP\f28 \uc2 \f2 , has indicated its willingness to continue in\par office. Resolutions for the re-appointment of \f28 \uc2 \f2 KPMG LLP\f28 \uc2 \f2  and to authorise the\par Board to determine its remuneration will be proposed at the Annual General\par Meeting.\par \par By order of the Board\par \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 M. J. Nokes\f28 \uc2 \f2 \par \par Secretary\par \par 24 November 2014\par \par Statement of Corporate Governance\par \par Introduction\par \par The Board is accountable to the Company's shareholders for the governance of\par the Company's affairs and this statement describes how the principles of the\par 2012 UK Corporate Governance Code ("the Code") issued by \f28 \uc2 \f2 the Financial\par Reporting Council\f28 \uc2 \f2  have been applied to the affairs of the Company. In applying\par the principles of the Code, the Directors have also taken account of the Code\par of Corporate Governance published by \f28 \uc2 \f2 the Association of Investment Companies\f28 \uc2 \f2 \par ("the AIC Code"), which has established a framework of best practice\par specifically for the boards of investment trust companies. There is some\par overlap in the principles laid down by the two Codes and there are some areas\par where the AIC Code is more appropriate for investment trust companies.\par \par Applications of the Code's principles\par \par The Board is committed to high standards of corporate governance and seeks to\par observe the principles and supporting principles identified in the Code and,\par where appropriate, the principles identified in the AIC Code. It should be\par noted that, as an investment trust, most of the Company's day-to-day\par responsibilities are delegated to third parties and the Directors are all\par non-executive. Thus not all the provisions of the Code are directly applicable\par to the Company.\par \par The Board\par \par The Board currently consists of six non-executive Directors, two of whom are\par female and the other four are male and is chaired by \f28 \uc2 \f2 Steven Bates\f28 \uc2 \f2 . The\par Chairman, Miss Dixon and Professor Estrin have all served on the Board for over\par nine years and under the Code may not be considered to be independent of the\par Company and the Alternative Investment Fund Manager. The Board however, takes\par the view that independence is not necessarily compromised by length of tenure\par on the Board and experience can add significantly to the Board's strength. It\par has therefore been determined that in performing their roles as Directors, the\par Chairman, Miss Dixon and Professor Estrin remain wholly independent and all the\par Directors are considered by the Board to be independent of the Company and the\par Alternative Investment Fund Manager. Their biographies are set out on page 20.\par Collectively the Board has the requisite range of business and financial\par experience which enables it to provide clear and effective leadership and\par proper stewardship of the Company.\par \par The number of meetings of the Board, the Audit Committee and the Nomination\par Committee held during the financial year and the attendance of individual\par Directors are shown below:\par \par                                   Board     Audit Committee          Nomination\par                                                                       Committee\par \par Number of meetings                    5                   2                   1\par in the year\par \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Steven Bates\f28 \uc2 \f2                           5                   2                   1\par \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Josephine Dixon\f28 \uc2 \f2                        4                   2                   0\par \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Saul Estrin\f28 \uc2 \f2                            5                   2                   1\par \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Jonathan Woollett\f28 \uc2 \f2                      5                   2                   1\par \par Ivo Coulson                           5                   1                   1\par \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Frances Daley\f28 \uc2 \f2                          3                   1                   1\par (appointed 29 April\par 2014)\par \par All of the Directors attended the Annual General Meeting held in January 2014,\par apart from \f28 \uc2 \f2 Frances Daley\f28 \uc2 \f2  who was appointed on 29 April 2014.\par \par In addition, as part of its responsibility to monitor investments, the Board\par visited, along with the portfolio manager, a number of companies and\par authorities in Russia during September 2014.\par \par The Board deals with the Company's affairs, including the consideration of\par overall strategy, the setting and monitoring of investment policy and the\par review of investment performance. The Alternative Investment Fund Manager takes\par decisions as to asset allocation and the purchase and sale of individual\par investments. The Board papers circulated before each meeting contain full\par information on the financial condition of the Company. Key representatives of\par the Alternative Investment Fund Manager attend most of the Board meetings,\par enabling Directors to probe further or seek clarification on matters of\par concern.\par \par Matters specifically reserved for discussion by the full Board have been\par defined and a procedure adopted for the Directors to take independent\par professional advice if necessary at the Company's expense.\par \par The Chairman of the Company is a non-executive Director. A senior non-executive\par Director has not been identified as the Board is comprised entirely of\par non-executive Directors.\par \par Performance evaluation/re-election of Directors\par \par An appraisal process has been established in order to review the effectiveness\par of the Board, the Committees and individual Directors. This process involves\par the Chairman meeting with individual Directors to obtain their views on the\par performance of the Board and its Committees. In addition, the other Directors\par meet collectively once a year to evaluate the performance of the Chairman. The\par Board has also reviewed the Chairman's and Directors' other commitments and is\par satisfied that the Chairman and other Directors are capable of devoting\par sufficient time to the Company.\par \par The performance of the Company is considered in detail at each Board meeting.\par \par Board Committees\par \par The Board believes that the interests of shareholders in an investment trust\par company are best served by limiting its size so that all Directors are able to\par participate fully in all the activities of the Board. It is for this reason\par that the membership of the Audit and Nomination Committees is the same as that\par of the Board as a whole.\par \par Audit Committee\par \par The Directors have appointed an Audit Committee consisting of the whole Board,\par and is chaired by \f28 \uc2 \f2 Josephine Dixon\f28 \uc2 \f2 . The Board's view is that the members of the\par Committee, taken as a whole, have the necessary recent and relevant financial\par experience. The Audit Committee reviews audit matters within clearly-defined\par written terms of reference (copies of which are available upon request from the\par Company Secretary).\par \par In particular, the Committee shall review and challenge where necessary:\par \par the consistency of, and any changes to, accounting policies both on a year on\par year basis and across the Company;\par \par the methods used to account for significant or unusual transactions where\par different approaches are possible;\par \par whether the Company has followed appropriate accounting standards and made\par appropriate estimates and judgements, taking into account the views of the\par external auditor;\par \par the clarity of disclosure in the Company's financial reports and the context in\par which statements are made; and\par \par all material information presented with the financial statements, such as the\par Strategic Report and the Statement of Corporate Governance (insofar as it\par relates to the audit and risk management).\par \par The main significant issue that the Committee has considered is around the\par completeness, valuation and existence of quoted investments at the year ended\par 30 September 2014. The Committee is satisfied that the investments at the year\par ended 30 September 2014 exist and are correctly valued at fair value (which is\par the bid market price for listed investments and Directors' valuation for\par unquoted investments which is currently valued at nil).\par \par The Committee meets at least twice a year and is responsible for reviewing the\par annual and interim reports, the nature and scope of the external audit and the\par findings therefrom, and the terms of appointment of the Auditor, including its\par remuneration and the provision of any non-audit services. Non audit services\par provided by the Auditor mainly comprised work on the Company's taxation\par affairs. The Committee has considered the independence of the Auditor and the\par objectivity of the audit process and is satisfied that \f28 \uc2 \f2 KPMG LLP\f28 \uc2 \f2  has fulfilled\par its obligations to shareholders. The Audit Committee will meet if required with\par the Auditor to review the proposed audit programme of work and the findings of\par the Auditor. The Committee shall also use this as an opportunity to assess the\par effectiveness of the audit process. \f28 \uc2 \f2 KPMG LLP\f28 \uc2 \f2  has been the Company's Auditor for\par the last ten years and there has been no re-tendering of the Audit in that\par time. To comply with the provision in the Code the Company will review the\par option to re-tender the external audit on a regular basis.\par \par The Audit Committee regularly reviews the terms of the different service\par providers to the Company including contracts with the Alternative Investment\par Fund Manager, the Company Secretary and the Depositary and Custodian. The Audit\par Committee meets representatives of the Alternative Investment Fund Manager and\par its Compliance Officer who provides reports on the proper conduct of business\par in accordance with the regulatory environment in which both the Company and the\par Alternative Investment Fund Manager operate. The Company's external Auditor\par also attends this Committee at its request and report on its findings in\par relation to the Company's statutory audit.\par \par As the Company has no employees, section C.3.4 of the Code, which deals with\par arrangements for staff to raise concerns in confidence about possible\par improprieties in respect of financial reporting or other matters, is not\par directly relevant to it. The Audit Committee has however, confirmed with the\par Alternative Investment Fund Manager and the administrator that they do have\par "whistle blowing" policies in place for their staff.\par \par The Chairman of the Audit Committee will be present at the AGM to deal with\par questions relating to the financial statements.\par \par Nomination Committee\par \par The Nomination Committee consists of the whole Board and is chaired by the\par Chairman. The Committee meets at least annually and terms of reference are in\par place which include reviewing the Board's size, structure and diversity,\par succession planning and training. Possible new Directors are identified against\par the requirements of the Company's business and the need to have a balanced\par Board. External search consultants may be used to ensure that a wide range of\par candidates can be considered.\par \par A Director who has been appointed during the year is required under the\par provisions of the Company's Articles of Association, to retire and seek\par election by shareholders at the next Annual General Meeting. The Articles also\par require a Director who has held office at the time of the two preceding Annual\par General Meetings and who did not retire at either to seek re-election. In\par addition, a Director who has held office with the Company, other than\par employment or executive office, for a continuous period of nine years or more\par at the date of the meeting, shall retire from office and may seek re-election\par by the members. Notwithstanding the provisions of the Articles of Association,\par the Board has adopted a policy that Directors will offer themselves for annual\par re-election except where they intend to retire at an Annual General Meeting.\par \par The Board appointed one new Director, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Frances Daley\f28 \uc2 \f2 , during the year upon the\par recommendation of the Committee. This followed the appointment of a search\par agency for the purpose of finding a Director. The Committee considered an\par extensive list of candidates put forward by the search company and interviewed\par a short list of individuals for the position. A recommendation was then made to\par the Board and following acceptance by the Board as a whole, the appointment was\par confirmed.\par \par The Committee recommended to the Board, with the relevant Directors absenting\par themselves from these discussions, the nominations for re-election of the\par Chairman, Mr Coulson, Prof Estrin, and Mr Woollett for the following reasons,\par and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Frances Daley\f28 \uc2 \f2  who offers herself for election following her appointment to\par the Board on 29 April 2014:\par \par The Chairman, who was appointed a Director in 2003, has significant experience\par in both emerging and developed markets and has continued to lead the Board\par well.\par \par Mr Coulson, who was appointed a Director in 2010, has significant experience in\par the investment management industry and has been significantly involved with the\par Boards' shareholder relations.\par \par Professor Estrin, who was appointed a Director in 2004, has a significant\par knowledge about the Emerging European region and its economies.\par \par Mr Woollett, who was appointed a Director in 2008, has over 20 years experience\par in the Emerging European region with experience in both private equity and\par financial services.\par \par Ms Frances Daley, who was appointed a Director on 29 April 2014, has\par significant financial experience.\par \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Josephine Dixon\f28 \uc2 \f2  has decided to retire from the Board and will not seek\par re-election at the AGM.\par \par Remuneration\par \par The Board as a whole considers Directors' remuneration and therefore has not\par appointed a separate remuneration committee. As the Company is an investment\par trust and all Directors are non-executive, the Company is not required to\par comply with the Code in respect of executive Directors' remuneration. The\par Directors' remuneration policy and Directors' fees are detailed in the\par Directors' Remuneration report on page 33.\par \par Risk management and internal control\par \par The 2012 UK Corporate Governance Code requires the Directors, at least\par annually, to review the effectiveness of the Company's system of risk\par management and internal control and to report to shareholders that they have\par done so. This encompasses a review of all controls, which the Board has\par identified as including business, financial, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operational\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , compliance and risk\par management.\par \par The Directors are responsible for the Company's system of risk management and\par internal control which is designed to safeguard shareholders' investment and\par the Company's assets, maintain proper accounting records and ensure that\par financial information used within the business, or published, is reliable.\par However, such a system can only be designed to manage rather than eliminate the\par risk of failure to achieve business objectives and therefore can only provide\par reasonable, but not absolute, assurance against fraud, material misstatement or\par loss\par \par The Board as a whole is primarily responsible for the monitoring and review of\par risks associated with investment matters and the Audit Committee is primarily\par responsible for other risks.\par \par As the Board has contractually delegated to external parties the investment\par management, the depositary and custodial services and the day-to-day accounting\par and company secretarial requirements, the Company relies significantly upon the\par internal controls operated by those companies. Therefore the Directors have\par concluded that the Company should not establish its own internal audit\par function. The Board continues to monitor its system of internal control in\par order to ensure it operates as intended and the Directors review annually\par whether an internal audit function is required. Alternative investment fund\par management services are provided by BFM and details of the agreement with BFM\par are given in note 3 to the accounts. The Depositary and Custodian is State\par Street Bank & Trust Company Limited. Secretarial services are provided by\par Northern Trust Global Services Limited.\par \par The risk map has been considered at all regular meetings of the Board and Audit\par Committee. As part of the risk review process, regular reports are received\par from the Alternative Investment Fund Manager on all investment matters\par including compliance with the investment mandate, the performance of the\par portfolio compared with the Benchmark Index and compliance with investment\par trust status requirements.\par \par The Board also receives and reviews annual reports from the Alternative\par Investment Fund Manager and the Depositary and Custodian on their internal\par controls and their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . These reports are designed to provide details of\par the internal control procedures operated by the relevant entity and include a\par report by an independent reporting accountant.\par \par The Board confirms that appropriate procedures to review the effectiveness of\par the Company's system of internal control have been in place which cover all\par controls including financial, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operational\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and compliance controls and risk\par management. An assessment of internal control, which includes a review of the\par Company's risk map, an assessment of the quality of reports on internal control\par from the service providers and the effectiveness of the Company's reporting\par process, is carried out on an annual basis.\par \par Accountability and audit\par \par Set out on page 32 is a Statement by the Directors of their responsibilities in\par respect of the accounts.\par \par As noted earlier, an Audit Committee has been established consisting of\par independent Directors.\par \par The Board as a whole regularly reviews the terms of the management and\par secretarial contracts.\par \par The Directors who held office at the date of approval of this Directors' report\par confirm that, so far as they are each aware, there is no relevant audit\par information of which the Company's Auditor is unaware; and each Director has\par taken all the steps that they ought to have taken as Directors to make\par themselves aware of any relevant audit information and to establish that the\par Company's Auditor is aware of that information.\par \par The Directors were covered by directors' and officers' insurance that was in\par place during the financial year and at the date of this report.\par \par As permitted by the Company's Articles of Association, the Directors have the\par benefit of an indemnity which is a qualifying third party indemnity, as defined\par by Section 234 of the Companies Act 2006. The indemnities were executed on 20\par April 2011 and are currently in force.\par \par Relations with shareholders\par \par The Board regularly reviews the Alternative Investment Fund Manager's contacts\par with the Company's shareholders and monitors its shareholder profile. The Board\par supplements this with some direct contact with shareholders and is available to\par speak with any shareholder who wishes to do so. The Board supports the\par principle that the Annual General Meeting be used to communicate with private\par investors. The full Board attends the Annual General Meeting and the Chairman\par of the Board chairs the meeting. Details of the proxy votes received in respect\par of each resolution are made available to shareholders at the meeting. The\par Alternative Investment Fund Manager attends to give a presentation to the\par meeting. A quarterly newsletter is produced by the Alternative Investment Fund\par Manager and is available to shareholders.\par \par If a shareholder would like to contact the Board directly, he or she should\par write to the Chairman at 155 Bishopsgate, London EC2M 3XY and mark their letter\par private and confidential.\par \par Corporate Governance and Voting Policy\par \par The Company delegates responsibility for voting to its Alternative Investment\par Fund Manager, Baring Fund Managers Limited ("BFM"). BFM have in turn delegated\par this responsibility to \f28 \uc2 \f2 Baring Asset Management Limited\f28 \uc2 \f2  ("BAM"). The following\par is a summary of Barings statement on corporate governance and voting policy\par which has been noted by the Board. The full policy is available from the\par Barings website(\f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.barings.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.barings.com}}}\uc2 \f2 ) and is contained within the paper titled\par "Corporate engagement at Barings" dated June 2014.\par \par "Barings is charged to secure a satisfactory rate of return on capital\par entrusted to it by its clients. We do this by providing companies with their\par risk capital, buying stocks and shares which we believe will outperform the\par broader market and deliver these returns to our clients.\par \par We assess these companies and decide which to invest in through a process of\par fundamental research. As long-term investors, corporate engagement is at the\par heart of what we do. It is particularly relevant for equity investing, where we\par will develop and maintain a purposeful dialogue on strategy, performance and\par the management of risk, but it is also an integral part of the investment\par process for sub-investment grade (or "high yield") credit.\par \par In our assessment of the risk factors, before making an investment in these\par classes we will take in to account the corporate governance structure of the\par company; judging whether the structure could inhibit the delivery of good\par returns and whether the interests of the management are aligned with those of\par the investors in the company.\par \par We make use of an external agency, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Institutional Shareholder Services\f28 \uc2 \f2  (ISS)\par Voting Services to assist on our voting procedures. ISS gives recommendations\par which we assess and then we vote in accordance with what we believe to be in\par the best interests of our clients."\par \par Evaluation of performance of Alternative Investment Fund Manager\par \par Investment performance is reviewed at each regular Board meeting at which\par representatives of the Alternative Investment Fund Manager are required to\par provide answers to any questions raised by the Board. The Board conducts an\par annual formal review of the Alternative Investment Fund Manager which includes\par consideration of:\par \par performance compared with Benchmark Index and peer group;\par \par investment resources dedicated to the Company;\par \par investment management fee arrangements and notice period compared with the peer\par group; and\par \par marketing effort and resources provided to the Company.\par \par The Board believes that Baring Fund Managers Limited has served the Company\par well both in terms of investment portfolio management and general support and\par confirms the continuation of its appointment.\par \par Statement of compliance\par \par The Board considers that it has complied with all the material provisions set\par out in Section 1 of the Code throughout the year. It did not, however, comply\par with the following provisions as explained above:\par \par due to the small size of the Board and nature of the business a separate\par remuneration committee has not been established;\par \par a senior non-executive Director has not been identified;\par \par the Chairman is a member of the Audit Committee; and\par \par there is no internal audit function.\par \par By order of the Board\par \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 M. J. Nokes\f28 \uc2 \f2 \par \par Secretary\par \par 24 November 2014\par \par Audit Committee report\par \par The composition and summary terms of reference of the Audit Committee are set\par out on pages 26 and 27.\par \par The Audit Committee met in April 2014 and considered the form and content of\par the Company's half year report to 31 March 2014 which was published on 15 May\par 2014. The Committee also reviewed the key risks of the Company and the Internal\par control framework operating to control risk. The Committee also reviewed the\par terms of engagement of the audit firm and its proposed programme for the year\par end audit. The Committee met again in November 2014 and reviewed the outcome of\par the audit work and the final draft of the financial statements for the year\par ended 30 September 2014. During this review the Audit Committee met with\par representatives of both the Alternative Investment Fund Manager and the\par Administrator and sought assurances where necessary.\par \par Significant accounting matters\par \par The Audit Committee in its work consider that the key accounting issue in\par relation to the financial statements is the completeness, valuation and\par existence of quoted investments.\par \par Completeness, valuation and existence of quoted investments\par \par As part of the day to day controls of the Company there are regular\par reconciliations between the accounting records and the records kept by the\par custodian of the assets they safeguard which are owned by the Company. During\par the year and at the year end there were no matters brought to light which call\par in to question that the key controls in this area were not working, or that the\par existence of assets recorded in the books of account are not held in safe\par custody.\par \par As more fully explained in note 1 (b) on page 42 at the year ended 30 September\par 2014 the Committee agreed that the fair value of investments is the bid market\par price for quoted investments and the unquoted investment, of which there is a\par small holding which is currently valued at nil for the year ended 30 September\par 2014, is appropriate.\par \par The external Auditor attended the year end Audit Committee meeting on 14\par November 2014 and presented a report on the audit findings which did not\par include any significant issues in relation to the financial statements. During\par that meeting the Audit Committee satisfied itself that the Auditor was\par independent and also concluded to keep under review putting the audit out to\par tender. \f28 \uc2 \f2 KPMG LLP\f28 \uc2 \f2  have been the Auditor since the launch of the Company in 2002\par and during that time the audit has not been put out to tender.\par \par Contracts for non-audit services must be notified to the Audit committee who\par consider any such engagement in the light of the requirement to maintain audit\par independence. The Committee believe that all such appointments for non-audit\par work were appropriate and unlikely to influence the audit independence.\par \par During the year the value of non-audit services provided by \f28 \uc2 \f2 KPMG LLP\f28 \uc2 \f2  amounted\par to \u163\'00\'A312,000 (30 September 2013: \u163\'00\'A326,000). Whilst non-audit services as a\par proportion of audit services amount to approximately 43%, the overall quantum\par of non-audit services is not considered to be material and a significant\par proportion of the non-audit services provided relate to the following matters:\par \par the provision of tax compliance work \u163\'00\'A37,000 (30 September 2013: \u163\'00\'A37,000); and\par \par the provision of withholding tax work in Russia \u163\'00\'A35,000 (30 September 2013: \u163\'00\'A3\par 19,000).\par \par In finalising the financial statements for recommendation to the Board for\par approval the Committee has considered whether the going concern principle is\par appropriate, and concluded that it is. The Audit Committee has also satisfied\par itself that the Annual Report and financial statements taken as a whole are\par fair, balanced and understandable, and provide the information necessary for\par shareholders to assess the Company's performance, business model and strategy.\par \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Josephine Dixon\f28 \uc2 \f2 \par \par Chairman of the Audit Committee\par \par 24 November 2014\par \par Statement of Directors' responsibilities in respect of the Annual Report and\par the financial statements\par \par The Directors are responsible for preparing the Annual Report and the financial\par statements in accordance with applicable law and regulations.\par \par Company law requires the Directors to prepare financial statements for each\par financial year. Under that law they have elected to prepare the financial\par statements in accordance with UK Accounting Standards and applicable law (UK\par Generally Accepted Accounting Practice).\par \par Under company law the Directors must not approve the financial statements\par unless they are satisfied that they give a true and fair view of the state of\par affairs of the Company and of the profit or loss of the Company for that\par period. In preparing these financial statements, the Directors are required to:\par \par select suitable accounting policies and then apply them consistently;\par \par make judgments and estimates that are reasonable and prudent;\par \par state whether applicable UK Accounting Standards have been followed, subject to\par any material departures disclosed and explained in the financial statements;\par and\par \par prepare the financial statements on the going concern basis unless it is\par inappropriate to presume that the Company will continue in business.\par \par The Directors are responsible for keeping adequate accounting records that are\par sufficient to show and explain the Company's transactions and disclose with\par reasonable accuracy at any time the financial position of the Company and\par enable them to ensure that the financial statements comply with the Companies\par Act 2006. They have general responsibility for taking such steps as are\par reasonably open to them to safeguard the assets of the Company and to prevent\par and detect fraud and other irregularities.\par \par Under applicable law and regulations, the Directors are also responsible for\par preparing a Strategic report, Directors' report, Directors' Remuneration report\par and Corporate Governance statement that comply with that law and those\par regulations.\par \par The financial statements are published on the \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.bee-plc.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.bee-plc.com}}}\uc2 \f2  website, which is\par maintained by \f28 \uc2 \f2 Baring Asset Management Limited\f28 \uc2 \f2 . The maintenance and integrity of\par the website maintained by \f28 \uc2 \f2 Baring Asset Management Limited\f28 \uc2 \f2  is, so far as it\par relates to the Company, the responsibility of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Baring Asset Management Limited\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\par Legislation in the UK governing the preparation and dissemination of financial\par statements may differ from legislation in other jurisdictions.\par \par Responsibility statement of the Directors in respect of the annual report\par \par We confirm to the best of our knowledge that:\par \par a) the financial information has been prepared in accordance with applicable UK\par accounting standards, give a true and fair view of the assets, liabilities,\par financial position and profit or loss of the Company;\par \par b) the Annual Report and financial statements, to be published shortly,\par includes a fair review of the development and performance of the business and\par the position of the Company, together with a description of the principal risks\par and uncertainties that they face; and\par \par c) the Annual Report and financial statements, taken as a whole, is fair,\par balanced and understandable, and provides the information necessary for\par shareholders to assess the Company's performance, business model and strategy.\par \par For and on behalf of the Board\par \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Steven Bates\f28 \uc2 \f2 \par \par Chairman\par \par 24 November 2014\par \par Directors' Remuneration report for the year ended 30 September 2014\par \par This report is presented in accordance with Section 421 of the Companies Act\par 2006. As the Board of Directors is comprised solely of non-executive Directors,\par it is exempt under the Listing Rules from appointing a Remuneration Committee.\par The determination of the level of fees paid to Directors, which are reviewed on\par a periodic basis, is dealt with by the whole Board.\par \par The Directors' and their families' interests in the Company's shares are stated\par below (non-audited):\par \par Beneficial             21 November 2014   30 September 2014   30 September 2013\par \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Steven Bates\f28 \uc2 \f2                       3,000               3,000               3,000\par \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Josephine Dixon\f28 \uc2 \f2                    2,325               2,325               2,325\par \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Saul Estrin\f28 \uc2 \f2                        1,000               1,000               1,000\par \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Jonathan Woollett\f28 \uc2 \f2                  3,000               3,000               3,000\par \par Ivo Coulson                       2,000               2,000               3,000\par \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Frances Daley\f28 \uc2 \f2                      3,000               3,000                   -\par (appointed 29 April\par 2014)\par \par Directors' remuneration policy\par \par The Company's Articles of Association limit the aggregate fees payable to the\par Board of Directors. Subject to this overall limit, currently \u163\'00\'A3175,000, it is\par the Company's policy to determine the level of Directors' fees having regard to\par fees payable to non-executive Directors in the industry generally, the role\par that individual Directors fulfil, and the time committed to the Company's\par affairs.\par \par No Director has a service contract with the Company. A Director may be removed\par without notice and compensation will not be due on leaving office.\par \par The Company does not provide pension benefits, rights to any bonuses, share\par options or long-term incentive schemes for Directors.\par \par Directors' emoluments for the year (audited)\par \par The Directors who served during the year received the following emoluments in\par the form of fees:\par \par                                                 2014                      2013\par \par                                                 \u163\'00\'A3000                      \u163\'00\'A3000\par \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Steven Bates\f28 \uc2 \f2                                     32.2                      30.0\par \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Josephine Dixon\f28 \uc2 \f2                                  26.9                      25.0\par \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Saul Estrin\f28 \uc2 \f2                                      24.4                      22.5\par \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Jonathan Woollett\f28 \uc2 \f2                                24.4                      22.5\par \par Ivo Coulson                                     24.4                      22.5\par \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Frances Daley\f28 \uc2 \f2  (appointed                        10.6                         -\par 29 April 2014)\par \par Total                                          142.9                     122.5\par \par During the year ended 30 September 2014 up until 31 December 2013 the Chairman\par received a fee of \u163\'00\'A330,000 per annum, the Chairman of the Audit Committee\par received a fee of \u163\'00\'A325,000 per annum and other Directors \u163\'00\'A322,500 per annum.\par \par As reported last year following a review of Directors' fees it was agreed from\par 1 January 2014 to increase the annual fees to \u163\'00\'A333,000 for the Chairman, \u163\'00\'A327,500\par for the Chairman of the Audit Committee and \u163\'00\'A325,000 for other Directors. This\par represents the first increase in Directors' fees since 1 October 2007.\par \par Share price performance (not audited)\par \par The following graph compares the share price and net asset value performance\par against the Benchmark Index:\par \par [Graphic removed]\par \par Relative importance of spend on pay (audited)\par \par The following table compares the remuneration paid to the Directors with\par aggregate distributions to shareholders in the year to 30 September 2014 and\par the prior year. This disclosure is a statutory requirement, however, the\par Directors consider that comparison of Directors' remuneration with annual\par dividends does not provide a meaningful measure relative to the Company's\par overall performance as an investment trust with an objective of providing\par shareholders with long-term capital growth.\par \par                           Year ended 30       Year ended 30              Change\par                          September 2014      September 2013\par \par                                    \u163\'00\'A3000                \u163\'00\'A3000                \u163\'00\'A3000\par \par Aggregate                           143                 123                  20\par Directors'\par emoluments plus\par expenses\par \par Aggregate                         3,567               3,705               (138)\par shareholder\par distributions in\par respect of the year\par \par Voting at last Annual General Meeting\par \par At the Annual General Meeting of the Company held on 14 January 2014 a binding\par resolution was put to shareholders to approve the Directors' Remuneration\par Policy set out in the 2013 annual financial report. This resolution was passed\par on a show of hands. The proxy votes registered in respect of the binding\par resolution were:\par \par                                     For             Against            Withheld\par \par Number of proxy               9,616,914              24,369               3,889\par votes\par \par At the Annual General Meeting of the Company held on 14 January 2014 an\par advisory resolution was put to shareholders to approve the Directors'\par Remuneration report, set out in the 2013 annual financial report. This\par resolution was passed on a show of hands. The proxy votes registered in respect\par of the advisory resolution were:\par \par                                     For             Against            Withheld\par \par Number of proxy               9,570,895              68,943               5,334\par votes\par \par Approval\par \par A resolution for the approval of the Directors' Remuneration report for the\par year ended 30 September 2014 will be proposed at the Annual General Meeting.\par \par By order of the Board\par \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 M. J. Nokes\f28 \uc2 \f2 \par \par Secretary\par \par 24 November 2014\par \par Independent Auditor's report to the members of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Baring Emerging Europe PLC\f28 \uc2 \f2  only\par \par Opinions and conclusions arising from our audit\par \par Opinion on financial statements\par \par We have audited the financial statements of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Baring Emerging Europe PLC\f28 \uc2 \f2  for the\par year ended 30 September 2014 set out on pages 38 to 53. In our opinion the\par financial statements:\par \par give a true and fair view of the state of the Company's affairs as at 30\par September 2014 and of its loss for the year then ended;\par \par have been properly prepared in accordance with UK Accounting Standards; and\par \par have been prepared in accordance with the requirements of the Companies Act\par 2006.\par \par Our assessment of risks of material misstatement\par \par In arriving at our audit opinion above on the financial statements, the risk of\par material misstatement that had the greatest effect on our audit were as\par follows:\par \par Carrying amount of quoted investments (\u163\'00\'A3130.7m)\par \par Refer to page 24 (Audit Committee Report), page 42 (accounting policy) and\par pages 46 to 53 (financial disclosures)\par \par The risk - The Company's portfolio of quoted investments makes up 98.8% of the\par Company's Total Assets (by value) and is the key driver of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and\par performance results. We do not consider these investments to be at high risk of\par significant misstatement, or to be subject to a significant level of judgement\par because they comprise liquid, quoted investments. However, due to their\par materiality in the context of the financial statements as a whole, they are\par considered to be the area which had the greatest effect on our overall audit\par strategy and allocation of resources in planning and completing our audit.\par \par Our response - Our procedures over the completeness, valuation and existence of\par the Company's quoted investment portfolio included, but were not limited to:\par \par documenting and assessing the processes in place to record investment\par transactions and to value the portfolio;\par \par agreeing the valuation of 100 per cent of investments in the portfolio to\par externally quoted prices; and\par \par agreeing 100 per cent of investment holdings in the portfolio to independently\par received third party confirmations.\par \par Our application of materiality and an overview of the scope of our audit\par \par The materiality for the financial statements as a whole was set at \u163\'00\'A32.65m. This\par has been determined with reference to a benchmark of Total Assets (of which it\par represents 2%). Total Assets, which is primarily composed of the Company's\par investment portfolio, is the key driver of the Company's capital and revenue\par performance and, as such, we consider it to be one of the principal\par considerations for members of the Company in assessing its financial\par performance.\par \par We agreed with the Audit Committee to report to it corrected and uncorrected\par misstatements we identified through our audit with a value in excess of \u163\'00\'A3\par 132,000, in addition to other audit misstatements below that threshold that we\par believe warranted reporting on qualitative grounds.\par \par Our audit of the Company was undertaken to the materiality level specified\par above and was all performed at the administrator's head office in London.\par \par Our opinion on other matters prescribed by the Companies Act 2006 is unmodified\par \par In our opinion:\par \par the part of the Directors' Remuneration report to be audited has been properly\par prepared in accordance with the Companies Act 2006; and\par \par the information given in the Strategic report and Directors' report for the\par financial year for which the financial statements are prepared is consistent\par with the financial statements.\par \par We have nothing to report in respect of the matters on which we are required to\par report by exception\par \par Under ISAs (UK and Ireland) we are required to report to you if, based on the\par knowledge we acquired during our audit, we have identified other information in\par the Annual Report that contains a material inconsistency with either that\par knowledge or the financial statements, a material misstatement of fact, or that\par is otherwise misleading.\par \par In particular, we are required to report to you if:\par \par we have identified material inconsistencies between the knowledge we acquired\par during our audit and the Directors' statement that they consider that the\par Annual Report and financial statements taken as a whole is fair, balanced and\par understandable and provides the information necessary for shareholders to\par assess the Company's performance, business model and strategy; or\par \par the Audit Committee report does not appropriately address matters communicated\par by us to the Audit Committee.\par \par Under the Companies Act 2006 we are required to report to you if, in our\par opinion:\par \par adequate accounting records have not been kept by the Company, or returns\par adequate for our audit have not been received from branches not visited by us;\par or\par \par the financial statements and the part of the Directors' Remuneration report to\par be audited are not in agreement with the accounting records and returns; or\par \par certain disclosures of Directors' remuneration specified by law are not made;\par or\par \par we have not received all the information and explanations we require for our\par audit.\par \par Under the Listing Rules we are required to review:\par \par the Directors' statement, set out on page 21, in relation to going concern; and\par \par the part of the Corporate Governance Statement on pages 24 to 29 relating to\par the Company's compliance with the nine provisions of the 2012 UK Corporate\par Governance Code specified for our review.\par \par We have nothing to report in respect of the above responsibilities.\par \par Scope of report and responsibilities\par \par As explained more fully in the Directors' Responsibilities Statement set out on\par page 32, the Directors are responsible for the preparation of the financial\par statements and for being satisfied that they give a true and fair view. A\par description of the scope of an audit of financial statements is provided on \f28 \uc2 \f2 the\par Financial Reporting Council\f28 \uc2 \f2 's website at \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.frc.org.uk/auditscopeukprivate"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.frc.org.uk/auditscopeukprivate}}}\uc2 \f2 .\par This report is made solely to the Company's members as a body and subject to\par important explanations and disclaimers regarding our responsibilities,\par published on our website at \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.kpmg.com/uk/auditscopeukco2014a"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.kpmg.com/uk/auditscopeukco2014a}}}\uc2 \f2 , which