\par\par \b \uc2 US Hits Russia's Biggest Bank, Others with Sanctions -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,327 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 September 2014\par 16:24\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 10:24 EDT - \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  announces new sanctions against \uc2 Sberbank\uc2  of Russia, the country's largest bank, five defense technology firms and five companies engaged in exploration or production for Russian deepwater, Arctic offshore and shale projects. The latest steps by the U.S. and EU ratchet up pressure on Russia amid a ceasefire in \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "Given Russia's direct military \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and blatant efforts to destabilize \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2 , we have deepened our sanctions against Russia today," says Treasury Secretary \uc2 Jacob Lew\uc2 . But he also notes the latest penalties could be suspended if there's a lasting settlement to the conflict. (jeffrey.sparshott@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 10:23 EDT - The most powerful person in Peru isn't the president, an opposition politician, or even a banking or mining executive. It is First Lady Nadine Heredia, according to a new poll by Datum. The national poll of 1,209 people showed Heredia beating out her husband, President Ollanta Humala, when asked who has the most power in Peru. Heredia's influence in the government is well-known, but past polls have always shown that the president was considered holding more power. Heredia, currently the head of the ruling Nationalist party, is also seen as more influential than past president Alan Garcia, opposition leader Keiko Fujimori, and banking executive Dionisio Romero. (ryan.dube@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 10:08 EDT - Companies cautiously restocked in July amid a decent pickup in sales. Business inventories rose a seasonally adjusted 0.4% from June, while sales climbed 0.8%, the Commerce Department said. The ratio of firms' inventory to sales held steady and was slightly down from a year earlier. While inventories, relative to sales, haven't budged since February, they're generally running higher this year than earlier in the recession, a possible sign firms are gaining confidence in the strength of the recovery. (joshua.mitchell@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 09:51 EDT - A deluge of corporate-bond sales so far this month has weakened bond prices, but analysts at \uc2 Barclays\uc2  say it has "not taken us low enough in the range to believe it is a market-wide buying opportunity." Investment-grade US bonds have returned negative 1.27% for the month, while high-yield bonds have returned a negative 1.02%. Still, the analysts say the downward move over the summer offers a better buying opportunity. \uc2 Barclays\uc2  says they are not "overly negative on the asset class, but we are less inclined to position for a move that is too small to be considered a true dip." (mike.cherney@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 09:32 EDT - \uc2 Chile's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  reduced its monetary policy rate to 3.25% late Thursday, while the \uc2 Central Reserve Bank of Peru\uc2  chopped its key lending rate to 3.50%, and more cuts may be in store, BBVA Research says. Chile's policy decision will depend on "short-term inflation figures; nonetheless we consider that further stimulus is likely to be necessary for the rest of the year." In Peru, "the tone of the statement accompanying yesterday's decision suggests that we cannot rule out a possible further cut in the policy rate before year-end," the bank says. (robert.kozak@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 9:28 EDT - \uc2 Bank of America Merrill Lynch\uc2  now sees the \uc2 Fed\uc2  making its first rate increase in June 2015, up from its prior forecast for September 2015. The question now is when the \uc2 Fed\uc2  will change its rhetoric enough to scare markets. Will it come in next Wednesday's FOMC statement? It's a close call, \uc2 BofAML\uc2  says. "They will likely try to change the statement in a market neutral fashion, dropping the reference to 'considerable time'." Firm also expects the \uc2 Fed\uc2 's rate-forecast "dots" to shift slightly higher. (cynthia.lin@wsj.com; @cynthialin_dj)\par \par \uc2 09:22 EDT - Selling momentum sends the benchmark 10-year yield briefly above 2.6% for the first time in more than a month. The yield touches 2.601%, highest intraday level since the end of July, according to CQG. The bond market has sold off all week long amid concerns about the FOMC policy statement next week and if it'll signal a sooner rate hike. The 10-year Treasury note last down 18/32, yielding 2.598%. Still, the yield remains below 3%, where it started the year. (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj)\par \par \uc2 1259 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ]--Eurozone government bond issuance is scheduled from four countries next week. Slovakia taps the November 2018 and February 2023 bonds Tuesday with open volume, as usual. Germany follows Wednesday with the EUR4 billion reopening of the September 2016 Schatz. Spain launches a new three-year bond Thursday, maturing in October 2017, and on the same day France conducts its usual mid-month double feature. At its first auction, France offers EUR7-EUR8 billion November 2016, May 2019 and November 2019 conventional bonds, known as OATs. At the second auction it offers EUR1-EUR1.5 billion July 2018 and July 2024 OATei bonds [indexed to eurozone inflation] and July 2021 OATi [indexed to French inflation]. (emese.bartha@wsj.com; @EmeseBartha) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 8:58 EDT - Some economists are asking why \f28 \uc2 \f2 Colombia's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  continues to buy dollars to weaken the peso when the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has already fallen 7% in seven weeks, to 1981/USD? "The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  should soon reassess their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy," Jefferies says in a research note. Bank officials have their reasons; an even weaker peso would further help Colombian exporters, and keeping up with \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will build \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves against shocks. But Jefferies says "this strategy could be self-defeating if it feeds into a vicious cycle of de-leveraging and then need for more USD as well as the pressure on the inflation target." (dan.molinski@wsj.com @dmwsj)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 8:38 EDT - Bond prices hit fresh session lows after mixed retail sales. The headline number showed 0.6% growth, a tad below 0.7% forecast by economists, though excluding automotive purchases, sales rose 0.3% from July, vs 0.2% forecast. The key focus is the FOMC meeting next week and any changes in the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fed\f28 \uc2 \f2 's language regarding how long it will hold rates near zero could send bond yields higher still. The 10-year Treasury note was 15/32 lower, yielding 2.589%. (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1237 GMT The Spanish Treasury launches a long overdue three-year government bond at its next auction on Sept. 18, with maturity in October 2017 and a coupon, or annual fixed interest, of 0.50%. Analysts have been eyeing this bond for a while, given the relatively high outstanding volume of the previous three-year bond, the 2.10% April 2017. National treasuries and debt agencies launch new bonds in different sectors of the yield curve on a regular basis, which helps them keep their redemption profile smooth in order to avoid distortions in terms of pricing and future refinancing needs. (emese.bartha@wsj.com; @EmeseBartha) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 8:26 EDT - After a brief pause Thursday, Treasurys pull back again, with the 10-year yield trading at highest level in more than a month. Worries over next week's FOMC, higher yields in euro zone and new debt supply have sparked selling in Treasurys this week, a setback from the roaring market in 2014. US retail sales are due at 8:30 a.m. and a strong report likely adds more selling to the market over jitters that \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fed\f28 \uc2 \f2  could raise rates sooner than expected. The 10-year Treasury note was 12/32 lower, yielding 2.578%. (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 September 12, 2014 10:24 ET (14:24 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140912ea9c0028p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
