\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. Dollar Slips on Unlikely  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  in  \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine \highlight\uc2  -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,589 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 August 2014\par 07:49\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0549 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The U.S. dollar is retreating in Asia after starting Friday with a well-bid tone due to Ukrainian allegations that Russia has sent its troops across the border. Demand for safe-haven assets such as the greenback has subsided as U.S. president Obama reassured markets that a military confrontation between the U.S. and Russia is "not in the cards". While the possibility of additional economic sanctions against Russia still looms large, traders are slipping back into the complacent thinking that Asia is well-buffered against geopolitical tensions in the western hemisphere. One of the barometers of investor mood in the Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market, the USD/KRW, is down versus its Thursday close, implying a stronger South Korean won. Other USD/Asia \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  pairs are off their Friday highs. (ewen.chew@wsj.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0547 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] The NZX-50 closes 0.3% lower at 5223.31 dragged down by weakness in a number of large cap stocks, says \uc2 Craigs Investment Partners\uc2  broker Bryon Burke. Auckland Airport (AIA.NZ) ended 2.9% lower at NZ$3.67, Fletcher Building was down 1.0% at NZ$9.20 and \uc2 Air New Zealand\uc2  0.9% lower at NZ$2.195. Burke adds that market positioning due to it being the last trading day of the month had increased liquidity ahead of the close. Vector (VCT.NZ) ended up 1.5% at NZ$2.68 after news it had won a high court case against the NZ tax department, which will see the company receive a tax refund of NZ$7.6 million. (lucy.craymer@wsj.com;\uc2 Twitter\uc2 : @lucy_craymer)\par \par \uc2 0547 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] The USD/JPY's highest end of the trade range will likely ratchet up over the mid-term through July to September 2015, giving a good chance for investors to buy the greenback on dips at the time of geopolitical uncertainty, says \uc2 Mizuho Bank\uc2  chief market economist Daisuke Karakama. "Clear difference in the U.S.-Japan monetary policy is a reason to support yen selling," says Karakama, adding that whether the USD/JPY will be firmly fixed around over 110 in the year 2015 depends on how certain the \uc2 Fed\uc2  will be on track toward future rate hike. In a mid-term outlook, the USD/JPY tipped in 101-108 range in Oct-Dec; 103-110 in January-March 2015; 103-111 in April-June and 104-112 in July-September. (hiroyuki.kachi@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0543 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  says it has modestly lowered its oil price forecasts for 2014 as geopolitical risk premium in oil has deflated prematurely, "but geopolitical tension has not disappeared and remains an upside risk for oil prices." It expects WTI to average $99 a barrel in 2014, down by $3 and Brent to average $108 a barrel, down by $2. It says "with limited strength in fundamentals to put prices on a sustained upward trend, the oil market is faced once again with a range-bound future where any bounces in volatility are opportunities to sell into." \uc2 Nymex\uc2  crude is up 29 cents at $94.84/bbl, Brent crude is up 33 cents at $102.79/bbl. (eric.yep@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 Thailand's Manufacturing Activity Slips in July--Market Talk item at 0508 GMT, misstated manufacturing activity contracted 6.6% on-year in June. The revised figure for June is 6.3%.\par \par \uc2 0525 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Korea's July data packet hints at an economic recovery beginning to build prior to \uc2 the Bank of Korea\uc2 's rate cut in August, putting the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on track to maintain a dovish stance and hold rates at 2.25% until Q3 of 2015, says \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2 . "Indicators of consumption continued to improve as those for construction were mixed," the bank says. "July IP [industrial production] is in line with our view that improvements in domestic activities will continue, despite at a moderate pace." Industrial production rose at 1.1% in July from the preceding month, and up 3.4% from the same month last year, according to Statistics Korea, sharply beating consensus estimates. "While some soft patches in data may reappear in 2H2014, we expect KRW depreciation, the recent rate cut by the BOK, and government policy stimulus to sustain recovery momentum." The KOSPI is down 0.3% at 2,070.32. (gregor.hunter@wsj.com; \uc2 Twitter\uc2 : @gregorhunter)\par \par \uc2 0513 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  increases its forecast for Thailand 2015 GDP to 4.5% from 3.9% because of recovery in tourism and better external demand. Tourism is expected to grow between 15% and 20% on the back of political stability. Tourism growth should add 1.5% to 2% to headline GDP growth, economist Santitharn Sathirathai says. Exports should rebound to around 5% growth year over year in 2015 compared with 3% this year. The disappointment was largely because of temporary disruptions rather than a general loss of competitiveness, he says. However, private consumption is expected to grow below 3% owing to fiscal-policy drags and weak commodity prices and on top of a high debt burden especially for lower-income households. (warangkana.chomchuen@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0511 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] The Philippine peso currently trades at 43.67 versus the U.S. dollar, recovering from Thursday's dip which came despite better than expected second-quarter gross domestic product data. The daily USD/PHP chart continues to indicate consolidation for the peso within a range of 43.56-43.76 demarcated by the Ichimoku Cloud support zone. If USD/PHP ends Friday above 43.76, it could spur technically-motivated traders to reduce short-USD positions. On the other hand, a close below 43.56 would engage the daily Bollinger downtrend channel that would suggest more U.S. dollar weakness in the near term. Data on Thursday showed Philippine GDP rose 6.4% on a yearly basis, better than the median estimate for 6.3% growth. USD/PHP is now 43.67 versus its Thursday close of 43.71. (ewen.chew@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0508 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Thailand's industrial-production continues to slip with the latest official data showing a dip of 5.2% in July from a year ago, due to lower auto production, according to the Office of Industrial Economics at the Industry Ministry. Manufacturing activity contracted 6.6% on-year in June. The latest decline, for the 16th consecutive month, is in line with economists' expectations. "Export growth remained weak and domestic demand recovery remained on a gradual pacing," says Nalin Chutchotitham, an economist at \uc2 HSBC\uc2  in Bangkok. (nopparat.chaichalearmmongkol@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0451 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] The NZD/USD is trading slightly lower after ANZ Business Confidence was soft adding to concerns that the economic growth in New Zealand has lost some of its "gloss," says \uc2 HiFX\uc2  Senior Trader \uc2 Michael Johnston\uc2 . He says that the NZD/USD continues to be sold on any rallies higher and he expects this to continue for the year ahead. The pair is at 0.8369 vs. 0.8384 earlier this morning. Johnston puts short term support for the pair at 0.8320 with resistance coming in at 0.8405. (lucy.craymer@wsj.com;\uc2 Twitter\uc2 : @lucy_craymer)\par \par \uc2 0449 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Shares of \uc2 Pioneer\uc2  (6773.TO) are down 0.6% at Y326 while Sharp (6753.TO) rises 1.2% at Y329 following the pair's announcement of the dissolution of their mutual capital alliance. \uc2 Sharp\uc2  holds 30 million \uc2 Pioneer\uc2  shares, while \uc2 Pioneer\uc2  holds 10 million Sharp shares; the firms have not decided the timing and method for selling their shareholdings. Their JV (\uc2 Pioneer\uc2  owns 66% to Sharp's 34%), an optical disc drive (ODD) business for Blu Ray recorders and PC drives will continue, the companies said. The pact, which began in 2007, was aimed at joint development in the fields of ODD, networking, car electronics, and displays, but implied at the time that Sharp was rescuing \uc2 Pioneer\uc2 , notes \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  analyst Yasuo Nakane. "The sale will be very beneficial for Sharp in terms of the difference between the current price and book price of the shares (\uc2 Sharp\uc2 's price-to-book ratio is 2.86 times). However, we see the need for measures to firm-up the ODD business over the longer term as demand has continued to decline."\par \par \uc2 (bradford.frischkorn@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0440 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Japan's industry ministry plans to boost spending on renewable energy in the next fiscal year to start in April 2015. It has requested $1.5 billion, up 16% on year, from the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , says Toshimitsu Motegi, Minister of Economy Trade and Industry. In its latest long-term energy plan, which took effect in April, Prime Minister Shinzo Abe's administration promises to maximize the adoption of renewable energy and minimize the use of nuclear power. "We made the budget request aiming to materialize these targets," Motegi says. Among the outlays of the $1.5 billion total, 29% will be used to subsidize the high electricity prices set for renewable sources, 7% for enhancing the power grid to accept more renewable-sourced electricity and 5% for research and development of offshore wind technologies. The budget will be finalized by the end of this year. (mari.iwata@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 August 29, 2014 01:49 ET (05:49 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140829ea8t000jw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
