\par\par \b \uc2 RPT-GLOBAL MARKETS-Ukraine, Russia tensions rock stocks, buoy core bonds\b0\par\par\uc2 807 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 August 2014\par 13:45\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2014 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (Repeats to additional subscribers)\par     * Euro stocks tumble, Bund yields hit new lows\par     * Moscow markets see biggest fall in a month\par     * Wall Street expected to open lower, heavy list of data due\par     * Precious metals gain as risk assets lose out\par \par     By Marc Jones\par     LONDON, Aug 28 (Reuters) - European shares took a sharp\par tumble on Thursday as accusations by Ukraine that Russia had\par moved troops across the border brought a three-day global rally\par in risk assets to a shuddering halt. \par     Ukrainian President Petro Poroshenko said Russian forces had\par entered Ukraine, and he convened his security and defence\par council to decide how to respond. \par     The tensions put riskier assets firmly under pressure, with\par Moscow stocks slumping and the top share index in Germany\par  -- whose corporate sector has strong trade ties with\par Russia -- dropping more than 1.3 percent after other sources\par also reported Russian troops had crossed the border to fight\par alongside separatists.  \par     Safe-haven investments were in demand, with yields on \f28 \uc2 \f2 German\par government\f28 \uc2 \f2  bonds, the traditional go-to asset for\par risk-wary European investors, hitting all-time lows as\par the yen and Swiss franc rose. \par     The bruised euro also clung to modest gains as bets\par were laid on possible fresh ECB stimulus next week aside amid\par the fast-moving developments in Ukraine and as German CPI data\par suggested deflation remains unlikely in the bloc for now.\par     Ahead of the Germany-wide August inflation number due at\par 1200 GMT, there were solid readings from three of its biggest\par regions. Similar Spanish figures also saw a slightly\par smaller-than-forecast drop as revised second quarter GDP numbers\par held up too.  \par     Talk of fresh ECB policy easing erupted last week following\par a strongly-worded speech from the bank's President. But sources\par at the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  told Reuters on Wednesday that new action\par was unlikely next week unless Friday's inflation numbers showed\par the bloc clearly heading for deflation. \par     "Germany is roughly 30 percent of the euro zone so if that\par (inflation rate) stays stable, it limits the downside for the\par overall region," said \f28 \uc2 \f2 Berenberg Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  economist Christian\par Schultz. "It seems to me the weaker euro is helping."\par     \par     RUSSIAN CHILL\par     European stocks had started the day quietly\par following a subdued showing in Asia but by\par mid-session, markets in London, Frankfurt and\par Paris were nursing losses of 0.5, 1.4 and 0.8 percent.\par     Futures markets pointed to Wall Street opening down 0.4\par percent when U.S. trading restarts. For economists there is\par plenty of U.S. data slated, including the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fed\f28 \uc2 \f2 's favoured measure\par of inflation, revised second quarter GDP and unemployment\par claims. \par     The news out of Ukraine dealt a blow to hopes of an easing\par of the conflict after this week's meeting between Poroshenko and\par his Russian counterpart \f28 \uc2 \f2 Vladimir Putin\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\par     Russia denies \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ukraine\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by arming the rebels or\par sending soldiers across the border.\par     Ukrainian Prime Minister Arseny Yatseniuk appealed on\par Thursday to the U.S. Europe and other \f28 \uc2 \f2 G7\f28 \uc2 \f2  countries "to freeze\par Russian assets and finances until Russia withdraws armed forces,\par equipment and agents". \par     Dollar-traded stocks in Moscow tumbled 3.3 percent\par and the rouble dropped 1.4 percent as they saw their\par biggest falls in a month, while Ukraine's credit default swaps\par surged to three-month highs and its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and dollar bonds\par fell. \par     "Recent optimism around a negotiated solution to the crisis\par as reflected in market pricing appears overdone in our view," \f28 \uc2 \f2 JP\par Morgan\f28 \uc2 \f2  analysts wrote in a note to clients.\par   \par         \par     INFLATION EXPECTATIONS\par     Away from the political tensions, the Australian dollar was\par in demand after second quarter business investment data beat\par forecasts, while the U.S. dollar all but sat on recent\par gains.\par     U.S. Treasuries had rallied overnight as month-end buying\par helped send 30-year yields to their lowest in over a year. \par Core euro zone government bond yields continued to plough record\par lows as markets waited on the ECB's next move, though the drop\par in risk appetite left periphery bonds out of favour for once.\par     "For the euro area, inflation may now be 0.3 or 0.4 percent\par but I don't think this one figure is of major importance," said\par Piet Lammens, a strategist at KBC in Brussels.\par     "What is important is that (ECB head Mario) Draghi said (in\par a recent speech) that during August inflation expectations have\par dropped substantially."\par     Among commodities, Brent crude oil dipped towards $102 a\par barrel after choppy trading overnight following a report showing\par declining U.S. gasoline demand. \par     Aluminium -- of which Russia is a large producer -- pushed\par higher as traders mulled possible supply issues while\par precious metals and safe havens gold, silver and\par  platinum all made ground. \par \par  (Additional reporting by Sujata Rao and Marius Zaharia in\par London, editing by John Stonestreet)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Released: 2014-8-28T12:45:54.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020140828ea8s006cs\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
