\par\par \b \uc2 CEE Morning Briefing: Polish July CPI Seen Swinging into Deflation\b0\par\par\uc2 By Leos Rousek   \par \uc2 724 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 August 2014\par 04:14\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 TODAY'S CALENDAR \par  \par Local/GMT \par  0900/0700 Czech July grain harvest estimates \par  1000/0700 Czech June current account balance \par  1400/1200 Poland June Current Account \par  1400/1200 Poland July CPI \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Poland is likely to report its first-ever deflation reading, a period of declining prices, driven by a fall in the cost of food, apparel and fuel.\f28 \par \par \uc2 Analysts forecast the July consumer price index to contract 0.3% against the same month in 2013, reversing its 0.3% annual increase in June. This will bolster expectations the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to ease its monetary policy in the following months. The bank has kept its headline interest rate on hold at 2.50% since July 2013.\par \par \uc2 The Czech and Polish central banks are also going to release current account balance data for June. Both readings are seen to have swung into deficits, likely because of the impact of the \uc2 European Union\uc2 's sanctions on exports of some manufactured goods to Russia for the Moscow's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Analysts expect the Polish current account balance deficit of 425 million euros ($567 million), compared with a EUR280 million surplus in May. In the Czech Republic they see the gap at 7.87 billion koruna ($377 million) against the CZK9.00 billion surplus a month earlier.\par \par \uc2 Also in Prague, the Czech statistics bureau is to release its latest grain harvest estimate for the 2014 crop season. The reading, based on data collected from local farmers in July, may indicate a likely surplus of flour and baked products on the Czech market. Last week, Russia retaliated against western sanctions by banning imports of some food products from the EU for one year, creating a glut on grocery markets in the 28-nation bloc. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par  \par EUR/CZK \par Latest 0250 GMT 27.849-80 \par Previous close 27.847-76 \par %Chg   +0.01 \par  \par EUR/HUF \par Latest 0250 GMT 313.50-4.16 \par Previous close 313.44-97 \par % Chg  +0.04 \par  \par EUR/PLN \par Latest 0250 GMT 4.1984-2048 \par Previous 2150 close 4.1979-2038 \par % Chg  +0.02 \par  \par FIXED INCOME \par  \par Hungary \par          Tue    Mon \par 3 yrs   3.59%  3.60% \par 5 yrs   3.96%  3.96% \par 10yrs   4.97%  4.98% \par  \par Poland \par          Tue    Mon \par 3 yrs   2.33%  2.29% \par 5 yrs   2.87%  2.79% \par 10yrs   3.37%  3.31% \par  \par Czech Republic \par          Tue    Mon \par 3 yrs   0.18%  0.19% \par 5 yrs   0.55%  0.55% \par 10yrs   1.43%  1.43% \par  \par  \par STOCKS \par  \par WIG 20 \par 2,349.48-2.50-0.11% \par  \par BUX \par 17,082.22-191.78-1.11% \par  \par PX \par 957.63-7.65-0.79% \par  \par  \par OTHER NEWS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 HUNGARY: Citi economist Eszter Gargyan says the rebound in Hungary's July CPI is unlikely to change the loose monetary policy stance for now. Headline inflation was +0.1% on the year, slightly above the forecast, mainly reflecting a reversal of soft food prices in May-June.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 UKRAINE: For most of this year's smoldering conflict between Russia and Ukraine, investors haven't been too concerned about economic fallout in the rest of Eastern Europe. Countries like Poland, Hungary and the Czech Republic have big trade (especially energy) ties to Russia, but a short-lived spat wasn't expected to upset these markets amid a broader European recovery. But as sanctions against Russia ramp up, some analysts see growing impact. Including the Russian ruble, five of the six worst performing \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  against the dollar in 3Q are in Eastern Europe. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Morgan Stanley\f28 \uc2 \f2  sees risk of further losses, particularly among bonds of countries like Romania and Hungary, which were standout performers in EM the last few years.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 BULGARIA: Bulgaria's state prosecutor on Tuesday charged the lead shareholder in the country's fourth-largest lender with embezzlement and issued an international warrant for his arrest, the latest step in the country's efforts to resolve its worst banking crisis in nearly 20 years.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Patryk Wasilewski in Warsaw contributed to this article.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Write to Leos Rousek at leos.rousek@wsj.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Go to \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://blogs.wsj.com/emergingeurope"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://blogs.wsj.com/emergingeurope}}}\uc2 \f2  for the new WSJ and Dow Jones blog on Central and Eastern Europe, covering business, politics, society and more, written by our correspondents across the region.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 August 12, 2014 22:14 ET (02:14 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140813ea8d0007t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
