\par\par \b \uc2 CEE MARKETS 2- \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  fall on concerns over Ukraine, low rates\b0\par\par\uc2 1,150 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 August 2014\par 15:48\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2014 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (Adds new comments and prices, Russian food ban on Romania)\par     By Sandor Peto\par     BUDAPEST, Aug 6 (Reuters) - Hungary's forint and the Czech\par crown hit multi-year lows against the euro on Wednesday as\par Central European assets fell on concerns that Ukraine's crisis\par could deepen and that yields in the region may be too low.\par     "The market is very, very nervous, we have not seen anything\par like that since January this year," a fixed income trader said\par as Hungarian bond yields rose by about 15 basis points.\par     Polish Prime Minister \f28 \uc2 \f2 Donald Tusk\f28 \uc2 \f2  said the threat of a\par direct \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by Russia's military in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ukraine\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has risen\par over the last couple of days.  \par     Hungarian bonds were also depressed because some investors\par probably closed positions after changes in the short-term\par investment instruments offered by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , thinking\par that a 5-month rally had pushed yields too low.\par     The bank stopped issuing two-week bills as of Aug. 1 and now\par allows local banks to deposit funds at the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  for two\par weeks, and earn interest, instead. The change prompted foreign\par banks who held two-week bills to shift the money into government\par debt because they have no access to the deposit accounts.  \par     Central European assets have taken a beating in the past\par week on rising expectations that the U.S. Federal Reserve may be\par ready to raise interest rates earlier than expected, making\par emerging market assets less attractive.\par     Volatility jumped in the region's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets after\par months of range-bound trading and falling government debt\par yields.\par     The forint touched its weakest levels against the\par euro since January 2012, at 317.30. By 1211 GMT though it had\par rebounded to 315.90, down 0.1 percent from Tuesday.\par     "Record lows are only 8 forints off... I would not bet on a\par move there yet but in this market I don't dare to make plans for\par more than 1-2 days," one Budapest-based dealer said.\par     The forint and the crown regained some ground \par early in the session helped by stronger-than-expected industrial\par output data for Hungary and the Czech Republic for June.\par     But later the crown fell beyond 28 against the euro, to its\par weakest level since March 2009, before rebounding to 27.856,\par still weaker by 0.2 percent from Tuesday.\par     "Markets are being driven by geopolitical news flow and by\par speculation on the U.S. Fed monetary policy outlook," said\par Generali PPF Asset Management chief analyst Radomir Jac.\par     The zloty hit its weakest level since April versus\par the euro and was half a percent weaker from Tuesday, at 4.204.\par The region's main equities indices declined, led by 1.7 percent\par fall in Budapest. \par     The shares of Hungary's \f28 \uc2 \f2 Magyar Telekom\f28 \uc2 \f2  plunged 3.5\par percent, tracking a fall in its parent company, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Deutsche Telekom\f28 \uc2 \f2 \par  after \f28 \uc2 \f2 Sprint\f28 \uc2 \f2  reportedly dropped its bid to\par acquire the German company's \f28 \uc2 \f2 T-Mobile U.S. Inc\f28 \uc2 \f2  unit.\par     The forint could start to face fresh pressure as investors\par assess the impact of a new law on banks, which forces them to\par pay refunds on the grounds that they have overcharged borrowers,\par Budapest-based brokerage Equilor said in a note.\par     The \f28 \uc2 \f2 European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  warned in a legal opinion that the\par law and a planned conversion of foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  loans into\par forints later this year could hurt financial stability in\par Hungary. \par     \par     DEBT SELLOFF\par     The yield on Hungary's 3-year government bonds rose 18 basis\par points, to retreat 5 basis points later to 3.8 percent.\par     "The ECB opinion, Ukraine and everything else is there, too,\par but the main factor is the selloff in the government debt\par market," a Budapest-based \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  dealer said. \par     The Czech crown had largely held around 27.400 to the euro\par in recent months but the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's indications last month\par that it favoured keeping the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  weak into 2016 to maintain\par relaxed monetary policy has helped it weaken along with other\par emerging market assets since last week.\par     \f28 \uc2 \f2 Romania's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  cut interest rates on Monday,\par surprising some investors, and rate cut expectations have also\par strengthened in Poland in recent months after economic data\par suggested economic recovery is slowing.\par     New \f28 \uc2 \f2 European Union\f28 \uc2 \f2  sanctions on Russia last month have \par increased that risk and Moscow has also imposed a ban on imports\par from Poland, a staunch supporter of tougher EU measures against\par Russia. It extended the ban to Romania on Wednesday.\par     The move is largely symbolic as Russia accounts for only 3\par percent of Romania's exports, and the head of the association of\par cattle farmers said Romania did not export a gramme of meat to\par Russia in the past decade.  \par     The leu eased 0.1 percent to 4.443.  \par  *                                                            *\par  **************** CEE MARKETS SNAPSHOT AT 1411 CET ************ \par  ************************* \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  *************************\par                             Latest  Previous   Daily   Change  \par                             bid     close      change  in 2014 \par  Czech crown                 27.856    27.794   -0.22%  -1.88% \par  Hungary forint             315.900   315.600   -0.09%  -5.99% \par  Polish zloty                 4.204     4.185   -0.46%  -1.29% \par  Romanian leu                 4.443     4.437   -0.13%  +0.39% \par  Croatian kuna                7.647     7.644   -0.04%  -0.38% \par  Serbian dinar              116.720   116.780   +0.05%  -1.90% \par  Note: daily change calculated from previous close at 1800 CET \par  **************************** STOCKS **************************\par                             Latest   Previous  Daily   Change  \par                                      close     change  in 2014 \par  Prague                       948.69   953.80   -0.38%  -3.87% \par  Budapest                   17348.39 17651.90   -1.72%  -5.38% \par  Warsaw                      2350.17  2369.33   -0.81%  -2.25% \par  Bucharest                   6960.29  6980.25   -0.29%  +7.45% \par  Ljubljana                    798.34   801.67   -0.42% +24.55% \par  Zagreb                      1804.33  1811.23   -0.38%  +1.35% \par  Belgrade                     596.34   601.89   -0.92%  +8.11% \par  Sofia                        540.78   542.14   -0.25% +10.05% \par  ***************************** BONDS **************************\par                         Yield    Yield    Spread     Daily     \par                         (bid)    change   vs Bund    change in \par  Czech Republic                                      spread    \par 