\par\par \b \uc2 RPT- \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Euro hurt by German industrial data, Ukraine worries\b0\par\par\uc2 677 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 August 2014\par 10:34\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2014 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 (Repeats to additional subscribers)\par \par \uc2 * Euro recovers slightly after hitting 9-month low vs dollar\par \par \uc2 * Dollar index hits fresh 11-month peak\par \par \uc2 * Kiwi dented by fall in dairy prices, mixed NZ jobs data\par \par \uc2 By Jemima Kelly\par \par \uc2 LONDON, Aug 6 (Reuters) - The euro recovered a touch after sliding to a nine-month low against the dollar on Wednesday, hit by a sharp drop in German industrial orders and uncertainty about the intentions of Russian troops operating near the Ukrainian border.\par \par \uc2 The dollar hovered just under an 11-month high against a basket of major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  that it had hit earlier in the day, boosted by upbeat U.S. data as well as a move by investors away from \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  perceived as higher-risk plays.\par \par \uc2 The single European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dropped after data showed German industrial orders falling at their steepest rate in almost three years in June.\par \par \uc2 That appeared to have been partly the result of companies being more cautious about taking on contracts amid escalating geopolitical tensions, as well as lower euro zone demand.\par \par \uc2 Polish Prime Minister \uc2 Donald Tusk\uc2  said on Wednesday that he had reasons to suspect that the threat of a direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Russia's military in \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2  has risen over the last couple of days. [ID:nW8N0PY006\}\par \par \uc2 "Germany is very heavily linked to Russia on a trade basis, so given the trade sanctions that are now being put in place, Germany is going to be one of the countries that is hardest hit by that," said Ian Stannard, head of European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  in London.\par \par \uc2 "If we're in a position where activity data was softening already, and that's likely to be hit further by sanctions, that's going to leave the euro in quite a vulnerable position from here."\par \par \uc2 The euro fell to as low as $1.3349, its weakest since November. It last stood at $1.3370, flat on the day.\par \par \uc2 The dollar index surged to a peak of 81.637, its highest since early September.\par \par \uc2 Helping to support the greenback, data on Tuesday showed that the U.S. services sector activity hit an 8-1/2 year high last month and factory orders surged in June, bolstering expectations of solid economic growth in the third quarter.\par \par \uc2 "We're back into the medium-term trend, which is for a higher dollar," said Jesper Bargmann, head of trading for \uc2 Nordea Bank\uc2  in Singapore.\par \par \uc2 Over time the euro is likely to head lower versus the dollar, due to a divergence in the outlook for monetary policies of the United States and the euro zone, he said.\par \par \uc2 In addition to the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's policy meeting on Thursday, traders will be focused on forthcoming U.S. economic data and whether they come in strong enough to push forward market expectations for the timing of a \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  rate hike, Bargmann added.\par \par \uc2 The dollar held steady versus the safe-haven yen near 102.51 .\par \par \uc2 KIWI FALLS\par \par \uc2 The New Zealand dollar skidded to a two-month low after milk prices fell again at an auction held by Fonterra Co-operative Group, the world's biggest dairy exporter.\par \par \uc2 It extended its decline on data showing a moderation in jobs growth at home, an outcome that some suspect could buy the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  more time to stay on the sidelines following four successive interest rate hikes this year.\par \par \uc2 The latest retreat in equities and risk sentiment also dented the New Zealand dollar, said Hamish Pepper, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist for \uc2 Barclays\uc2  in Singapore.\par \par \uc2 "It's a confluence of factors, all negative for the kiwi dollar at the moment," Pepper said, adding that a focal point is whether support for the kiwi at its early June trough near $0.84 would hold.\par \par \uc2 The kiwi dropped 0.2 percent to $0.8449, having fallen as far as $0.8423, its lowest level in two months. (Additional reporting by Masayuki Kitano in Singapore and Ian Chua in Sydney; Editing by Toby Chopra) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2014-8-6T09:34:55.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020140806ea86004i3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw145\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2175\pichgoal600
