\par\par \b \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2  Bullish On Ukraine And Russia -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,507 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Mai 2014\par 18:47\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par Markettalk.eu@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1647 GMT [Dow Jones]--Despite the huge challenges Ukraine has to maintain its territorial integrity, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Societe Generale\f28 \uc2 \f2  thinks the situation will stabilise and the disbursement of the IMF/EU package should provide substantial support to Ukrainian assets. "We upgrade our view on Ukraine to overweight," says SocGen's Regis Chatellier. On the other hand, he expects that a direct military \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  from Russia in eastern \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ukraine\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is unlikely, and in that context, heavy sanctions against Moscow have become a low probability scenario. "Although we still could see selling pressures in the very short term, Russian bonds are poised to outperform in the coming weeks, as we think that most of the geopolitical risk affecting Russia is now behind us, and largely reflected into market valuations. We turn tactically overweight Russia," he says. (chiara.albanese@wsj.com @chiaraalbanese) \u61548\'F0\'6C\f28 \par \par \uc2 1613 GMT [Dow Jones]--Japanese investors have turned into net buyers of foreign bonds over the past month, according to data released by Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry Of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "Investors started the year out as net sellers, shedding foreign bonds in 12 out of the first 16 weeks of 2014 for a total net outflow during that period of $57.6 billion," notes BBH. Over the last 4 weeks inflows have been $26.4 billion of net foreign bond purchases, with nearly $14 billion over last week only. "Despite the increased purchases of foreign bonds by Japan investors, Uridashi issuance has not really picked up," notes analyst BBH Win Thin. Marketed to retail investors, Uridashi bonds represent a small slice of the \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. "But we believe that the observed trends in this segment can reflect those of the larger Japan investment community as well," he says. (chiara.albanese@wsj.com @chiaraalbanese). \u61548\'F0\'6C\par \par \uc2 1601 GMT [Dow Jones]--Trading volumes in G-10 \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  are rising ahead of next week's ECB meeting, mainly as increased activity among central banks and private clients, says \uc2 Credit Agricole\uc2 . "The EUR was sold last week, still on the back of firm expectations of the ECB considering more aggressive monetary policy action at next week's meeting," the bank said in its client monitor. Hedge funds and private clients selling the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  suggests rising upside risk should the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  disappoint elevated market expectations, the bank adds. (chiara.albanese@wsj.com @chiaraalbanese). \u61548\'F0\'6C\par \par \uc2 1530 GMT [Dow Jones]--In the short term, investors might be attracted back into Russian markets as growth looks resilient, supported by the tight labor market, a high oil price and a weak ruble, says \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2 . But while the short-term outlook is positive, medium-term the picture is different. "We see a lasting impact on investor willingness to hold Russian assets, and a lack of domestic reform appetite, which would keep future Russian growth prospects subdued, capped at around 2% per annum," says \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2 . (chiara.albanese@wsj.com @chiaraalbanese).\par \par \uc2 11:22 EDT - \uc2 Barclays\uc2  says several themes appear to be simmering in \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets which could disrupt the "current benign market environment." Uncertainty over ECB and BOE policy is unlikely to force an immediate break from the recent market tranquility, but do "suggest that a period of more compelling price action may soon be upon us," \uc2 Barclays\uc2  says. Firm says there's more uncertainty over the timing of BOE action after a hawkish shift seen in the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's May meeting minutes, which sets up a bigger potential shakeup once action is taken. Investors are also expecting the ECB to cut rates June, though it's uncertain if it'll be paired with a more-aggressive stimulus program. (don.curren@wsj.com; @dbcurren)\par \par \uc2 1521 GMT [Dow Jones]--Tensions between the Ukraine and Russia are relaxing, but in the long term, Russia will likely keep breathing down its neighbor's neck, says Jan Dehn, head of research at Ashmore. "We expect the de-escalation that began following the second round of Western sanctions against Russia to continue, albeit unevenly," he writes in a note to clients. He explains that it will be uneven "because Russia is likely to continue to exercise its influence in Eastern Ukraine in order to extract concessions from the Poroshenko administration to ensure that Ukraine does not become a Poland-like, staunchly pro-Western country." Longer-term, Mr. Dehn says, Russia is still hoping to create the conditions that push Ukraine towards joining the Eurasian Economic Union --an EU-like institution with the ruble as the main \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for setting investment and trade transactions. (josie.cox@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 11:02 EDT - Fresh research from the \uc2 Cleveland Fed\uc2  points to how the residential real-estate market may not be as healthy as 2013's price increases might indicate. "The experiences of the past year highlight the strong interest-rate sensitivity of the housing sector." Activity, especially on the mortgage-refinance front, slowed after the mid-2013 pop in long-term interest rates. Bank researchers' model "suggest that even moderate increases in mortgage rates" through 2015 to around 5.5%--"which remains low by historical standards--potentially could pose a headwind to residential investment." As such, "low mortgage rates are likely to be an important factor underlying ongoing recovery in the housing market and, by extension, the broader economy." Something else for Yellen & Co. to chew on as the long-awaited rate-hike cycle looms? (kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com; @kevinkingsbury)\par \par \uc2 10:54 EDT - The drop in Treasury yields hasn't had the usual negative effect on MBS, mostly as the Fed's tapered purchases continue to overwhelm a market devoid of supply, says FTN Financial. QE3 has kept current coupon MBS yields little changed relative to 10-year Treasury yields for 4 months; the spread is currently 67.4 basis points, according to \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  data, versus 96 last summer as yields soared. However, investment banks such as \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  expect the supply/demand dynamic to shift in June or July, applying technical pressure to MBS as the Fed continues to taper and as buyers such as mortgage REITs stick to the sidelines. (albert.yoon@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 10:37 EDT - The monthly survey carried out by \uc2 Chile's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of financial traders finds expectations for Chile's monetary policy rate to remain unchanged in June at 4%, although the traders see a decline to 3.75% going three months out. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has cut its policy rate by 50 basis points so far this year. The survey also finds expectations for a 0.3% month-over-month rise in inflation in Chile in May. (robert.kozak@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1418 GMT [Dow Jones]--GBP/USD remains in a corrective mode since the recent topside failure close to 1.69, and a daily close later Wednesday below the most recent reaction low of 1.6737 would add to this corrective pressure, says RBC Capital Market's chief technical analyst George Davis. Such a move would open the downside to 1.6687 ahead of 1.6558, while topside resistance at 1.6793 and 1.6898 should attract renewed selling interest as corrective tendencies play-out. Mr. Davis says only a daily close above 1.6916 would nullify the current downside risks. GBP/USD now at 1.6710. (gary.stride@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1405 GMT [Dow Jones]--Selloff, what selloff? Italian and Spanish bonds are rallying again after a recent wobble, with yields in Spain plunging to fresh record lows. And RBS thinks this recent strength may still have some legs. The bank notes that financial institutions in higher-rated euro zone countries are seeking alternatives to their own government bonds to lock in better returns. RBS notes that German, Dutch, Austrian and Finnish banks' holdings of non domestic government bonds are at the highest since 2011, for instance. According to RBS, this demand should continue to underpin Italian and Spanish bonds and help shrink the yield spread between these bonds and German Bunds to 100 bps from 163 bps and 152 bps, respectively. (neelabh.chaturvedi@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1351 GMT [Dow Jones]--Markets are positioned almost uniformly for an ECB rate cut next week. A clients' survey by Nomura suggests that 95% expect a refi rate cut and 90% expect a depo rate cut. Moreover, 62% of the bank's clients expect some form of liquidity injection and 53% expect quantitative easing to be announced sometime in 2014. "It is this set of expectations, which makes it tricky to view the ECB meeting as a strong catalyst for a EUR decline," says Nomura analyst Jens Nordvig. Nomura is positioned for a grinding move lower in EUR/USD. "There has been no real bounces in EUR/USD since May 8. But we would consider adding downside risk on any temporary backup," says the bank. (chiara.albanese@wsj.com @chiaraalbanese).\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 May 28, 2014 12:47 ET (16:47 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140528ea5s002z4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
