\par\par \b \uc2 GLOBAL MARKETS-Shares cautious on Ukraine anxiety; China, India lead gains\b0\par\par\uc2 616 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Mai 2014\par 08:07\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2014 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Markets focus on reaction to east Ukraine referendum\par \par \uc2 * China shares up on capital market reform hopes\par \par \uc2 * Mumbai shares up ahead of elections\par \par \uc2 * Oil rises on Ukraine concerns\par \par \uc2 * Euro under pressure after ECB comments\par \par \uc2 By Hideyuki Sano\par \par \uc2 TOKYO, May 12 (Reuters) - Shares made a cautious advance on Monday though gains were capped as investors braced for a possible escalation in Ukraine's civil conflicts after anti-Kiev rebels declared victory in a referendum on self-rule.\par \par \uc2 Hopes of capital market reform boosted Chinese shares while Indian shares surged to record high on the prospect of a more business-friendly government winning India's general election, with exit polls later coming out later in the day.\par \par \uc2 These expectations helped to lift MSCI's broadest index of Asia-Pacific shares outside Japan 0.6 percent.\par \par \uc2 Hong Kong shares rose 2.2 percent while India's benchmark index rose as much as 1.8 percent and the Indian rupee also hit nine-month high.\par \par \uc2 European shares are also expected to rise, with Germany's DAX seen gaining as much as 0.4 percent and Britain's FTSE up to 0.2 percent.\par \par \uc2 "Given a dearth of trading factors today, markets will pay attention to how Kiev and Moscow will react to the results of referendum," said \uc2 Masafumi Yamamoto\uc2 , chief strategist at Praevidentia Strategy.\par \par \uc2 Organisers of the weekend referendum in Ukraine said nearly 90 percent had voted in favour, possibly opening the way for the region to break away from Kiev in a conflict that is increasingly out of control.\par \par \uc2 Western leaders have threatened more sanctions in the key areas of energy, financial services and engineering if Moscow disrupts a presidential election planned in Ukraine on May 25.\par \par \uc2 Concerns of more violence pushed up oil prices, with U.S. crude futures rising above $100 per barrel.\par \par \uc2 "There could be military \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Russia, or more armed \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the West could impose more sanctions on Russia. These could lead to falls in U.S. bond yields and the dollar/yen," Praevidentia's Yamamoto said.\par \par \uc2 For now, \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  prices dipped slightly, lifting the benchmark 10-year yield to 2.636 percent, compared to 2.623 percent at the end of last week.\par \par \uc2 The yen also weakened slightly, with the dollar trading at 101.98 yen, having found support around 101.40 last week.\par \par \uc2 Despite simmering geopolitical concerns, global shares have been resilient, with the Dow Jones Industrial average posting a record closing high on Friday. European shares also hit a six-year high.\par \par \uc2 "At the end of the day, U.S. corporate earnings will be little affected by Ukraine. Unless market sentiment drastically changes, stocks are likely to be supported," said Takuro Nishida, deputy manager of investment planning at Nipponkoa Insurance.\par \par \uc2 One of the driving forces for stock markets is the prospect of continued policy support from the Federal Reserve and the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Dovish comments from Fed Chair \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2  last week underpinned risk assets around the globe, including many emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President \uc2 Mario Draghi\uc2  warned on Thursday that the euro's strength was a serious concern and that the ECB was comfortable with taking more action to support economic growth.\par \par \uc2 The euro has been on the defensive since then, and last traded at $1.3762, not far from the one-month low of $1.3745 hit on Friday.\par \par \uc2 There is little in the way of major economic data releases on Monday. On Tuesday, industrial production and retail sales in China and U.S. retail sales will be closely watched. (Editing by Eric Meijer) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2014-5-12T07:07:47.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020140512ea5c0005t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
