\par\par \b \uc2 Ukraine, Russia CDS See-Saw on New Sanctions -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,423 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 April 2014\par 17:43\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 11:43 EDT - Cost of protection from a Ukraine bond default rose after the US and Europe on Monday announced additional sanctions against Russian officials and companies in retaliation to Russian military \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The cost of the so-called credit default swaps on Russian bonds fell when the new sanctions included far fewer targets than had been expected. The annual cost of insuring $10M worth of Ukraine bonds for five years rose 2.5% after the announcement to $1.27M, according to Markit. The price of Russian credit default swaps fell 6% over the same period. (matthieu.wirz@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 11:13 EDT - Canadian data out Wednesday is expected to show GDP advanced in February, but at a markedly slower pace following a strong January result. Expectations are for a 0.2% monthly gain, after January's 0.5% advance as the economy rebounded following a bout of nasty winter weather in late 2013. But pent-up demand likely ebbed as highlighted by weak volumes in February retail sales. The data, economists warn, could also include a downward revision for January GDP as previous estimates for manufacturing, retail and wholesale were downgraded. A slowdown in March is also in the cards as a strike at the main port in Vancouver -- North America's fourth-largest by tonnage -- crippled trade. (paul.vieira@wsj.com; @paulvieira)\par \par \uc2 11:07 EDT - Chuck Burge, head of US investment grade portfolio management at Invesco, told WSJ on Tuesday he plans to spend $100M to buy Apple's (AAPL) new bonds maturing in 7, 10 and 30 years. "The bonds are an attractive alternative to Treasury bonds," he says. "Apple is a brand name everyone knows about." Burge says the size of the offering could come in smaller than many expect, which sets up stronger demand as investors clamor for pieces of a smaller pie. He says he might not be able to buy the amount he wants. One drawback about the offering he notes is that the sales aim to benefit shareholders at the expense of creditors, but adds that new bonds with a double A rating are "hard to beat." (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj)\par \par \uc2 1506 GMT [Dow Jones]--The U.K. debt office says it will sell the 2044 inflation-linked bond for GBP1.2 billion next week. The auction will take place on May 7 and will take up the amount of the bond outstanding to GBP14.62 billion. Inflation-linked bonds differ from conventional bonds that the original investment and interest payments are adjusted for inflation. (neelabh.chaturvedi@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1505 GMT [Dow Jones]--\uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  recommends investors to buy bonds in the belly of the Hungarian and Polish yield curves. More specifically, \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  recommends buying Poland's July 2018 bond, \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  unhedged, at a yield of 3.45%, targeting 3%, with stop-loss at 3.70%, and Hungary's June 2019 bond, \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  hedged, at 4.5%, targeting 4%, with stop loss at 4.75%. Amid growing chances of an ECB rate cut and/or a QE program, yields in Poland, Hungary and Romania are looking increasingly attractive, \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  says, adding that with market \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related fears behind, Central and Eastern European bonds could start catching up with the rally in the southern European countries. (emese.bartha@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1449 GMT [Dow Jones] Hungary is to leave its main monetary policy rate unchanged for the rest of the year, Capital Economics forecasts. This follows the \uc2 National Bank of Hungary\uc2  cutting its main rate to a fresh record low of 2.50% and the rate setters keep all options open in their rate statement. CapEcon wouldn't rule out another small cut of 5-10 bps, "but irrespective of whether the monetary policy council cuts interest rates again, the bigger picture is that monetary policy is likely to remain loose for longer than most expect," says Capital Economics economist William Jackson. In the event of a fresh bout of financial market tensions in the euro zone, the HUF would weaken sharply, forcing the MPC to tighten policy. "But assuming this scenario can be avoided, the MPC is likely to be under little pressure to raise interest rates," Mr. Jackson adds. Modest rate increases may follow in 2015, probably 50bp to 3.00% by end-2015, he adds. (margit.feher@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 10:46 EDT - Bankers managing Apple's (AAPL) bond sale on Tuesday have received about $24B in orders for the bonds, according to one investor following the sale. Another investor said the order book was at about $30B "and growing." No size has been set yet on the deal, but one person familiar with the matter said guidance was in the $8B to $10B range, though that can change throughout the day. Apple last year sold $17B in bonds, the largest corporate bond deal on record at that time. (mike.cherney@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 10:41 EDT - The \uc2 New York Fed\uc2 's research staff last fall had a study which showed the Conference Board's "present situation index" being highly correlated to moves in the unemployment rate the past 35 years. Based on that trend, today's consumer-confidence report sets up risks for an unexpected uptick in the US jobless rate--though not likely in Friday's report as the NY Fed said the strongest correlation is with the unemployment rate 2 months into the future. The present index slipped to 78.3 from 82.5 in March. Economists expect April's unemployment rate to decline to 6.6% from 6.7%. (cynthia.lin@wsj.com; @cynthialin_dj)\par \par \uc2 1435 GMT [Dow Jones]--\uc2 Hungary's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has adopted a neutral stance and is likely to keep that view for several quarters to come, says Gabor Ambrus at 4Cast. Hungary has cut its main policy rate to 2.50% from 2.60%, in line with expectations, and the central bankers published a non-committal statement to any future rate path after the move. With its March statement reiterated now, that the main rate has significantly approached a level consistent with price stability and supporting the economy "the monetary policy council indicates that it sent a warning last month and now this warning has become reality. This, we think shows that council has shifted stance" from its easing bias to a neutral one, says Mr. Ambrus. (margit.feher@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1430 GMT [Dow Jones]--\uc2 Hungary's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has now started its wait-and-see period, says \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2 , after it cuts its main interest rate by 10 bps to a new record low of 2.50%. "The statement was not as clear in marking the end of the easing cycle as we had hoped. However, our take is that the \uc2 National Bank of Hungary\uc2  feels that it has delivered the desired rate cuts in the easing cycle that began at 7% in summer 2012," says \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  economist Pasquale Diana. There is still some chance of near-term easing, Mr. Diana adds, but that's not \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2 's base case. Sees the main rate at 2.75% by year-end and 4% by end-2015, with downside risks. Notes Funding for Growth Scheme and change of two week-bills into two-week deposits "represent a further easing of monetary conditions." (margit.feher@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1425 GMT [Dow Jones]--Greek bonds rise, recovering from recent selloff that saw yields climb by over 40 bps in a week. The sentiment has been helped by the upgrade of the outlook of Greece's banking system by \uc2 Moody's\uc2 . The 10-year yield is down 8.5 bps from Monday's close at 6.22%. (neelabh.chaturvedi@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 10:23 EDT - Apple's (AAPL) new bond sale on Tuesday is offering more yield than where the company sold bonds last year, according to investors. Ten-year bonds could yield roughly 0.90 percentage points more than comparable US Treasurys, while 30-year bonds could yield 1.15 to 1.20 percentage points more. Last year, Apple's 10-year bonds yielded 0.75 more and the 30-years yielded 1.00 percentage point more. Pricing on the new bonds, though, could change today depending on the level of demand from investors. One investor said the deal is being "very well received." (mike.cherney@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 April 29, 2014 11:43 ET (15:43 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140429ea4t0031t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
