\par\par \b \uc2 Macro Horizons: Russia, Suffering Pains of Sanctions, Is Forced to Raise Rates -- WSJ Blog\b0\par\par\uc2 1,349 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 April 2014\par 13:10\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 By Michael J. Casey, Alen Mattich and Michael S. Arnold\par \par \uc2 Macro Horizons covers the main macroeconomic and policy news events affecting \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 , fixed income and equity markets around the world, as selected by editors in New York, London and Hong Kong.\par \par \uc2 WRAP: Friday's news flows finds three of the world's biggest economies facing very different policy dilemmas for very different reasons. Russia is facing big capital outflows, mostly because of concerns among its residents that the sanctions imposed by Western nations in response to the Kremlin's incursions in Ukraine will hurt the economy. The way out for President Vladimir Putin is to admit Russia's direct use of special forces in Crimea and eastern Ukraine and pull them out. But do so would no doubt create political risks, which is why he seems prepared to ride out the economic hardship, including allowing the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to increase rates. In Japan, the problem is that the policy options running out. After all the aggressive monetary stimulus used to bolster demand, inflation is still far from what the 2% that the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  is targeting. What does the BOJ do now? Even more bond-buying? And China's problems revolve around exports, whose weakness has manifested in the narrowest current-account surplus in three years. In actual fact, the lower surplus, gives it ammunition to fend off Western criticism of its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yuan, but it also highlights a different sort of dilemma. Beijing needs to wean the economy off an dependence on exports and allow it to become more focused on consumer spending, and to some extent that would be helped by a strong yuan that would make imports cheaper and boost domestic purchasing power. However, the export sector continues to be a massive provider of jobs and the government needs to keep finding employment for millions of young Chinese who reaching working age every year and are migrating from the cities to \uc2 seek\uc2  work. (MC)\par \par \uc2 RUSSIA: \uc2 Standard & Poor's\uc2  downgraded Russia to one notch above junk, citing large capital outflows, after which the Russian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raised its key interest rate by half a percentage point to 7.5% in an effort to defend the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the effect of those same outflows. Meanwhile, Ukrainian forces pulled back after taking action against pro-Russian militants in the east of the country amid reports of a build-up of Russian forces along the border.\par \par \uc2 The Russian economy has already started to suffer from sanctions and investor nervousness over its involvement in the Ukrainian crisis and the annexation of Crimea. Yet this doesn't appear to be deterring President Putin or helping ease the crisis, as would be the West's goal. The risk of outright war between the two countries has grown. Although a Russian military victory would be a foregone conclusion - no one expects any direct western \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2 's behalf - but the longer term repercussions for Russia's economy could be drastic. (AM)\par \par \uc2 JAPAN: Core consumer prices in Tokyo rose 2.7% on-year in April - the first price data since Japan's sales tax was raised on April 1 -- but by just 1% if the tax impact is stripped out.\par \par \uc2 CPI data for Tokyo is reported a month before the rest of Japan, and is considered indicative of the national trend. Nationwide, core consumer prices for March -- before the tax increase -- rose 1.3% on-year for the third straight, slightly below expectations. Friday's readings add to the view that the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  will find it difficult to achieve 2% inflation by its April 2015 deadline -- especially as the impact of the weaker should drop out of the calculation in coming months. Indeed, only four of 33 respondents in a Wall Street Journal poll expect inflation to reach 2% by next spring. The \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  will issue its semi-annual economic outlook next week, and many observers expect it to implement additional monetary easing later this year if the economy slows as anticipated after the tax increase. (MA)\par \par \uc2 CHINA: China's current-account surplus fell to its narrowest in three years in the first quarter, at just 0.3% of GDP -- a far cry from its peak at 10.1% in 2007 and even last year's 2.1%. In absolute terms, the surplus narrowed to $7.2 billion, from $44 billion in the final quarter of 2013.\par \par \uc2 The shrinking surplus suggests the yuan may already be close to fair value, and should blunt pressure from Washington for significant further appreciation. Indeed, the yuan is down more than 3% against the dollar this year and continued falling Friday, hitting a fresh 16-month low at 6.2583 to the dollar. The recent plunge began shortly as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  prepared to widen the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's daily trading band earlier this year, leading to accusations that the drop was engineered in Beijing, but Chinese policy makers say the weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is justified by the slowing economy. (MA)\par \par \uc2 U.K.:\par \par \uc2 -- March retail sales up 0.1% on the month and up 4.2% on the year against expectations of down 0.1% and up 4.1% respectively.\par \par \uc2 --March \uc2 British Bankers Association\uc2  mortgage approvals 45,933 from 47,196 in February; net mortgage lending up GBP806 million from GBP849 million in February; and consumer credit up GBP400 million from a fall of GBP100 million in February.\par \par \uc2 British retail sales growth steadied March while February data were revised down leaving sales growth up 0.8% in the first quarter. That's a tick up from the final quarter of last year. Data from the BBA suggest people are leaning on their credit cards to keep spending ticking over, though a pick-up in real earnings after years of declines should offer some support over the coming quarters. (AM)\par \par \uc2 CYPRUS: S&P upgraded Cyprus' credit rating and Fitch raises the outlook to Stable as the financial crisis there is slowly resolved. However it cites Russia's problems as possible risks to the Cypriot economy.\par \par \uc2 COMING UP:\par \par \uc2 BRAZIL: 9:30 a.m. EDT. (10:30 a.m, Sao Paulo.) March balance of payments. [Previously current account deficit $7.44 billion, or 3.69% of GDP.]\par \par \uc2 Brazil's current account deficit continues to deteriorate, and while it isn't getting out of control it represents something of a vulnerability for its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the real. The deficit represents an external funding gap for the Brazilian economy. (MC)\par \par \uc2 U.S.: 9:55 a.m. EDT. Thomson Reuters/\uc2 University of Michigan\uc2  end-April consumer sentiment survey. [Consumer sentiment index 83 vs. mid-month 80.]\par \par \uc2 This measure of consumer confidence dropped to a four-month low at the end of March. Economists are looking for April to have delivered a bounceback. (MC)\par \par \uc2 MEXICO: 10 a.m. EDT. (9 a.m. Mexico City.) \uc2 Banco de Mexico\uc2  monetary policy decision announcement. [Currently overnight rate at 3.5% after no change at previous meeting.]\par \par \uc2 The decision to hold rates steady last month reflected greater comfort among officers of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  that an inflation threat is subsiding. But with the peso having weakened, the risk of price pressures remains something for \uc2 Banxico\uc2 's policy makers to keep their eye on. (MC)\par \par \uc2 COLOMBIA: Time N/A. Banco de la Republica Colombia monetary policy meeting and decision. [Current benchmark rate 3.25%]\par \par \uc2 Will the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  continue its dollar-purchase program? That's arguably a more important question than that of interest rates, which are expected to stay unchanged, where they have been for the past year. The fact that Colombia has continued to buy dollars to weaken the Colombian peso while other emerging-market countries have done the opposite over the past year reflects how positively foreign investors view the overall assessment of Colombia's economic outlook. (MC) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  More at The Wall Street Journal's MoneyBeat blog, \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://blogs.wsj.com/moneybeat/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://blogs.wsj.com/moneybeat/}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 April 25, 2014 07:10 ET (11:10 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140425ea4p001pf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
