\par\par \b \uc2 European Morning Briefing: Valuations, Ukraine Top Concerns\b0\par\par\uc2 3,440 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 April 2014\par 06:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SNAPSHOT:\par \par \uc2 ========\par \par \uc2 -European bourses seen mostly lower, bunds and gilts mixed; euro lower, oil, spot gold higher\par \par \uc2 -Germany's Angela Merkel Treads Softly With Russia's Putin On Ukraine\par \par \uc2 -Ukrainian Leaders Slam Russia for Attempt to 'Tear Country Apart'\par \par \uc2 -US Warns Russia Of \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  'Costs'\par \par \uc2 -Obama Administration Shows Optimism on Iran Nuclear Talks\par \par \uc2 -Fed To Give Banks Two Extra Years For Some Securities To Comply With Volcker Rule\par \par \uc2 -Exports by U.K. Service Providers Grow at Fastest Ever Rate\par \par \uc2 -Watch for: German manufacturing turnover, federal budget; U.K. industrial production; U.S. Fed speakers, weekly oil data\par \par \uc2 EUROPEAN OUTLOOK & US/ASIAN SUMMARIES:\par \par \uc2 ========\par \par \uc2 Investors remain cautious, leaving European stock markets likely to start lower, with bunds and gilts mixed, as Ukraine, monetary policy and stock valuations remain at the forefront of worrisome issues.\par \par \uc2 Japanese stocks led Asia lower on Tuesday, as the yen strengthened before the end of the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2 's policy meeting, while regional technology stocks fell. Investors look ahead to see whether the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's governor, \uc2 Haruhiko Kuroda\uc2 , will hint at additional easing pressures when he speaks to the media\par \par \uc2 Wall Street futures were higher Tuesday, with Treasurys slightly lower, as the euro fell, while oil futures and spot gold gained.\par \par \uc2 STOCKS:\par \par \uc2 ======\par \par \uc2 Investors are likely to see a mostly lower opening in Europe after a Wall Street selloff as they prepare for what could be a tricky earnings season. Ahead of the opening, IG has called the FTSE down 16 at 6606, the DAX down 5 at 9505, and the CAC up 5 at 4441.\par \par \uc2 "People are trying to get more defensive ahead of this earnings season," said Keith Bliss, senior vice president at brokerage Cuttone & Co. "There have been revisions down on both earnings growth and revenue growth. They've been coming down pretty steadily since December."\par \par \uc2 IG strategist Evan Lucas noted, "With very little on the macro news front and the U.S. earnings season not starting in earnest until Friday, the bears are likely to overwhelm most of the over valued stocks on the NASDAQ in the next three days."\par \par \uc2 For Europe, the risk of quantitative easing and lower interest rates "would seem to encourage equity market flows," but "negative sentiment is prevailing" in the region, said \uc2 Brown Brothers Harriman\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Wall Street futures were higher Tuesday, after stocks tumbled Monday as a three-day selloff in technology shares spread to other sectors, reflecting investor anxiety over the coming first-quarter earnings season.\par \par \uc2 Many of the highflying growth stocks pummeled last week continued to slide Monday, but the selling spread to other corners of the market, with financial stocks and consumer discretionary stocks posting the biggest losses in the S&P 500. The declines reflected investor positioning ahead of what analysts expect to be a lackluster series of first-quarter earnings reports, traders said.\par \par \uc2 The season kicks off Tuesday with a report from aluminum giant \uc2 Alcoa\uc2  and continues Friday with updates from financial firms \uc2 J.P. Morgan\uc2  and \uc2 Wells Fargo\uc2 .\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 :\par \par \uc2 ======\par \par \uc2 The euro and dollar fell against the yen in Asia as risk appetite stumbled while the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  mulled policy and speculation over the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  continued.\par \par \uc2 ECB President Mario Draghi said last week that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's board is "unanimous in its commitment" to use "unconventional instruments" such as bond purchases.\par \par \uc2 "Draghi had raised the market's hopes for QE on Thursday, causing the market to lean more bearish to the euro," said Marc Chandler, global head of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  at \uc2 Brown Brothers Harriman & Co\uc2 ., referring to quantitative easing. "Now there's been some pushback against that, which has given short-term traders reason to cover their short positions."\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the U.S. is looking closely at the recent weakness in China's yuan and is concerned about the global economic fallout of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decline.\par \par \uc2 A senior official at the Treasury Department said it would "raise serious concerns" if Beijing is moving away from plans to allow market forces to have a greater impact on the yuan's exchange rate, especially if Chinese officials are at the same time citing greater flexibility in the yuan's movements.\par \par \uc2 BONDS:\par \par \uc2 =====\par \par \uc2 Bunds may start mixed while locked in ranges on influences from the Ukraine and from potential monetary policy.\par \par \uc2 In euro zone, where speculation has increased about the ECB launching its own quantitative easing measures in the coming months, ECB board member Yves Mersch on Monday played down hopes prospects for imminent asset purchases, saying "QE is above all a theoretical concept."\par \par \uc2 In the U.K., gilts may start mixed with data on tap. British manufacturing has been expanding healthily, though largely in service of the housing industry, which is galloping ahead, rather than towards exports. This trend is likely to remain in place over the coming months as euphoria over property gathers pace.\par \par \uc2 Exports by British services providers grew at the fastest rate on record in the first quarter, a survey showed Tuesday, adding to evidence that a recovery fuelled by consumer spending is beginning to broaden out to the wider economy.\par \par \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  bonds were lower, after markets extended their winning streak into a third straight session on Monday as concerns continued to ease that the Fed may raise interest rates sooner than expected.\par \par \uc2 "The Fed will be patient with respect to the removal of its monetary accommodation," said Tony Crescenzi, market strategist at \uc2 Pacific Investment Management Co\uc2 ., which has $1.91 trillion of global assets under management.\par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, the release of the minutes from the latest Fed meeting will provide some further insight into the reasoning of the Fed's policymakers.\par \par \uc2 "We expect the minutes to suggest that the FOMC believes that rate hikes are probably further off than some thought after the post-meeting press conference," the Nordic region's largest bank, Nordea, said in a weekly note. Ms. Yellen has indicated that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could start hiking interest rates as soon as six months after the end of quantitative easing.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Crescenzi said that for the 10-year yield to rise above 3%, the U.S. economy needs to accelerate toward "the consensus of close to 3%" growth.\par \par \uc2 St. Louis Fed President James Bullard said the rebound in hiring during March suggests the economy is getting back on track after taking a hit from adverse weather over the start of the year.\par \par \uc2 In Japan Tuesday, government bonds rose. Market watchers welcomed a decision by Japan's largest pension fund manager, the Government Pension Investment Fund, to revamp its conservative Japanese stock investing strategy. The only meaningful downside may be felt in the bond market if GPIF's exposure to stocks comes at the expense of buying Japanese government bonds, said some traders.\par \par \uc2 ENERGY:\par \par \uc2 =======\par \par \uc2 Crude-oil futures rebounded in Asia after dropping overnight, ahead of initial weekly U.S. oil inventory data. Both Nymex WTI and Brent crude have settled lower for four of the past six sessions. ANZ said the market has been cautious about Libyan ports reopening. "The ongoing supply disruptions have left Libya--the holder of Africa's largest crude oil reserves--at the bottom of the OPEC production table this year," it added. Initial U.S. oil stocks data from the API are expected later today. "Expectations are that crude oil inventories gained over one million barrels last week whilst gasoline stocks are forecast to continue their recent draw," ANZ said. Meanwhile, pro-Moscow protesters in eastern Ukraine have called for their own referendum on independence, indicating fresh tensions in the regions. Nymex WTI crude was up 44 cents at $100.88/bbl, and Brent crude was up 33 cents at $106.15/bbl.\par \par \uc2 METALS:\par \par \uc2 ======\par \par \uc2 Base metals were likely to trade mostly rangebound, looking for catalysts to push prices, while nickel could remain underpinned by tensions in Ukraine and by the ban on the export of unprocessed ore from Indonesia. LME 3-month copper was up $56 at $6,675, while nickel was down $2 at $16,520.\par \par \uc2 Spot gold edged higher, but analysts remain skeptical about a sustained rally with investors cautious about the prospects for the metal. Traders will look to the minutes of the Federal Reserve's latest meeting, due Wednesday, for clues about the future of monetary policy. The Fed's monthly bond-buying has been supportive of gold prices. Gold could find support around $1,270/oz, which could underpin a move towards $1,350/oz, \uc2 Barclays\uc2  said in a report citing technical indicators. Spot gold was at $1,300.36/oz, up $3.00.\par \par \uc2 Write to Dennis Baker at dennis.baker@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 8 Apr 2014 00:00 ET  European Morning Briefing: DJ Dominant Headlines; Other Top Stories\par \par \uc2 DJ Dominant Headlines:\par \par \uc2 AT&T\uc2  Experiment Would Cut Landline in Alabama Town\par \par \uc2 Sheridan IPO Could Raise Up to $500M, Value Co. At More Than $2B, Sources Say\par \par \uc2 Citi Is Bracing to Miss a Profit Target\par \par \uc2 Other Top Stories:\par \par \uc2 Movie Studios Sue Megaupload and Kim Dotcom for Copyright Infringement\par \par \uc2 The major Hollywood studios fired a legal salvo against what was once one of the world's most popular file-sharing sites, suing Megaupload Ltd. and its executives for money they say was earned through copyright infringement.\par \par \uc2 USEC Paid Executives and Advisors $30 Million on Way to Bankruptcy\par \par \uc2 Nuclear power plant fuel supplier \uc2 USEC Inc\uc2 . (USU), which is being pushed out of its flagship project by the \uc2 U.S. Department of Energy\uc2 , shelled out more than $30 million in the past 12 months for top executives and bankruptcy advisers, court papers say.\par \par \uc2 Senate Passes Bill to Restore Unemployment Benefits Through May\par \par \uc2 With bipartisan support, the Senate voted Monday to restore emergency unemployment benefits that expired last year through May, but Republican leaders in the House aren't expected to take up the legislation.\par \par \uc2 8 Apr 2014 00:00 ET  European Morning Briefing: Market Prices, Data Calendar\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par DJIA           16245.87 -166.84 -1.02% \par Nasdaq          4079.75  -47.97 -1.16% \par S&P 500         1845.04  -20.05 -1.08% \par FTSE 100        6622.84  -72.71 -1.09% \par DAX             9510.85 -184.92 -1.91% \par CAC40           4436.08  -48.47 -1.08% \par Closing \par  \par Dow Future     16204.00  +26.00 +0.2% \par NASDAQ Future   3505.00   +5.75 +0.2% \par S&P Future      1841.90   +3.80 +0.2% \par As of 0330 GMT \par  \par N