\par\par \b \uc2 RPT-European Factors to Watch-Shares set to continue bounce on U.S. optimism\b0\par\par\uc2 1,223 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2014\par 08:57\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2014 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Repeats to additional subscribers)               \par \par \uc2 LONDON, March 26 (Reuters) - European stocks were seen rising at the open on Wednesday, buoyed by increasing signs of life in the U.S. economy, with a key index set to continue a rally off a one-week closing low back towards multi-year highs.               \par \par \uc2 At 0720 GMT, futures for the Euro STOXX 50, Britain's FTSE 100 , Germany's DAX and France's CAC were 0.3-0.5 percent higher.               \par \par \uc2 Stock markets in Europe were broadly expected to track gains made in Asian and U.S. trade, where buoyant U.S. consumer confidence and house prices helped to fuel gains.               \par \par \uc2 The two reports were the latest in a series of positive data releases from the United States, supporting the view that bad weather rather than inherent economic weakness affected weaker reports earlier this year.               \par \par \uc2 "Concerns around the three C's (cold, Crimea, China) are dropping off as the effect of the US winter subsides, the Crimean conflict is no longer affecting markets and China has seen stimulation bets ramping up," Evan Lucas, market strategist at IG, said in a note.               \par \par \uc2 "This will mean markets will continue to push higher in the short term, with the US and China currently driving most market reactions; and signs of stability or growth will be equity and commodity supportive."               \par \par \uc2 Growing bets that China will act to stimulate its economy and an increased resilience to uncertainty over relations between Russia and the West over Crimea helped to support European equity markets on Tuesday.               \par \par \uc2 The pan-European FTSEurofirst 300 rose 1.3 percent in the previous session, bouncing off a one-week closing low set at the start of the week.               \par \par \uc2 The index remains 3 percent off its closing high in February, however, which was its highest close since May 2008.               \par \par \uc2 Economic confidence in Europe also held up despite ongoing concerns about Russian \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2 , with market research group GfK finding that German consumer morale held steady at its highest reading since January 2007.               \par \par \uc2 The reading comes on the back of comments from European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Mario Draghi and Bundesbank President \uc2 Jens Weidmann\uc2  that stimulus from the euro zone's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was not out of the question as the euro zone still battles with deflationary pressures.               \par \par \uc2 The unexpected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from Weidmann, whose Bundesbank had previously expressed scepticism about such measures, also helped to boost European shares on Tuesday.               \par \par \uc2 "While talk is cheap, the ECB will ultimately need to back its words with credible measures, with the standard toolbox running on empty," Anatoli Annenkov, senior European economist at \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2 , said in a note.                              \par \par \uc2 Europe bourses in 2014: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://link.reuters.com/pad95v"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://link.reuters.com/pad95v}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2   > GLOBAL MARKETS-Asia shares hit 2-wk highs on upbeat U.S. data    \par   > US STOCKS-Wall St rebounds; biotech shares snap losing streak    \par   > Nikkei up slightly in choppy trade, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Kirin Holdings\f28 \uc2 \f2  soars     \par   > TREASURIES-Solid demand, but light dealer purchases        \par   > \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 -Euro steadies after ECB clarifies monetary policy stance    \par   > PRECIOUS-Gold rebounds from near 6-week low             \par   > METALS-Copper slips after sharp rise on China stimulus hopes    \par   > Brent rises above $107 on supply concerns, strong US data\par               \par               \par COMPANY NEWS\par               \par               \par LLOYDS BANKING GROUP\par               \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Britain sold 4.2 billion pounds ($6.9 billion) of shares in \f28 \uc2 \f2 Lloyds Banking\par Group\f28 \uc2 \f2  to cut its stake in the country's largest retail bank to 25\par percent and put it on course for a complete exit in the next year at a profit.\par               \par               \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 DEUTSCHE BANK\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 German prosecutors widened an investigation to include a second Deutsche\par Bank board member in another twist to the lender's legal battle with heirs of\par late media mogul Leo Kirch that included a search of the bank's headquarters on\par Tuesday.\par               \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Related news\par               \par               \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 LUFTHANSA\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. Federal Aviation Administration\f28 \uc2 \f2  on Tuesday ordered an immediate fix\par to the latest version of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Boeing Co\f28 \uc2 \f2 's BA.N 747-8 plane, saying a software glitch\par could cause it to lose thrust when close to landing and fly into the ground.\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Lufthansa\f28 \uc2 \f2  said it was checking to what extent its fleet of 11 747-8s was\par affected.\par               \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Related news\par               \par               \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 GENERALI\par               \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Standard & Poor's\f28 \uc2 \f2  has removed Generali from its Credit Watch list after\par ascertaining that Italy's biggest insurer would not exhaust its regulatory\par capital even if its home country were to default on its debt.\par               \par               \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 MEDIASET\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Italy's biggest TV broadcaster \f28 \uc2 \f2 Mediaset\f28 \uc2 \f2  said on Tuesday advertising\par trends for this year were difficult to predict after beating 2013 profit\par expectations thanks to higher-than-expected cost cuts.\par               \par               \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ORPEA\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The retirement homes group said full-year 2013 revenues rose by 12.5 percent\par to 1.608 billion euros ($2.22 billion) on the back of brisk organic growth of\par 7.1 pct and the contribution made by acquisitions, especially in international\par markets.\par               \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 EBITDA earnings before rents (EBITDAR) advanced by 17.1 percent to 433.2\par million euros.\par               \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 With a growth pipeline of 8,339 beds under development and a proven ability\par to generate new projects, the company said it "boasts impressive growth\par potential"\par               \par               \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 EDF, \f28 \uc2 \f2 VEOLIA\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The French utility and the water-and-waste-management company said they had\par agreed how to split up their Dalkia energy-services joint venture after months\par of talks.\par               \par               \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SYNGENTA\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Syngenta AG\f28 \uc2 \f2  said that grain prices have played a role in China's\par rejection of U.S. corn shipments containing an unauthorized, genetically\par modified trait developed by the company.\par               \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 For more, click on\par               \par               \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 UBS\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The broker tug-of-war between \f28 \uc2 \f2 Morgan Stanley\f28 \uc2 \f2  and \f28 \uc2 \f2 UBS\f28 \uc2 \f2  Wealth Americas\par  in Florida continued this month as veteran adviser David Behar jumped\par to \f28 \uc2 \f2 Morgan Stanley\f28 \uc2 \f2  after nine years with \f28 \uc2 \f2 UBS\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\par               \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 For more, click on\par               \par               \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 HANDELSBANKEN\par               \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Swedish banking group said late on Tuesday it and Handelsbanken Pension\par Foundation and Handelsbanken Pension Fund had reorganized their holdings in\par investment group Industrivarden and hygiene group SCA.\par               \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Handelsbanken said as a result of the transactions it controls 10.46 percent\par of votes in Industrivarden and 10.15 percent of votes in SCA. The two companies\par have long had close connections with Handelsbanken and share several board\par members.\par               \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Business daily Dagens Industri said the move was to enable Handelsbanken to\par get round new EU rules on how many board positions bank board members are\par allowed to have.\par               \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Dagens Industri wrote that Handelsbanken's increased stake in the two\par companies could lead investors to see it as an investment firm rather than a\par bank. Investment firms often trade at a discount to the value of their holdings\par and the paper reckoned Handelsbanken shares could fall 3-4 percent on opening.\par               \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 For more on the company, double click on\par               \par               \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A.P. MOLLER-MAERSK\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Danish shipping and oil group is preparing a divestment of offshore\par service company \f28 \uc2 \f2 Esvagt\f28 \uc2 \f2  of which it controls 75 percent, daily Borsen wrote,\par based on a source who has knowledge of the matter.\par               \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Esvagt\f28 \uc2 \f2  operates a fleet of 37 units ranging from anchor handling, tug,\par supply, tanker assist vessels - to oil recovery-, survey- and rescue vessels.\par Sources told the newspaper valuation of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Esvagt\f28 \uc2 \f2  could be between 2 and 3 billion\par Danish crowns ($369 -$554 million).\par               \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A spokeswoman from \f28 \uc2 \f2 A.P. Moller-Maersk\f28 \uc2 \f2  declined to comment in a mail to\par Reuters.\par               \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 For more on the company, double click on\par               \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Reporting by Alistair Smout; Editing by Atul Prakash) \f28 \par \par \uc2 Released: 2014-3-26T08:57:52.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020140326ea3q009hr\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
