\par\par \b \uc2 MORNING BID: EUROPE-A daily note from our Economics/Politics Editor\b0\par\par\uc2 810 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2014\par 08:52\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2014 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 LONDON, March 25 (Reuters) - G7 leaders didn't move the dial far last night, telling Russia it faced more damaging sanctions if it took any further action to destabilize Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 They will also shun Russia's G8 summit in June and meet "\u224\'00\'E0 sept" in Brussels, marking the first time since Moscow joined the group in 1998 that it will have been shut out of the annual summit.\par \par \uc2 There were some other interesting pointers. For one, the G7 agreed their energy ministers would work together to reduce dependence on Russian oil and gas. Could this lead to the United States exporting shale gas to Europe in the long-run?\par \par \uc2 A committee of U.S. lawmakers will hear testimony on Tuesday from those who favour loosening restrictions on gas exports.\par \par \uc2 Sanctions imposed so far may be limited but they are hitting investment and Russia's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and stock market. The economy is barely growing and the government said yesterday it now expected net capital outflows of up to $70 billion in the first quarter of the year.\par \par \uc2 The G7 leaders remain in The Hague for day two of a nuclear security summit and the EU leaders plus Barack Obama will reconvene in Brussels on Wednesday for the annual EU/U.S. summit.\par \par \uc2 But the big moment of the day could come from the IMF which is due to wind up its work in Kiev and is expected to offer an economic reform and aid programme soon.\par \par \uc2 Aid for Kiev will also hurt Putin.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. and EU would row in behind any IMF package, helping Ukraine meet its debt obligations and begin the process of rebuilding. The EU has signed the political elements of an "association agreement" with Kiev, promising closer ties that will help draw it more closely into the heart of Europe.\par \par \uc2 That is precisely what Putin was trying to avoid.\par \par \uc2 Remaining Ukrainian troops are being withdrawn from Crimea, the last step in cementing Russia's control of the region. Russian \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in eastern or southern \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be the trigger for additional sanctions, as would violence in Crimea.\par \par \uc2 Having also been in the Hague, Chinese President \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2  will begin a three-day state visit to France. The bilateral focus will be on business and trade deals. But Xi is also a big player in the Ukraine crisis whether he likes it or not.\par \par \uc2 Beijing has stayed studiously to one side of the Ukraine crisis, as is its wont, and Moscow still has high hopes of a huge natural gas supply deal with China that is apparently close after years of negotiations.\par \par \uc2 China has said sanctions are not the way forward but it abstained on a \uc2 U.N. Security Council\uc2  resolution, which Russia vetoed, that declared the Crimea referendum on joining Russia was invalid. Xi called for a political solution on Monday but did not harden Beijing's position towards Moscow.\par \par \uc2 Germany's Ifo index will be the main event for the macro markets after its March PMI survey showed solid expansion but at a slightly slower rate than in February. The Ifo is expected to hold steadyish at a high level although German business lobbies are starting to warn about the threat to them from tough sanctions on Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Germany's finance ministry\uc2  will release its monthly report and both European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President \uc2 Mario Draghi\uc2  and Bundesbank chief \uc2 Jens Weidmann\uc2  are making speeches.\par \par \uc2 Hungary's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  - full of government appointees - appears not to have sated its appetite for interest rate cuts despite having lowered them month in, month out since late 2012.\par \par \uc2 A Reuters poll of economists found most expect a further 10 basis points reduction today, taking rates down to 2.6 percent although some think it will hold for the first time in over 18 months. Further cuts could increase the risk of a sell-off in Hungarian assets given the U.S. Federal Reserve's steady reduction of its money creation.\par \par \uc2 Nigeria's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , on the other hand, is expected to leave rates at 12 percent today to support its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at its first meeting since President Jonathan suspended \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Lamido Sanusi, a monetary-policy hawk, last month. It could even hike.\par \par \uc2 Arab leaders meet in Kuwait, the first high-level political meeting since Saudi Arabia, the United Arab Emirates and Bahrain withdrew their ambassadors from Qatar in a dispute about Doha's involvement in the region, such as its support for the \uc2 Muslim Brotherhood\uc2  in Egypt.\par \par \uc2 Saudi Arabia designated the \uc2 Muslim Brotherhood\uc2  as a terrorist group shortly afterwards. Kuwait is attempting to mediate.\par \par \uc2 We will also be watching for news of more Gulf aid to Egypt and there are differences over Syrian and Iran too. (Editing by Jon Boyle)\par \par \uc2 Released: 2014-3-28T07:52:34.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020140325ea3p00ayi\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
