\par\par \b \uc2 EUR/USD May Face Pressure After G-7 Agrees to Exclude Russia, Increase Sanctions -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,662 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2014\par 22:54\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 2154 GMT [Dow Jones] The EUR may face pressure in Asia after news leaders of the world's largest economies agreed to effectively remove Russia from the \uc2 Group of Eight\uc2  nations in response to Moscow's military \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The leaders, meeting in emergency session, also agreed that they would begin to exert punitive sanctions on Russia's energy, banking, finance and arms industries unless Moscow dialed back plans to seize the Crimea region from Ukraine. While the EUR/USD gained overnight, the possibility of further sanctions could drag it down, says Stuart Ive, senior client advisor at OM Financial in Wellington. "Anything along those lines may see the EUR dip down, mainly because central Europe receives the majority of its oil supplies from Russia and the euro zone has very strong economic links with Russia. Any sanctions will weigh on the EUR," he says. The EUR/USD is at 1.3836 mid-morning in New Zealand. (rebecca.howard@wsj.com @FarroHoward)\par \par \uc2 2152 GMT [Dow Jones] AUD/USD's rise to a 3 1/2-month high of 0.9150 last night was driven more by U.S. dollar weakness and flows, rather than a "bad news is good news" view that China data is sufficiently weak that it will elicit a strong policy response, says NAB's Co-Head of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Strategy, Ray Attrill. "Flows-wise, we note that repatriation flows by local asset managers look to be offering support for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at present, alongside ongoing offshore asset manager interest in Australian assets, and suspected corporate demand," he adds. Focus turns to a speech by RBA deputy governor Phil Lowe at an ASIC forum at 0445 GMT, followed by German IFO business conditions data, U.S. data on new home sales, house prices, consumer confidence and the \uc2 Richmond Fed\uc2 's manufacturing survey overnight. On a closing basis, AUD/USD remains below potentially strong resistance from its 200-day moving average at 0.9138. Any negative catalysts could trigger a dip toward uptrend line support near 0.9000 on a technical basis. AUD/USD last 0.9133. (david.rogers@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 17:31 EDT - BMO Capital says real housing prices in Canada since 1980 have averaged 2.7% growth a year, "amazingly close" to average real GDP growth over that time. By one measure, Toronto's great real estate bubble of the '80s hit its apex 25 years ago, BMO says. Adjusted for inflation and seasonality, average home prices topped out in February '89, and didn't bottom out until more than seven years later with an after-inflation drop of 38%. "But, notably, even a first-time buyer at the very peak of that episode has still seen an annualized price gain of nearly 1% after inflation," BMO says. "Real prices more than doubled in a four-year period from 1985 to 1989, while it took 18 years to double this time," firm adds. (don.curren@wsj.com @dbcurren)\par \par \uc2 2112 GMT [Dow Jones] Asian stock markets may open slightly lower after some profit-taking in U.S. markets, Philip Hunter, a broker at First NZ Capital in Wellington, says. "There has been some weakness in stocks that have run pretty hard and we might see some consolidation in Asian markets," he says. He says sentiment may also still be weighed by weaker-than-expected China data from Monday. In New Zealand, the NZX-50 is down 0.4% at 5100.886 early but Hunter expects light trading due to a dearth of drivers. Investors will be watching for results from Fonterra Cooperative Group (FCG.NZ) and Synlait (SML.NZ) later in the week. (rebecca.howard@wsj.com @FarroHoward)\par \par \uc2 17:00 EDT - The latest position reports show US dealers' Treasurys inventory up a slight $4.3B for the week ended March 12. That was the same week Treasurys held at the NY Fed for foreign accounts dropped a record $105B. The lack of pick-up in dealer inventory is yet another sign that the drop in custody holdings did not represent selling. Instead, it corroborates what many believe is a government -- perhaps Russia -- deciding to shift it Treasurys holdings to a custodian outside the US. One popular custodian destination lately has been Belgium, as suggested in recent TIC data. (cynthia.lin@wsj.com; @cynthialin_dj)\par \par \uc2 1942 GMT [Dow Jones] The AUD/USD's "remarkable buoyancy"--the pair gained strongly overnight despite a disappointing data release out of China--is attributable to investors believing the Chinese data tone has become negative enough to warrant a policy response from Beijing, BNZ \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Strategist Raiko Shareef says. "This 'bad news is good' dynamic for risk assets harks back to days of crises past," he says. Scheduled remarks from RBA Deputy Governor Phil Lowe in Hong Kong may push the pair around as "with the AUD/USD bumping up against its 200-day moving average, the RBA will no doubt be tempted to talk it lower." The pair is at 0.9128 early in New Zealand. (rebecca.howard@wsj.com @FarroHoward)\par \par \uc2 1927 GMT [Dow Jones] The NZD/USD remains well supported early in New Zealand trading as markets digest weaker-than-expected HSBC China PMI, German PMI and the U.S. Markit PMI, say \uc2 ANZ Bank\uc2 . The pair is at 0.8538. "Domestic factors will ensure NZD/USD will remain strong on slow global growth," it says. The USD was also weighed after \uc2 San Francisco Federal Reserve Bank\uc2  President John Williams, in an interview with the Washington Post posted on the newspaper's website Monday, said last week's Fed statement--seen by some as signaling a more hawkish Fed--didn't constitute a policy shift. Looking ahead, markets will now focus on U.S. consumer confidence and the \uc2 Richmond Fed\uc2  to provide direction later in the global trading day. \uc2 ANZ Bank\uc2  tips a short-term range of 0.8520 to 0.8620 for the NZD/USD. (rebecca.howard@wsj.com @FarroHoward)\par \par \uc2 15:20 EDT - The Australian Dollar strengthened against the US dollar despite discouraging factory numbers from China, touching $0.9151 at one point, its highest since December 11. As an export-driven economy that relies heavily on China, Australia is affected by a slowdown in demand from its trading partner. Yet, traders say today's weak manufacturing gauge actually fueled expectations that China's policymakers may opt to stimulate the economy sooner. That turned around to boost AUD, lifting it above its 200-day moving average at $0.9139. AUD/USD last up 0.5% at $0.9127. (cynthia.lin@wsj.com; @cynthialin_dj)\par \par \uc2 13:35 EDT - Economists at the \uc2 San Francisco Fed\uc2  find potential losses incurred by the Fed due to rising rates are "very likely to be manageable." The team bases its analysis on the $1.6T of Treasury securities owned by the Fed at the end of 2012, accounting for nearly 60% of the Fed's portfolio then. "The likelihood that the Treasury portfolio's value will fall below its face value and incur a financial loss by the end of 2015 is less than 5%," they say. As for the income the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  remits to the Treasury, the study finds only a 5% chance the Fed will have no investment income to pass to the agency from 2016-8. (cynthia.lin@wsj.com; @cynthialin_dj)\par \par \uc2 1307 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2 's economists are gloomier than most about this week's major U.S. data releases, and, notably, have below-consensus forecasrts for consumer confidence, durable goods orders and consumption. However, they say, if they're proven correct, they'd still enter fresh long dollar trades should the greenback slip. They think Fed Chair Yellen's comments last week suggest the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is still willing to look through weak, weather-hit data and also that front-end rates will continue rising, supporting the dollar. USD/JPY now at 102.45, EUR/USD 1.3770.gary.stride@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 1243 GMT [Dow Jones] Following a visit by its analysts to the country, \uc2 HSBC\uc2  forecasts a 15bps cut in Hungarian rates Tuesday, taking the policy rate down to another all-time low of 2.55%, deeper than the consensus forecast for 2.60%. \uc2 HSBC\uc2  lowers its forecast for the bottom of the Hungarian easing cycle to 2.45% from its earlier view of 2.75%. Economist Agata Urbanska-Giner reckons the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may want to "bring interest rates on new HUF mortgages below the level of the outstanding CHF mortgages. This could contribute towards the outstanding government target to eliminate foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  mortgages." Revises December CPI forecast downward to 2% on the year, from 3%, and sees 2015 CPI at 2.6%, still below Hungary's 3% inflation target. Expects a gradual, "orderly" tightening of interest rates in 2015, in line with a narrowing negative output gap. The interest rate decision is due at 1300 GMT Tuesday. (margit.feher@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 8:40 EDT - US Treasurys brush off a disappointing China manufacturing report, with prices drifting lower overnight. Ten-year notes off 7/32 to yield 2.77% as US trading gets underway. Focus in the bond market remains on the timing of the Fed's rate hike, putting the most pressure on middle-dated maturities. "The perceived path of rates in 2016 and beyond has led to a recalibration of expectations in 3- to 7-year sectors in particular," CRT says. That's ironing out the tail-end of the yield curve, with the gap between 5- and 30-year yields at 1.866%, its lowest since October 2009. (cynthia.lin@wsj.com; @cynthialin_dj)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 March 24, 2014 17:54 ET (21:54 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140324ea3o003io\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
