\par\par \b \uc2 Offshore Russian stocks attract premium in jittery markets\b0\par\par\uc2 By Carolyn Cohn   \par \uc2 498 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2014\par 17:49\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2014 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 LONDON, March 19 (Reuters) - Offshore Russian stocks are trading at a high premium to onshore stocks, brokers and analysts said on Wednesday, as traders worry about access to domestic Russian markets.\par \par \uc2 The higher price for offshore stocks - global (GDR) or American (ADR) depositary receipts - compared with the locally listed stocks mirrors the premium investors have been paying for offshore derivative contracts in the rouble \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 With the possibility of increased sanctions on Russia and weakness in the country's economy due to tensions with the west over Crimea, market participants fear Russia could shut down foreign access to markets and make it harder to remove roubles from the country.\par \par \uc2 This concern has seen rouble-listed stocks in Moscow trade at a discount of as much as 20 percent to the GDR/ADR market.\par \par \uc2 "There is a dislocation in the market, it's not normal," said Joseph Dayan, managing director of brokerage BCS Financial Group. "It's to do with the geopolitical issues."\par \par \uc2 GDRs are denominated in hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as dollars or euros and usually entitle the holder to several shares in a company. There is a large market for Russian GDR/ADRs listed in London or New York and sold independently of the local shares.\par \par \uc2 The GDRs have traditionally traded at a premium to onshore stocks, investors say, due to the relative ease of access to offshore markets. But reform of Russia's local markets has seen that premium shrink in recent years.\par \par \uc2 President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 's assertions of Russia's rights to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2  and make Crimea part of Russia are expected to cause net capital outflows in the first quarter to $50-$70 billion, compared with $63 billion for the whole of 2013.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will not impose capital controls to defend the rouble, the bank told Reuters this week.\par \par \uc2 But there is still longer-term uncertainty, investors said.\par \par \uc2 "People have generally taken that at face value, but the situation is fluid, that stance could change if other factors change," said Collin Crownover, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  management at State Street Global Advisers.\par \par \uc2 Sberbank\uc2 's stock in Moscow normally trades at the same price as the London-listed GDR and is currently trading at a 3 percent discount. Traders said that discount deepened to as much as 7 percent a week ago, before Crimea voted to join Russia.\par \par \uc2 Investors have become a little less anxious, however, after reassurances by President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  this week that Russia does not need further division of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 The price discrepancies between the two markets could make the local stocks a buy, analysts at Bank of America-Merrill Lynch said in a note:\par \par \uc2 "Existing and rising spreads between local (stocks) and GDR/ADRs may offer considerable opportunities if there is... stabilisation in the political situation." (Additional reporting by Jason Bush in Moscow and Neal Kimberley in London; Editing by Hugh Lawson)\par \par \uc2 Released: 2014-3-22T16:49:03.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020140319ea3j00tbl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
