\par\par \b \uc2 European Morning Briefing: Markets on Pause Before Fed's Verdict\b0\par\par\uc2 4,363 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2014\par 06:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SNAPSHOT:\par \par \uc2 ========\par \par \uc2 European bourses, bunds and gilts seen mixed; euro, oil lower, spot gold higher\par \par \uc2 -Japan's Trade Gap Narrows\par \par \uc2 -Speedy Traders Face U.S. Scrutiny\par \par \uc2 -BOE's Carney: Welcome Move By China to Widen Yuan Trading Band\par \par \uc2 -BOE's Carney: U.K. Economy to be Buffeted by Global Forces\par \par \uc2 -White House Spokesman's Advice: Don't Invest in Russian Stocks\par \par \uc2 -MPS Foundation Launches Sale of Monte dei Paschi Stake\par \par \uc2 -Insurer Allianz Pays Compensation to \uc2 Malaysia Airlines\par \par \uc2 -\uc2 Oracle\uc2  Profit Rises, but Disappoints Wall Street\par \par \uc2 WATCH FOR: U.K. unemployment data, Bank of England meeting minutes, Chancellor Osborne's budget statement; U.S. oil data, Federal Reserve decisions\par \par \uc2 EUROPEAN OUTLOOK & U.S./ASIAN SUMMARIES:\par \par \uc2 ========\par \par \uc2 European stock markets, along with bunds and gilts, may start mixed as attention shifts to the U.S. Federal Reserve's meeting announcements, followed by a press conference by Fed Chairwoman Janet Yellen.\par \par \uc2 The Fed is expected to announce a further cut of 10 billion dollars per month in its bond-buying program. But despite jitters in stock markets last week and some weather-affected economic data, investors doubt the Fed will shift from that path.\par \par \uc2 Investors will also continue to keep an eye on Ukraine and on Russia's reactions to sanctions imposed by the U.S. and the EU. Although investors were relieved after President Vladimir Putin addressed the Russian parliament Tuesday and indicated he had no further designs on Ukraine territories, any signs that tensions are escalating will put a damper on risk appetite.\par \par \uc2 Chinese stocks moved lower, as property developers continued to weigh on the market, while the rest of Asia was softer ahead of the conclusion of Federal Reserve's policy meeting.\par \par \uc2 Wall Street futures were lower, with Treasurys nudging higher, as the euro and oil trader slightly lower, and spot gold higher.\par \par \uc2 STOCKS:\par \par \uc2 ======\par \par \uc2 Investors will see a mixed European opening with the focus on the Federal Reserve after Russian President Vladimir Putin turned down the volume a bit.\par \par \uc2 Ahead of the opening, IG has called the FTSE down 5 at 6600, the DAX flat at 9242, and the CAC off 8 at 4305.\par \par \uc2 "Putin's speech didn't indicate he is looking for an escalation of the crisis. All the focus was on Crimea rather than any further areas of Ukraine," said Guillaume Tresca, emerging-market strategist at \uc2 Cr\u233\'00\'E9dit Agricole\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 With Mr. Putin largely steering clear of inflammatory rhetoric in response to the sanctions, markets are likely to turn their immediate focus to other issues, including the U.S. Federal Reserve meeting, Mr. Tresca said.\par \par \uc2 Even so, investors remain nervous.\par \par \uc2 "I don't really see this as a de-escalation. The situation is still a very tense one," said Fran\u231\'00\'E7ois Savary, who helps oversee roughly $10 billion of assets as chief investment officer at Swiss bank Reyl.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Savary thinks European stocks have the potential to rise further as the economy improves, but avoids buying while the Ukraine situation remains so uncertain. Reyl reduced its exposure to stocks last week, he said.\par \par \uc2 U.S. stock futures were lower Wednesday after markets rallied for a second straight session Tuesday, bringing the S&P 500 within a fraction of another record.\par \par \uc2 Traders said concerns over Russia's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2  remain in the background.\par \par \uc2 Jim Russell, senior equity strategist at U.S. Bank Wealth Management, which oversees about $115 billion, said he has seem some flows into domestic equity mutual funds. Those flows may not be heavy, but given the light volumes and a lack of selling, it may be having an "outsize impact on the market," Mr. Russell said.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 :\par \par \uc2 ======\par \par \uc2 The euro was lower in Asia as traders wait to compare the Federal Reserve's stance against that of the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The resilience of the euro is impressive, noted Brian Martin, senior European strategist at ANZ. The ability to engineer a "permanent" depreciation in the euro is difficult at present, Martin said. "We feel that current statements on the exchange rate are aimed more at trying to slow the euro's rise rather than reverse it," Martin said.\par \par \uc2 The dollar fell against the yen amid soft sentiment toward the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "In general, markets are content to wait for the FOMC before making a move," said Brad Bechtel, managing director at Faros Trading.\par \par \uc2 The Federal Open Market Committee is expected to keep interest rates unchanged and to announce an additional $10 billion monthly reduction to its bond purchases. Investors also expect confirmation that the recent soft patch in the U.S. economy has been a temporary setback caused by extreme winter weather.\par \par \uc2 "All of that would be positive for the dollar," said Omer Esiner, chief market analyst at \uc2 Commonwealth \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Inc\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 BONDS:\par \par \uc2 =====\par \par \uc2 Bunds may start mixed with a lack of focal points in the euro zone, leaving investors to spotlight the Federal Reserve's meeting in the presumed absence of a flare-up in the Ukraine situation.\par \par \uc2 In the U.K., gilts may also start mixed with unemployment data on tap. The unemployment rate is expected to remain steady at 7.2% while another sharp fall in monthly jobless claims is expected of 25,000, noted Michael Hewson, chief market analyst at CMC Markets UK.\par \par \uc2 The U.K. will also issue its budget statement. Chancellor George Osborne has said it will keep a strong focus on austerity but will also include measures to support business investment and exports, long-term soft-spots in the economy.\par \par \uc2 Treasury prices were slightly higher Wednesday, after markets traded slightly higher as investors awaited the Fed's monetary-policy decision.\par \par \uc2 Prices eased from prior highs after Mr. Putin said Moscow has no intentions to partition Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, the Fed will announce its policy decision and Ms. Yellen will hold a news conference.\par \par \uc2 "The market is put in a range now as they are waiting for the decision Yellen will make at the FOMC," said Millan L. Mulraine, deputy head of U.S. research and strategy at \uc2 TD Securities\uc2 . "This is a new Fed with a new slate of players. As an investor, you wouldn't want necessarily to prejudge that until you have more clarity on where Yellen wants to take the Fed."\par \par \uc2 The Fed's decision could add pressure on short-term yields by pushing the market's expectation for the firs rate increase further out. Therefore, the Treasury yield curve will steepen.\par \par \uc2 Some investors said there is a possibility that Ms. Yellen takes a more pro-growth approach at the meeting, for example, by reducing the pace of tapering.\par \par \uc2 "Just looking from the domestic perspective, I wouldn't rule that out," said Robert Tipp, chief investment strategist at Prudential Fixed Income, referring to the recent softness in economic data.\par \par \uc2 In Japan Wednesday, government bonds edged higher ahead of the Fed.\par \par \uc2 ENERGY:\par \par \uc2 =======\par \par \uc2 Crude-oil futures drifted lower in Asia after settling higher Tuesday on reports of the Seaway pipeline expanding capacity by May-June. "U.S. inflation figures, which were broadly in line with expectations and positive housing data, helped WTI close the Brent discount to $7/bbl," ANZ said. "Putin's signing of a treaty to annex Crimea also supported oil markets a little. However, the general consensus is that neither Russia nor Europe can afford to cut energy ties," the bank noted. The closely watched survey from the U.S. EIA is due later Wednesday. U.S. oil inventories are projected to have increased by 2.3 million barrels, on average, in the week ended March 14, according to a Wall Street Journal survey. The API said late Tuesday that its own data for last week showed crude stocks rose by 5.9 million barrels.\par \par \uc2 Nymex crude was down 28 cents at $99.42/bbl, and Brent crude was down 19 cents at $106.60/bbl.\par \par \uc2 METALS:\par \par \uc2 ======\par \par \uc2 Gold edged higher in Asia with attention remaining fixed on the outcome of the Fed's meeting. With the geopolitical issue in Ukraine returning to a "high simmer from last Friday's boil" the temperature has lowered enough to take the edge off the fear, Peter Hug, global trading director at Kitco Metals, said in a report. "Metals tend to be an advance trigger trade against upcoming events, but many times after the perception becomes reality, the hedge wanes because the event can be quantified," he said.\par \par \uc2 Spot gold was at $1,357.90/oz, up $2.40.\par \par \uc2 Copper looks set to remain rangebound as traders reassess risks in China after news Chinese property developer Zhejiang Xingrun was unable to repay almost $600 million in loans. "The knock-on impact stemming from China, allowing various small privately held companies to default, is hard to gauge, but will likely see a re-appraisal of risk by Chinese investors and banks," Leon Westgate, an analyst with Standard Bank, said in a report.\par \par \uc2 LME 3-month copper was little changed at $6,483/ton.\par \par \uc2 Write to Dennis Baker at dennis.baker@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 19 Mar 2014 01:00 EDT  European Morning Briefing: DJ Dominant Headlines; Other Top Stories\par \par \uc2 DJ Dominant Headlines:\par \par \uc2 High-Speed Trading Firms Face New U.S. Scrutiny\par \par \uc2 How Putin Parried Obama's Overtures on Crimea\par \par \uc2 Hillary Clinton's Circle Isn't Sure She Should Run for President in 2016\par \par \uc2 Airline Stocks Are Looking Up Again\par \par \uc2 Top Australia Lawmaker Vows to Stay On Amid Corruption Probe\par \par \uc2 Other Top Stories:\par \par \uc2 Acer Spokesman Detained in Insider Trading Probe\par \par \uc2 Prosecutors have detained \uc2 Acer Inc\uc2 .'s longtime spokesman \uc2 Henry Wang\uc2  as part of an investigation into alleged insider trading of shares of the Taiwanese personal-computer maker, the New Taipei District Office said Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 Japan's Trade Gap Narrows\par \par \uc2 Japan's trade deficit in February narrowed sharply from the previous month's record high, as exports regained some momentum following a slump during the Lunar New Year holiday, while growth in imports showed signs of slowing.\par \par \uc2 U.S. to Announce Toyota Settlement\par \par \uc2 The Justice Department is expected to announce a settlement with \uc2 Toyota Motor Corp\uc2 . as early as Wednesday that could cost the auto maker more than $1 billion to end a criminal probe into its disclosure of safety issues, people familiar with the matter said.\par \par \uc2 BOJ Kiuchi Warns Against Risk of Side Effects From Additional Easing\par \par \uc2 A \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  policy board member expressed caution Wednesday about the bank continuing its large-scale easing campaign indefinitely or taking further steps, warning that those actions risk causing unintended effects in markets and destabilizing the economy.\par \par \uc2 Japan Display Comeback Marred by Weak IPO\par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, \uc2 Japan Display Inc\uc2 .--which is now the world's No. 1 maker of smartphone screens and a key supplier to \uc2 Apple Inc\uc2 .-- engineered a comeback, listing its shares in a $3.1 billion initial public offering. But its turnaroun story was overshadowed as shares opened 15% below its IPO price.\par \par \uc2 Stockland Buys Australand Stake From Singapore's \uc2 CapitaLand\par \par \uc2 Stockland bought a 20% interest in developer \uc2 Australand Property Group\uc2  from Singapore's \uc2 CapitaLand Ltd\uc2 . for around $397.5 million.\par \par \uc2 19 Mar 2014 01:00 EDT  European Morning Briefing: Market Prices, Data Calendar\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par DJIA    16336.19 +88.97 +0.55% \par Nasdaq   4333.31 +53.36 +1.25% \par S&P 500  1872.25 +13.42 +0.72% \par FTSE 100 6605.28 +36.93 +0.56% \par DAX      9242.55 +61.66 +0.67% \par CAC40    4313.26 +41.30 +0.97% \par Closing \par  \par Dow Future   16247.00 -15.00 -0.1% \par NASDAQ Future 3692.75  -2.25 -0.1% \par S&P Future    1861.80  -2.00 -0.1% \par As of 0430 GMT \par  \par Nikkei       14338.72 -72.55 -0.50% \par Hang Seng    21549.49 -34.01 -0.16% \par S&P/ASX 200   5348.30  +3.70 +0.07% \par MumbaiSensex 21801.34 -31.27 -0.14% \par Kospi         1937.90  -2.31 -0.12% \par ShanghaiComp  2006.20 -18.99 -0.94% \par Taipei        8698.14 -33.80 -0.39% \par As of 0425 GMT \par  \par USD/JPY 101.30-36 -0.10% \par Range   101.48 101.30 \par EUR/USD 1.3919-24 -0.09% \par Range   1.3934 1.3924 \par GBP/USD 1.6595-98 +0.02% \par Range   1.6603 1.6584 \par USD/CHF 0.8735-38 +0.06% \par Range   0.8738 0.8730 \par AUD/USD 0.9113-16 -0.13% \par Range   0.9137 0.9110 \par EUR/JPY 141.07-12 -0.18% \par Range   141.36 141.08 \par EUR/GBP 0.8389-90 -0.11% \par Range   0.8400 0.8389 \par EUR/CHF 1.2162-68 -0.02% \par Range   1.2168 1.2162 \par As of 0350 GMT vs. NY close \par  \par USD/JPY Vol 