\par\par \b \uc2 Indian Morning Briefing: Asian Markets Lower Ahead of Fed Review\b0\par\par\uc2 1,061 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2014\par 03:59\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par GLOBAL MARKETS \par               Latest   Change   %Change \par DJIA         16336.19  +88.97 +0.55% \par Nasdaq        4333.31  +53.36 +1.25% \par S&P 500       1872.25  +13.42 +0.72% \par FTSE 100      6605.28  +36.93 +0.56% \par Nikkei Stock 14361.60  -49.67 -0.34% \par Hang Seng    21543.44  -40.06 -0.19% \par Kospi         1940.80   +0.59 +0.03% \par SGX Nifty     6580.00  +20.50 +0.31% \par March 2014 contract \par  \par           Latest   %Change \par USD/JPY 101.33-36  -0.09% \par Range   101.48-101.34 \par EUR/USD 1.3931-34  -0.01% \par Range   1.3934-1.3928 \par  \par CBOT Wheat May 2014   $6.924 per bushel (Settlement Price) \par                   Last   Change \par Comex Gold       $1,359    -$13.90 \par Comex Gold Range $1,351.10-$1,362.70 \par Nymex Crude (NY)   $99.62   +$1.54 \par  \par  \par U.S. STOCKS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. stocks rallied for a second straight session, bringing the S&P 500 within a fraction of another record high.\f28 \par \par \uc2 Traders say there was no single piece of news driving the market higher. They say concerns over Russia's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2  remain in the background, but investors believe over the long term, it will end up as a nonevent for the market. If recent upbeat economic data were providing the boost, investors would expect a rise in bond yields, but yields fell on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 And with volumes light, they aren't seeing a big increase in buying.\par \par \uc2 Clark Yingst, chief market analyst at New York brokerage firm Joseph Gunnar, said he couldn't find a fundamental reason for the magnitude of this week's gains. "It just looks like it is another quiet day," Mr. Yingst said.\par \par \uc2 In corporate news, \uc2 Hertz Global Holdings\uc2  slipped as the company's plan to spin off its equipment-rental business was offset by fourth-quarter earnings and revenue that missed expectations. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par ASIAN STOCKS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Chinese stocks moved lower, as property developers continued to weigh on the market, while the rest of Asia was softer ahead of the conclusion of Federal Reserve's policy meeting.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The largest movements were in China, where the Shanghai Composite lost 0.7% and Hong Kong's Hang Seng Index fell 0.1%. Local sentiment continued to be weighed by concerns over the property sector--a key driver of the Chinese economy that accounts for 16% of gross domestic product. So far this week, data showed that growth continued to moderate in property developers, while a real-estate firm in eastern China was unable to pay almost $600 million in debt.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "There is too much negative news in the property industry," said Amy Lin, an analyst at Capital Securities. "Reports that housing prices are diving in major cities are spreading through the Internet, which has severely weighed down sentiment."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Shares in developers fell for a second day, with \f28 \uc2 \f2 China Vanke Co\f28 \uc2 \f2 . down 3.8% in Shenzhen and \f28 \uc2 \f2 China Resources Land\f28 \uc2 \f2  2.3% lower in Hong Kong.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 More broadly, the region was cautious ahead of the conclusion of the Federal Reserve's policy meeting later Wednesday. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is expected to reduce its bond-buying program by another $10 billion and change its 6.5% unemployment threshold to keep its fund rate near zero into 2015.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's Nikkei fell 0.3%, giving up its earlier gains as the yen strengthened against the dollar.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Elsewhere, Australia's S&P/ASX 200 was flat and South Korea's Kospi was up less than 0.1%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The region failed to take a positive lead from the U.S., where the S&P 500 ended the day close to a record as fears of escalating tensions over Crimea abated. Russian President Vladimir Putin Tuesday signed a treaty to formally annex Ukraine's Crimea region and said that while Crimea must be under Russian sovereignty, "we don't want a partition of Ukraine." Over the weekend, Crimeans taking part in a referendum voted overwhelmingly in favor of joining Russia.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In corporate news, shares in \f28 \uc2 \f2 Japan Display Inc\f28 \uc2 \f2 . slumped 15% on debut trading in Tokyo. The world's largest manufacturer of screen for smartphones and tablets raised as much as $3.2 billion earlier this month in a deal that was priced at the bottom of the proposed range.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan Display's poor start came just one day after shares in \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hitachi Maxell Ltd\f28 \uc2 \f2 . sank 5% below its IPO price on its debut trading Tuesday. The performance for these companies suggests that demand for new listings in Japan might not be strong, and that could bode badly for railway and hotel operator \f28 \uc2 \f2 Seibu Holdings Inc\f28 \uc2 \f2 ., which has a listing next month that could raise as much as $4.9 billion. \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 The yen strengthened against the dollar. The dollar was last at Y101.32 compared with Y101.43 late Tuesday in New York. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par METALS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Nickel closed at is highest price in almost a year on the London Metal Exchange Tuesday, after moving into a bull market amid persistent concerns over supply of the metal to the market. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par OIL SUMMARY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Oil futures strengthened Tuesday after a pipeline company said it would double the capacity of a pipeline connecting Oklahoma storage with Gulf Coast refineries sooner than previously expected. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par TOP HEADLINES \par Japan's Trade Gap Narrows \par U.S. to Announce Deal With Toyota \par Canadian Finance Minister Resigns \par High-Speed Trading Firms Face New U.S. Scrutiny \par Stockland Buys Australand Stake From Singapore's \f28 \uc2 \f2 CapitaLand\f28 \uc2 \f2  \par NYSE Said to Be Front-runner for Alibaba Listing \par Merger Talks Between J. Crew, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fast Retailing\f28 \uc2 \f2  Break Down \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Wells Fargo\f28 \uc2 \f2  Chief Gets $19.3 Million Pay Package \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Sony\f28 \uc2 \f2  Pictures To Cut 216 Jobs \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Oracle\f28 \uc2 \f2  Reports 2.4% Rise in Profit \par Hillary Clinton's Circle Isn't Sure She Should Run for President in 2016 \par Top Australia Lawmaker Vows to Stay On Amid Corruption Probe \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Steel Dynamics\f28 \uc2 \f2  Forecasts Surprise Drop in 1st-Quarter Profit \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Adobe\f28 \uc2 \f2  First-Quarter Net Income Falls \par Putin Signs Treaty to Annex Crimea \par Activists Win Vote to Topple Board of CommonWealth REIT, Sources Say \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Mercury Systems\f28 \uc2 \f2  on Auction Block -- Sources \par New York Stock Exchange is Front-runner to Land Prized Alibaba Listing -- Sources \par CFTC is Expected to Again Delay Planned Overseas Derivatives Trading \par Yuan Extends Slide Against Dollar \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 March 18, 2014 22:59 ET (02:59 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140319ea3j000c9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
