\par\par \b \uc2 Russia capital flight $45-50 bln this year, full year may be $130 bln -Goldman\b0\par\par\uc2 483 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2014\par 14:50\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2014 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 LONDON, March 13 (Reuters) - Capital flight from Russia has risen sharply since the start of this year to $45-$50 billion, \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  said on Thursday, predicting full-year outflows could be as much as $130 billion, or double 2013 levels.\par \par \uc2 Goldman says its calculations show capital outflows have jumped 60 percent from year-ago levels as the economy slows and the threat of Western sanctions bites. It also slashed its forecast for Russian economic growth this year to 1 percent.\par \par \uc2 Western powers are considering imposing sanctions on Moscow in response to its military \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in neighbouring \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2 , especially if the Crimea region votes this weekend in a referendum to secede from Ukraine and join Russia.\par \par \uc2 "The Achilles heel of the Russian economy remains the flow abroad of Russian capital following any shock. We would also think that any sanctions or even the threat of sanctions will be ultimately targeted at these flows," Goldman analysts Clemens Grafe and Andrew Matheny said in a note.\par \par \uc2 "Most of these flows, in our view, are driven by Russian capital outflows rather than lower foreign inflows."\par \par \uc2 Grafe and Matheny calculate that total net private capital outflows amounted to $45-50 billion in the year to date, or more than a 10th of Russia's economic output in the first 2014 quarter. The outflows also represent a big jump over the $28 billion that left the country in the first three months of 2013, they said.\par \par \uc2 Their models indicate total capital outflows for the year of $130 billion.\par \par \uc2 If confirmed, the data indicates growing pressure on the Russian economy which is already showing its slowest growth since 2008. Official data shows capital flight was $62.7 billion in 2013, with outflows totalling $420 billion since 2008.\par \par \uc2 Goldman based its capital flight calculations on estimates of a $25 billion current account surplus in the first three months of the year and around $30 billion in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "If we are right in our estimate of net private capital flows, pressure on the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves will continue all year," the analysts said.\par \par \uc2 Goldman also is the latest investment bank to cut economic growth forecasts, predicting the economy will grow 1 percent this year. Citi this week also cut its 2013 growth estimates to 1 percent.\par \par \uc2 "Recent events including an even sharper than we had forecasted depreciation in the rouble, an even more aggressive reduction in public sector wage growth and, most importantly, a sharp shock to confidence on the back of the heightened political tensions ... require a significant change in our forecasts," Goldman told clients.\par \par \uc2 They added however that given the fluidity of the Ukraine situation and possible policy responses, the margin of errors in all the forecasts remained large.\par \par \uc2 Released: 2014-3-16T13:50:44.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020140313ea3d00ob2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
