\par\par \b \uc2 PREVIEW-Ukraine tensions to keep \uc2 Russian  \highlight22\uc2 central bank \highlight\uc2  on guard\b0\par\par\uc2 By Oksana Kobzeva and Lidia Kelly   \par \uc2 676 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2014\par 07:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2014 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 MOSCOW, March 12 (Reuters) - \uc2 Russia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will probably keep key policy rates on hold at its regular meeting on Friday, economists say, but any escalation of tensions over Ukraine could prompt a further tightening in monetary policy.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Russian \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has had to hike interest rates to defend the rouble and stem capital flight since President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  said he had the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to protect the rights of ethnic Russians in \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Russian forces' seizure of Crimea has been bloodless so far, but it has cost Moscow dearly. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has spent at least $13 billion on defending the rouble and Russian equities have tumbled, knocking $60 billion off the MICEX index.\par \par \uc2 The tightening of the one-week repo rate, by 150 basis points to 7 percent, interrupts the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's long-promised shift towards inflation targeting and risks tipping the Russian economy into recession in order to ensure temporary financial market stability.\par \par \uc2 Sunday's referendum in Crimea, on whether the region should join Russia, carries additional risks of a further rate hike.\par \par \uc2 But "if the situation remains more or less stable, like now, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is unlikely to change anything (rates)," said Dmitry Polevoy, an economist at ING Bank.\par \par \uc2 The risk of the West imposing sanctions or of Crimea voting in favour of joining Russia are likely to encourage investors to ditch Russian assets, with economists predicting as much as $100-$150 billion in capital will flee Russia this year.\par \par \uc2 "For the time being, we think monetary policy will be under manual control, dominated by the need to stabilise the rouble," Jacob Nell and Alina Slyusarchuk, economists at \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2 , wrote in a note.\par \par \uc2 "If tensions escalate, and foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  demand rises again, more tightening is possible. If tensions ease, we think the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will initially hold rates, but increase the rouble's flexibility again."\par \par \uc2 Just a couple of weeks ago, analysts saw the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  keeping its monetary policy unchanged until the fourth quarter, worrying about risks to inflation.\par \par \uc2 VICTIMS OF FINANCIAL MARKET STABILITY\par \par \uc2 Panic on the market, forcing repeated \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  and rate rises, has shunted worries about the stagnating economy to the sidelines.\par \par \uc2 Although many economists say the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had no option but to raise borrowing costs and douse the rouble's volatility, such moves are doomed to backfire.\par \par \uc2 "Raising (benchmark) rates can translate into increased lending rates, which increases the risks of recession," said Alexander Morozov, chief economist for Russia at \uc2 HSBC\uc2  in Moscow.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's latest forecast from mid-February envisages Russia's gross domestic product (GDP) expanding by 1.5-1.8 percent this year.\par \par \uc2 "In the first and second quarter we can easily see negative GDP data," said Vladimir Kolychev, an economist at VTB Capital, who reckons the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will spend the next three months defending the rouble before tensions ease.\par \par \uc2 Ivan Tchakarov, an economist at Citi in Moscow, cut his GDP forecast for this year to 1 percent from previous 2.6 percent.\par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was right, in our view, to hike rates ... but this will also come at the cost of stronger headwinds to growth even if the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  fully unwinds the hike by year-end," he said in a note.\par \par \uc2 A weaker rouble will push up the prices of imported goods, alarming Putin's inflation-conscious electorate.\par \par \uc2 "It makes the goal of 5 percent (inflation at the end of the year) difficult to achieve," \uc2 HSBC\uc2 's Morozov said.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  says recent moves to defend the rouble have not changed its commitment to shifting its chief monetary tool to inflation targeting and away from controlling the exchange rate by 2015.\par \par \uc2 But economists are not so sure.\par \par \uc2 "If Russia annexes Crimea ... then we can forget about inflation targeting," ING's Polevoy said. (Reporting by Oksana Kobzeva and Lidia Kelly; Writing by Lidia Kelly; Editing by Ruth Pitchford) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2014-3-12T07:00:00.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020140312ea3c0086j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
