\par\par \b \uc2 Australian Equities Roundup -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 2,232 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2014\par 04:59\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 0502 - The Australian dollar is riding positive economic data to its highest level against the greenback since December 11, and it's pulling the Kiwi along with it. The Aussie is up 1.3% against the U.S. dollar, to US$0.9101. The New Zealand dollar is up 0.9% to US$0.8489, its highest level since October 23. Australia's consensus-beating retail sales number, combined with a jump in its trade surplus, provided fuel for liftoff. In addition, a more stable yuan, a weaker yen and easing tensions in Ukraine have all boosted the Aussie, says Marc Chandler, global head of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  at \uc2 Brown Brothers Harriman\uc2 . (james.ramage@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0617 - Markets will be closely watching Governor Glenn Stevens appearance before parliament's committee on economics as "he is unlikely to be particularly happy about last night's surge in the AUD, especially since the latest policy statement brought back references to the AUD being too 'high,'" says BNZ \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Strategist Raiko Shareef. The AUD/USD continued to benefit from strong retail sales and trade data and is at 0.9098 early in New Zealand versus 0.9014 late Thursday in Australia. A stronger-than-expected payrolls number from the U.S. late in the global trading day would "engender some significant retracement" in \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  like the AUD, says Shareef. (rebecca.howard@wsj.com @FarroHoward)\par \par \uc2 0845 - Australia's S&P/ASX 200 is poised for a slight opening rise after the S&P 500 crept up 0.2% in a "risk on" environment after the ECB raised its GDP growth forecasts. However, BHP's (BHP.AU) ADR equivalent close at A$37.46 suggests the miner may fall about 0.5%. International markets brushed off potentially destabilizing geopolitical developments--the Ukrainian leadership rejected a call for a self-determination vote from the local pro-Russian legislature in Crimea. Focus today is on RBA Gov. Glenn Stevens' parliamentary testimony from 2230 GMT. Stronger jawboning on the exchange rate is possible after recent strength. A significant pullback in AUD/USD could boost companies with offshore earnings, while potentially undermining high-yield plays. The market will then be awaiting February U.S. non-farm payrolls data due overnight. Technical focus remains on whether or not the index can break above the 5 1/2 year peak at 5461.7, thereby breaking clear of major Fibonacci retracement resistance at 5426.0. Index last 5445.90. (david.rogers@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0846 - \uc2 JP Morgan\uc2  says it remains overweight \uc2 Carsales.com\uc2  (CRZ.AU) after the company announced the purchase of an almost 50% stake in Korean automotive website SK Encarsales for A$126 million. Carsales, while offering only limited financial details, said the deal will be earnings accretive in fiscal 2014. The buy is the third in 12 months and signals a more acquisitive profile for the company, which the market likes, says \uc2 JP Morgan\uc2 . It has a price target of A$10.80. Carsales shares ended Thursday at A$11.03. (robb.stewart@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0857 - \uc2 JP Morgan\uc2  lifts its price target on \uc2 Bank of Queensland\uc2  (BOQ.AU) to A$10.94 a share from A$10.86 after nudging up its earnings forecast for the bank for fiscal 2014 by roughly 1.5%. It says balance sheet growth for the bank has been virtually absent in recent periods, but this has provided room for margin management via the unwinding of expensive deposits. The growth versus margin trade off has also meant improved capital generation and increased dividend capacity, \uc2 JP Morgan\uc2  says. It has a neutral recommendation on the shares. \uc2 Bank of Queensland\uc2  ended Thursday at A$12.48. (robb.stewart@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0900 - \uc2 Fortescue Metals Group Ltd\uc2 . (FMG.AU) has made good strides lifting production from its Pilbara iron-ore \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but meeting FY14 guidance of 127 million tons could be a stretch, according to \uc2 J.P. Morgan\uc2  analyst Lyndon Fagan. While there's been significant rainfall already this year, slowing \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , its Kings project is also slightly behind schedule and there's been disruptions at Christmas Creek as the miner took control of the processing plants following two worker fatalities. "While none of these problems appear structural, the company looks under pressure to meet production guidance," says Fagan, who estimates FY14 production of 124 million tons instead. Still, he says JPM remains bullish on the stock, and sees the recent pullback in its share price as a good opportunity to buy. "While we acknowledge the iron-ore price could be volatile this year as the market adjusts to new supply, we believe the imminent derisking of FMG's production and balance sheet present a compelling outlook for it to outperform this year," he says. The bank retains an Overweight rating, with a price target of A$6.85 a share. FMG closed 2.3% higher Thursday at A$5.41. Still, it's down around 10% on where the stock traded two weeks ago. (rhiannon.hoyle@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0926 - Australia's benchmark S&P/ASX 200 is heading for a flat open at roughly 5447, predicts IG. It says a barrier around the 5460 level has held back prices over the past couple of weeks and a fresh catalyst is needed to catapult the market through this. IG adds that with U.S. non-farm payrolls data due later in the global day, subdued trading is expected in Asia with most investors likely happy to remain on the sidelines. \uc2 BHP Billiton\uc2  (BHP.AU) could weaken early after its U.S. ADRs fell 0.5% overnight. The ASX 200 ended Thursday little changed at 5445.9. (robb.stewart@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0931 - \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  says in the current environment it prefers those companies that are in a position to give investors what they want: dividends. It suggests going long \uc2 Vocus Communications\uc2 (VOC.AU) and Alumina (AWC.AU) but short Leighton (LEI.AU). \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  argues Vocus offers superior growth relative to the small-cap telecommunications sector, with earnings per share forecast to rise 33% on a compound annual basis for the three years through fiscal 2017. For Alumina, it says that while 2014 continues to look expensive for the company and there is potentially no dividend this year, 2015 looks positive. Its concerns around Leighton focus on cash flow generation, its balance sheet and a challenging outlook as resources-exposed markets weaken. Vocus ended Thursday at A$4.33, Alumina at A$1.235 and Leighton at A$19.24. (robb.stewart@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0947 - Momentum in Australian iron-ore exports appears to have carried through into 1Q14 from 4Q13, with shipments from Port Hedland rising on a seasonally adjusted basis to a record 30.5 million tons, according to \uc2 Westpac\uc2  strategist Robert Rennie. But this momentum looks likely to slow in the months ahead. Rennie says that with port inventories rising, investors should expect somewhat of a plateau in the pace of shipments. "High frequency port data suggests a softer pace of exports is coming," he says, although quickly points out that it's still "hardly an anecdote of an approaching 'Lehman moment'." The price for 62% Fe iron ore at China's Tianjin port rose 0.2% to US$116.90 a ton Thursday. (rhiannon.hoyle@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0953 - Qantas (QAN.AU) has slipped out of the ranks of Australia's top 50 companies by market capitalization, as steep losses continue to keep its shares in the doldrums. Australia's national carrier has been removed from the S&P/ASX 50 index, which is reassessed each quarter, while building materials company James Hardie (JHX.AU) has re-entered. At A$1.15 each, Qantas shares have jumped 12% since the last index rebalancing in December and it remains in the country's benchmark S&P/ASX 200 index. Some index managers, however, focus on other indexes. State Street, for example, manages an exchange traded fund that tracks the S&P/ASX 50. Qantas shares hit a record low of 96 cents in July, 2012. (Ross.Kelly@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0957 - Australia's S&P/ASX 200 has found a natural resting place around the 5450 mark, and early signs are it will end the week snuggly in this zone, says Ric Spooner, chief market analyst at CMC Markets. The market seemingly is constrained by a rising Aussie dollar and falling iron-ore prices, Mr. Spooner says. "Relatively full valuations and the potential for a decent-sized pullback in response to risk events are also keeping buyers cautious," he says, adding that news Crimea has decided to have a succession vote is a reminder of the potential risks remaining in Ukraine. Still, if domestic economic news remains as positive as it has been in recent days the buyers may find it difficult to stay sidelined, he says. The ASX 200 ended Thursday flat at 5445.9. (robb.stewart@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1046 - Nymex crude is likely to trade in a higher range near term after hitting a two-week low of $100.13 Thursday but settling 11 cents higher at $101.56/bbl, Dow Jones technical analysis shows. The daily continuation chart is mixed as the MACD and slow stochastic indicators are bearish, but a bullish hammer candlestick pattern was completed Thursday. Resistance is at $102.08 (Thursday's high); a breach would expose the upside to $103.53 (Wednesday's high), then to $104.96 (Tuesday's high), $105.22 (five-month high hit Monday), $108.99 (Sept. 19 reaction high) and $109.16 (Sept. 12 high). But a drop below the $100.13 support would temper the positive near-term view, targeting the 200-day moving average (now at $100.05), then $99.40 (Feb. 13 low), $99.11 (Feb. 10 low), the 55-day moving average (now at $98.31) and 100-day moving average (now at $97.39). April crude is up 26 cents at $101.82/bbl on Globex. (jerry.tan@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 6 Mar 2014 22:59 EDT  Australian Equities Roundup -- Market Talk -2-\par \par \uc2 1123 - Base metals could remain rangebound as traders wait for nonfarm payrolls data, scheduled for later in the global trading day. The monthly jobs report is widely considered a measuring stick for the health of the U.S. economy. While weak jobs data could weigh on prices, an upbeat figure could improve market sentiment about demand. LME 3-month copper ended 0.3% higher overnight at $7,050/ton after U.S. initial claims for unemployment benefits fell by 26,000 to a seasonally adjusted 323,000 in the week ended March 1. That was the lowest level since November and less than the 335,000 forecast by economists. "Copper got a boost from this morning's jobs numbers," says Dave Meger, director at Vision Financial Markets. (arpan.mukherjee@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1236 - Australian \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Gov. Glenn Stevens has again made it clear the bank would prefer a lower domestic \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the economy. In his opening statement to the House of Representatives Standing Committee on Economics, the governor merely noted that the Aussie dollar was "still high by historical standards." But in response to questioning, Mr. Stevens added that he retained the view that an AUD/USD rate "in the US$90's or above US$1 is too high." That was enough to push the exchange rate down to US$0.9070 after an earlier rise to US$0.9100. The AUD/USD was last US$0.9085. (shani.raja@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1248 - Crude-oil futures extend gains in early Asian trade Friday after U.S. financial markets ended higher overnight and with events in Ukraine affecting oil market sentiment. The Moscow-backed Crimean government has called for a referendum in 10 days to ratify its decision to secede from Ukraine and join Russia. "Looking ahead, we expect markets to ebb and flow on geopolitical news, but increasingly factor physical indicators," ANZ says. "With northern hemisphere refineries starting to transition to summer fuels in the coming weeks, we expect slightly weaker performance across both benchmarks," the bank says. The U.S. labor market report due later Friday will also provide cues for oil prices. "Payrolls are on tap and markets have been giving every piece of lousy data a free pass to the Bahamas," DBS says. Nymex crude is up 17 cents at $101.73/bbl, Brent crude is up 10 cents at $108.20/bbl. (eric.yep@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1317 - Gold prices are moving sideways in a tight range as traders wait for nonfarm payrolls data from the U.S., scheduled later in the global trading day. A weak figure could suggest that economic recovery remains fragile and underpin gold, which is considered a safe-haven asset. Spot gold prices rose 1.2% overnight after U.S. President Barack Obama said that Crimea's referendum on seceding from Ukraine to join Russia is illegal and added that the U.S. and \uc2 European Union\uc2  are united against Russia's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Geopolitical tensions in \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2  have underpinned gold prices this week. Spot gold is at $1,350/oz, down 40 cents from its previous close. (arpan.mukherjee@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 March 06, 2014 22:59 ET (03:59 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140307ea37000mb\par }\par \b \uc2 Zusammenfassung der Suche\b0\par \par  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9072\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adj